role: model
parts:
- text: "Reflection Tick 2/5 — Theme: Formalizing a parsimonious feasible‑path homotopy (HRG) and pre‑mortem\n\n1) HRG: the concrete formulation\n- Goal\n  - Starting from a high‑quality feasible packing (C, r), find a small, strictly improving step in radii sum by “growing radii” and “minimally moving centers” along a provably feasible, convexly approximated path.\n- Variables and data\n  - C ∈ R^(N×2): current centers; r ∈ R^N: current radii (feasible).\n  - Propose Δr ∈ R^N, Δr ≥ 0 (not necessarily uniform; see weighting below).\n  - Decision variables: δC ∈ R^(N×2), bounded componentwise by |δx_i|, |δy_i| ≤ τ (trust region).\n- Linearized feasibility constraints (first‑order safe)\n  - Pairwise non‑overlap, for every (i, j), with u_ij the unit vector along (C_i − C_j):\n    u_ij · (δC_i − δC_j) ≥ (r_i + r_j + Δr_i + Δr_j) − ||C_i − C_j||\n    This is the standard supporting‑hyperplane inequality used in SCP, but here the RHS encodes the proposed radius growth.\n  - Boundary ties (center\
    \ remains within unit square and “covers” its radius with zero slack):\n    r_i + Δr_i ≤ x_i + δx_i\n    r_i + Δr_i ≤ 1 − (x_i + δx_i)\n    r_i + Δr_i ≤ y_i + δy_i\n    r_i + Δr_i ≤ 1 − (y_i + δy_i)\n  - Trust region:\n    −τ ≤ δx_i ≤ τ,  −τ ≤ δy_i ≤ τ\n- Objective (parsimonious movement)\n  - Minimize L1 movement to find the “closest” feasible displacement:\n    minimize ∑_i (|δx_i| + |δy_i|)\n    (Introduce positive/negative split variables to keep it an LP; add small isotropic weights if needed to break ties deterministically.)\n- Step acceptance\n  - Solve LP for δC with (Δr, τ). If infeasible, reduce either τ or Δr (backtrack) and retry.\n  - If feasible, set C′ = C + δC. Compute exact zero‑slack radii r′ by solving the full radii LP at C′ (global, not linearized), finalize with zero‑tolerance. Accept if ∑ r′ ≥ ∑ r + ε (ε ≈ 1e−6). Otherwise, backtrack.\n- Outer schedule (deterministic)\n  - Choose an initial growth magnitude α0 (e.g., α0 ∈ {0.002, 0.001} depending on scale).\n \
    \ - Backtracking on α: {α0, 0.7α0, 0.5α0, 0.35α0, 0.2α0, …}. For each α, try τ ∈ {0.06, 0.04, 0.025, 0.015, 0.01, 0.006, 0.004, 0.002}, shrinking if infeasible.\n  - Weights w for Δr = α·w:\n    - Uniform: w_i = 1 (baseline).\n    - Boundary‑aware: w_i ∝ max(0, min(x_i, 1 − x_i, y_i, 1 − y_i) − r_i + η), with a tiny η (e.g., 1e−6) to keep determinism when slack is zero; normalizing ∑ w_i = 1. This discourages growth of circles pinned on walls/corners unless the LP finds room.\n    - Contact‑aware (optional): w_i ∝ average of pairwise slacks around i (clamped to [0, …]) + η; also normalized.\n  - Terminate when all α, τ combinations fail to produce an improvement or when improvement per accepted step < ε for k consecutive attempts.\n\n2) Why HRG can help (mechanism vs SCP‑LP)\n- SCP‑LP jointly optimizes radii and centers per step under a linearized objective (maximize sum of radii surrogate) and linearized feasibility; the objective can bias the center update away from “just enough” movement\
    \ that unlocks a feasible increase.\n- HRG decouples “how much to grow radii” (propose Δr) from “how to move as little as necessary” (find minimal δC) in a feasibility LP. This separation can explore a different slice of the feasible cone and capture tiny gains that a single joint linearized objective might miss or reject.\n\n3) Pre‑mortem: failure modes and mitigations\n- FM1: LP infeasible even for tiny α or τ (rigid contact graph)\n  - Interpretation: Given current contact graph and boundary pins, no first‑order feasible displacement exists to support any Δr ≥ 0.\n  - Mitigations:\n    - Switch weights to boundary‑aware w to avoid insisting on growing pinned circles.\n    - Allow circle‑wise gating: set Δr_i = 0 for the top‑k most pinned circles (by boundary or pairwise tightness), distribute α over the rest.\n    - Try max‑min slack creation variant (below) to first create geometric room.\n- FM2: Feasible δC exists, but exact radii LP yields no net improvement (linearization gap)\n\
    \  - Tighten linearization by adding a small safety margin σ (e.g., subtract σ from RHS of pair constraints) to counter first‑order optimism.\n  - Reduce τ and/or α, or add a second check: recompute true pair distances at C + δC; if any near‑binding pairs lost margin, shrink step and retry.\n- FM3: Numerical brittleness near boundaries\n  - Stick with zero‑tolerance finalize; ensure deterministic tie‑breaking in the LP; use consistent ε/η. Do not add ad hoc positive slacks that bias results.\n- FM4: L1 objective produces axis‑aligned “staircasing” moves that aren’t ideal\n  - Keep L1 for LP simplicity, but add tiny anisotropic weights (e.g., 1+1e−9·i) to avoid degeneracy deterministically.\n  - If needed, test an alternative objective: minimize max_i ||δC_i||_∞ (still LP) for more uniform displacement.\n- FM5: Endless micro‑steps with negligible gain\n  - Enforce an improvement budget: stop after k accepted steps with marginal gain < ε_gain (e.g., 5 steps each < 5e−7 total radii gain).\n\
    \  - If HRG stalls under multiple seeds/weights, publish the negative result with analysis and pivot.\n\n4) Alternative “room‑making first” variant (backup plan)\n- Max‑min slack LP (no Δr yet):\n  - Variables δC and scalar t ≥ 0; trust region as before.\n  - For each pair (i, j): new linearized slack s_ij_new ≥ s_ij + t, where s_ij = ||C_i − C_j|| − (r_i + r_j).\n  - For boundaries: min boundary slack_i_new ≥ slack_i + t, where slack_i = min(x_i − r_i, 1 − x_i − r_i, y_i − r_i, 1 − y_i − r_i).\n  - Maximize t subject to trust‑region bounds. If t > 0 accepted, you’ve created uniform slack; then run exact radii LP (zero‑slack) to harvest the room into radii increases.\n  - This variant explicitly creates geometric room before asking for radii growth, which can be safer if Δr homotopy fails frequently.\n\n5) Metrics and logs (insight over scores)\n- Per attempt: α, τ, weight mode, feasibility (Y/N), accepted improvement Δ∑r, #pairs near binding (<1e−10), min/mean slack changes, contact\
    \ count, and a bounded move norm.\n- Collect small histograms of slack distribution shifts to build a picture of the feasible cone’s local structure and how HRG navigates it.\n\n6) Why this stays within the Codex\n- Parsimony: One new LP with a clear objective and constraints; no baroque heuristics.\n- Integrity: Strict acceptance via exact radii LP + finalize; deterministic; thorough logging.\n- Insight: Produces structural knowledge (rigidity, local slack geometry, the efficacy of homotopy steps) regardless of a score bump.\n\n7) Commitments for next tick\n- Pin down α, τ schedules and weight definitions; write the exact LP in canonical form (split variables for L1) and a deterministic step‑selection protocol.\n- Decide the stopping criteria precisely (improvement thresholds, maximum attempts) to avoid grind while ensuring a fair test of the hypothesis."
thinking_content: "**Structuring the delta r plan**\n\nI'm thinking about how to define the delta r scheme. I want to start by setting Δr_i = α * w_i, where weights w are normalized to sum to 1. There are a few options: one could be uniform weights where w_i = 1. Another option is applying boundary-limited weighting, but this might have issues with slack at the boundaries. I could also explore pairwise slack for circles based on distances between neighbors. However, it seems tricky because if they’re too close, growth may contradict itself.**Examining feasibility and growth**\n\nI see that HRG addresses feasibility by adjusting circle centers to create slack as they grow. If some circles near the boundaries can move inward, that works, but those at the corners might be stuck, leading to a narrow feasible path. So, I’m proposing a global uniform increase for all circles based on non-zero values. If that path isn’t feasible, I might adjust the weights to support more interior circles while\
  \ defining constraints for the growth. Then, I can apply a trust region for adjustments.**Setting boundary constraints**\n\nI need to ensure boundary ties are within the range [0,1] by incorporating linear constraints for both x and y coordinates, so they stay valid. The goal is to minimize the total movement, represented as t_i, which could be done through various norms. I’ll set up a backtracking algorithm starting with a small alpha value around 0.002, and if the linear programming problem is feasible, I’ll accept and adjust radii while enforcing precise solutions to ensure they don’t exceed set boundaries afterward.**Finalizing radii constraints**\n\nIf the linear programming (LP) for exact radii yields less than r', I recognize that feasibility under linearization doesn’t guarantee exactness. I’ll only accept if there’s an improvement after acceptance. If things don’t improve, I might have to adjust tau or alpha. In edge cases where the LP is feasible, I’ll add a safety margin to\
  \ constraints if there's a gap. Also, I might perform a second-order check for violations with distance recalibration. Given the complexity, it’s manageable with around 32 circles and 496 pairs to consider!**Defining metrics for logging**\n\nI need to set up metrics to track during this process, like the step index, values for alpha and tau, the count of active constraints, acceptance criteria, and the sum of radii. I’ll also note changes in the contact graph, especially those within a tiny threshold, like 1e-10. \n\nIt could be useful to analyze pairwise slack increments and maintain minimal slack after finalization. I’m considering the implications of locked packing and how boundary relief might impact radius changes. If movement yields no improvement after small steps, I might have to stop. I’ll also think about how degeneracy in the L1 objective might require exploring L2 options instead. However, I’ll prioritize the LP for reproducibility and focus on maximizing minimum slack while\
  \ ensuring we adhere to our principle of simplicity and parsimony."
