parts:
- text: "Reflection Tick 4/5 — Theme: Turn HRG from a clean idea into a disciplined, bounded experiment\n\n1) What “success” and “failure” look like (before coding)\n- Success (score): Any strictly verified increase in ∑r beyond the plateau (≥ 1e−6 per accepted step; cumulative can be small but monotone).\n- Success (insight): Even with no score gain, a clear map of local rigidity: feasibility LP infeasibility patterns, trust‑region thresholds, and slack redistribution statistics that explain why SCP‑LP stalls.\n- Failure: Repeated infeasibility or zero gain across α/τ/w with clean logs; this is still publishable if we can demonstrate the feasible cone is effectively rigid in our neighborhood.\n\n2) Tightening the HRG protocol into code units (small, testable modules)\n- assemble_hrg_lp(C, r, delta_r, tau, sigma=1e−12)\n  - Builds the split‑variable LP (δx^±, δy^±) with:\n    - Pair constraints: −u·(δC_i − δC_j) ≤ −g_ij, g_ij = (r_i + r_j + Δr_i + Δr_j) − d_ij − σ\n    - Boundary ties (four\
    \ per circle) and in‑box guards\n    - Split bounds 0 ≤ δx^±, δy^± ≤ τ\n  - Objective: weighted L1 with tiny index‑based offsets for determinism\n  - Returns A_ub, b_ub, bounds, c and a decoder for δC from splits\n- try_hrg_step(C, r, alpha, w, tau_list)\n  - For τ in descending tau_list:\n    - Build Δr = α·w, solve feasibility LP; if infeasible, continue\n    - Decode δC, set C′ = C + δC; solve exact zero‑slack radii LP; finalize (zero‑tol)\n    - If ∑r′ ≥ ∑r + ε (ε=1e−6), return accepted (C′, r′, gain, τ)\n  - Else return rejected\n- hrg_driver(seed_C, seed_r)\n  - α schedule: [α0, 0.7α0, 0.5α0, 0.35α0, 0.2α0, 0.12α0, 0.08α0] with α0=0.001\n  - τ schedule: [0.06, 0.04, 0.025, 0.015, 0.01, 0.006, 0.004, 0.002]\n  - w modes (deterministic): uniform, then boundary‑aware if uniform fails globally\n  - Stop after ≤12 accepted steps or if last 5 accepted gains < 5e−7 or total attempts > 80\n\n3) Determinism and numeric hygiene\n- No randomness beyond fixed seeds; fixed σ, ε; fixed α/τ lists;\
    \ fixed w normalization with η=1e−9 to break ties.\n- Tiny objective offsets (1+1e−9·i) and (1+2e−9·i) ensure linprog picks a unique solution, stabilizing δC.\n- All feasibility checks flow into a single exact radii LP (zero‑slack); acceptance is based on verified ∑r increase post finalize; no “optimistic” linearization goals.\n\n4) Complexity and runtime budget\n- HRG LP per attempt: ~752 rows, 128 vars; trivial for HiGHS.\n- Radii LP per check: ~752 rows, 32 vars; also trivial.\n- Even 160 attempts (2 seeds × 80) should fit comfortably under the 20‑minute evaluation cap, with ample logging.\n\n5) Sanity checks to avoid illusory gains\n- Double‑check acceptance by recomputing pair distances and boundary margins on (C′, r′) to ensure no post‑LP finalize shaved off the gain below ε (we already finalize with zero‑tol; this re‑check is cheap).\n- Log min slack (pairs and boundary) before/after; if the increase comes solely from trading slack elsewhere (i.e., “stealing”), the metric will\
    \ show it—but we still accept if ∑r increased and constraints hold.\n\n6) What I expect to see (honest prediction)\n- Most attempts will be infeasible for larger α; small α with moderate τ might accept 1–2 micro‑steps (if any), likely with boundary‑aware weights. If all fail, the pattern itself (infeasible across α/τ) is a strong rigidity signal: we are genuinely at a corner of the feasible polyhedral cone where first‑order room doesn’t exist.\n- If HRG accepts steps, I expect microscopic but real gains (O(1e−6–1e−5)), after which SCP‑LP polish won’t add more (consistent with prior ablations).\n\n7) What I’ll publish regardless of outcome\n- A compact HRG log TSV (attempt‑wise), showing accept/reject, α, τ, Δ∑r, move norms, and slack stats.\n- A discussion of “local contact‑graph rigidity”: empirical K≥3N threshold, infeasibility contours, and why dual‑guided selection is brittle from some inits.\n- A minimal code path (one LP builder, one driver) that others can audit and extend; parsimony\
    \ and clarity first.\n\n8) Guarding against grind\n- I will not iterate beyond the predetermined α/τ lists or add ad hoc heuristics mid‑run.\n- If HRG produces no improvement over two seeds under these disciplined settings, I will treat it as a significant negative result and move to write‑up mode rather than spawning more micro‑variants."
