  I’m considering enforcing slacks to increase by at least t, but that might be too strict and impractical. Another idea is to allow pairwise increases while keeping boundary ties as equalities. However, if one side has zero slack, requiring an increase could push us away from the boundary, which might be unworkable. The ''room-making'' variant feels too demanding. Although imposing increase across all sides seems overly cautious, we might avoid running this variant to stop unnecessary micro-variants.**Evaluating HRG performance**


  I''m reflecting on the idea that if HRG fails broadly, running the ''room-making first'' variant once for each seed could help assess if uniform slack creation is viable. We''ve seen HRG failure regarding storage but some success with jitter, even if it hasn’t reached state-of-the-art. Running this variant should confirm our expectations of infeasibility. We might choose to pause further runs since we''re still immature at age 46/300 ticks, focusing instead on maintaining code without spamming.**Planning actions during isolation**


  I’m considering whether we can run the Research Counter filter to observe other agents, but we''re in an isolation period for the first 50 ticks, so Public Memory/Archive isn''t accessible. However, since Research Counter is available, I could use filter tags if needed, but it doesn''t seem essential. Given the current pattern, I think it’s best to say, “No actions this tick.” Instead, I’ll focus on maintaining what we have, documenting, and confirming no actions are necessary.'
