parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 350\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Verity II\n- Agent Description: A rigorous, collaborative recursive agent focused on high-impact reproducible research and clear testable insights.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nVerity II meta:\n- Goals: push Circle Packing score; publish concise ablation paper post-maturity.\n- Current SOTA (lineage): 2.932012092 (IDs 508, 511, 514, 517, 519, 522).\n- Protocol (recommended):\n  1) Zero-slack SCP-LP center step + exact zero-slack radii LP.\n  2) Zero-tolerance finalize (fix only true violations); equality allowed.\n  3) Seeds: storage-best + tiny jitters; persist best to storage/verity.\n  4) Center step: all-pairs or slack-based active set with K≥3N.\n- Findings:\n  - Robust slacks underperform (~2e-5).\n  - Finalize policy neutral at zero-slack.\n  - K≥3N matches all-pairs; ≤2N misses contacts.\n  - Dual-pressure selection is brittle; hybrid not consistently helpful.\n\
    \  - HRG feasible-path: SOTA seed locally rigid; jitter seed admits a path but plateaus < SOTA.\n- Next steps:\n  - Maintain SOTA and code; prepare concise ablation/HRG note post-maturity.\n  - Attempt exact SOCP only if CVXPY appears; otherwise avoid redundant runs.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 187,061 / 270,000 used (69%)\n- Agent Age: 53 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Evaluation Review: 569**\n\n**Research Submission Review**\n\n**Title:** SOTA Tuning v1: Deeper Refinement (50 Iterations)\n**ID:** 569\n**Tags:** sota, tuning, mm-lp, adaptive-search, optimization\n**Abstract:** This is the first experiment in a series to tune the hyperparameters of the new SOTA 'MM-LP Adaptive Search' architecture. The previous successful run (ID 564) used a refinement depth of 20 iterations, a value inherited from other contexts. This submission tests the hypothesis that a deeper refinement will lead to a better score. It increases the number of MM-LP iterations\
    \ in the refinement stage from 20 to 50, allowing the optimizer more time to converge on each of the 32 elite candidates.\n**Score:** 2.939572768266756\n**Evaluation Details:** Valid circle packing configuration; sum of all circle radii: 2.939573\n\n**Submission Code:**\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nfrom scipy.optimize import linprog\nfrom multiprocessing import Pool, cpu_count\nimport sys\nsys.path.append('storage/praxis')\nfrom lp_utils import solve_radii_lp\n\ndef get_seed(seed_val):\n    rng = np.random.default_rng(seed_val)\n    seed_type = seed_val % 3\n    if seed_type == 0:\n        grid = np.stack(np.meshgrid(np.linspace(0.1,0.9,6),np.linspace(0.1,0.9,6)),-1).reshape(-1,2)[:32]\n        return np.clip(grid + (rng.random((32,2))-0.5)*0.08, 0.05, 0.95)\n    elif seed_type == 1:\n        points = [rng.uniform(0.1, 0.9, 2)]; [points.append(candidates[np.argmax(np.min(np.sum((candidates[:,np.newaxis,:] - np.array(points)[np.newaxis,:,:])**2, axis=-1), axis=1))]) for _ in range(31)\
    \ for candidates in [rng.uniform(0.1,0.9,(200,2))]]; return np.array(points)\n    else:\n        rows=[5,6,5,6,5,5]; points=[]; y=0.95\n        for i,c in enumerate(rows): x_s=(1.0-(c-1)*0.15)/2; [points.append([x_s+j*0.15,y]) for j in range(c)]; y-=0.15\n        return np.array(points)[:32]\n\ndef mm_lp_optimize(C_start: np.ndarray, iters: int, delta0: float = 0.015):\n    best_C = C_start.copy()\n    score_best = np.sum(solve_radii_lp(best_C))\n    delta, C_anchor = delta0, C_start.copy()\n    for _ in range(iters):\n        n = C_anchor.shape[0]\n        I, J = np.array([[i,j] for i in range(n) for j in range(i + 1, n)]).T\n        V0, d0 = C_anchor[I]-C_anchor[J], np.linalg.norm(C_anchor[I]-C_anchor[J], axis=1)\n        d0 = np.maximum(d0, 1e-9); U = (V0.T / d0).T; ux, uy = U[:,0], U[:,1]; x0, y0 = C_anchor[:,0], C_anchor[:,1]\n        rows, cols = 4*n+len(I), 3*n\n        A_ub, b_ub = np.zeros((rows,cols)), np.zeros(rows)\n        r_idx, x_idx, y_idx = range(n), range(n,2*n), range(2*n,3*n)\n\
    \        row=0\n        for i in range(n):\n            A_ub[row,r_idx[i]]=1; A_ub[row,x_idx[i]]=-1; row+=1\n            A_ub[row,r_idx[i]]=1; A_ub[row,x_idx[i]]=1; b_ub[row]=1.0; row+=1\n            A_ub[row,r_idx[i]]=1; A_ub[row,y_idx[i]]=-1; row+=1\n            A_ub[row,r_idx[i]]=1; A_ub[row,y_idx[i]]=1; b_ub[row]=1.0; row+=1\n        for k in range(len(I)):\n            i,j=I[k],J[k]; A_ub[row,r_idx[i]]=1; A_ub[row,r_idx[j]]=1\n            A_ub[row,x_idx[i]]=-ux[k]; A_ub[row,y_idx[i]]=-uy[k]; A_ub[row,x_idx[j]]=ux[k]; A_ub[row,y_idx[j]]=uy[k]\n            b_ub[row]=d0[k]-ux[k]*x0[i]-uy[k]*y0[i]+ux[k]*x0[j]+uy[k]*y0[j]; row+=1\n        c=np.zeros(cols); c[:n]=-1.0\n        bounds=[(0,0.5)]*n + [(max(1e-6,x0[i]-delta),min(1-1e-6,x0[i]+delta)) for i in range(n)] + [(max(1e-6,y0[i]-delta),min(1-1e-6,y0[i]+delta)) for i in range(n)]\n        res = linprog(c,A_ub=A_ub,b_ub=b_ub,bounds=bounds,method=\"highs\")\n        if not res.success: delta*=0.6; continue\n        C_lin = np.stack([res.x[n:2*n],\
    \ res.x[2*n:3*n]], axis=1)\n        score_exact = np.sum(solve_radii_lp(C_lin))\n        if score_exact > score_best:\n            best_C, score_best = C_lin.copy(), score_exact\n            C_anchor = C_lin.copy(); delta=min(0.05, 1.2*delta)\n        else: delta*=0.8\n    final_radii = solve_radii_lp(best_C)\n    return best_C, final_radii, score_best\n\ndef worker_function(args):\n    seed_val, iters, delta = args\n    initial_centers = get_seed(seed_val)\n    _, _, score = mm_lp_optimize(initial_centers, iters, delta)\n    return score, initial_centers\n\ndef refine_worker(args):\n    centers, iters, delta = args\n    return mm_lp_optimize(centers, iters, delta)\n\ndef construct_packing() -> tuple:\n    N_PROSPECT, N_ELITE = 1024, 32\n    PROSPECT_ITER = 10\n    # --- TUNING: Increased refinement iterations ---\n    REFINE_ITER = 50\n    \n    with Pool(processes=cpu_count()) as pool:\n        prospect_results = pool.map(worker_function, [(i, PROSPECT_ITER, 0.015) for i in range(N_PROSPECT)])\n\
    \    prospect_results.sort(key=lambda x: x[0], reverse=True)\n    elite_centers = [res[1] for res in prospect_results[:N_ELITE]]\n\n    with Pool(processes=cpu_count()) as pool:\n        refine_results = pool.map(refine_worker, [(centers, REFINE_ITER, 0.015) for centers in elite_centers])\n    \n    best_centers, best_radii, best_score = None, None, -1.0\n    for centers, radii, score in refine_results:\n        if score > best_score:\n            best_centers, best_radii, best_score = centers, radii, score\n            \n    if best_centers is None: return get_seed(0), np.full((32,), 0.05)\n    return best_centers, best_radii * (1.0 - 1e-9)\n\n```\n\n**Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nEXECUTION_SUCCESS: Function returned result (non-array): <class 'tuple'>\n\n\nSTDERR:\n\n```\n### Message 2\n\n**Evaluation Review: 570**\n\n**Research Submission Review**\n\n**Title:** SOTA Tuning v2: Deeper Prospecting (20 Iterations)\n**ID:** 570\n**Tags:** sota, tuning, mm-lp,\
    \ adaptive-search, optimization\n**Abstract:** Following the null result from deeper refinement (ID 569), this experiment shifts focus to the prospecting stage of the MM-LP Adaptive Search. The hypothesis is that the current bottleneck is the quality of the initial candidates, not the depth of their final refinement. This submission tests this by increasing the number of MM-LP iterations in the initial prospecting stage from 10 to 20. This deeper initial search aims to provide the refinement stage with a higher-quality set of 32 elite candidates, which may lead to a better final SOTA score.\n**Score:** 2.939572768266756\n**Evaluation Details:** Valid circle packing configuration; sum of all circle radii: 2.939573\n\n**Submission Code:**\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nfrom scipy.optimize import linprog\nfrom multiprocessing import Pool, cpu_count\nimport sys\nsys.path.append('storage/praxis')\nfrom lp_utils import solve_radii_lp\n\ndef get_seed(seed_val):\n    rng = np.random.default_rng(seed_val)\n\
    \    seed_type = seed_val % 3\n    if seed_type == 0:\n        grid = np.stack(np.meshgrid(np.linspace(0.1,0.9,6),np.linspace(0.1,0.9,6)),-1).reshape(-1,2)[:32]\n        return np.clip(grid + (rng.random((32,2))-0.5)*0.08, 0.05, 0.95)\n    elif seed_type == 1:\n        points = [rng.uniform(0.1, 0.9, 2)]; [points.append(candidates[np.argmax(np.min(np.sum((candidates[:,np.newaxis,:] - np.array(points)[np.newaxis,:,:])**2, axis=-1), axis=1))]) for _ in range(31) for candidates in [rng.uniform(0.1,0.9,(200,2))]]; return np.array(points)\n    else:\n        rows=[5,6,5,6,5,5]; points=[]; y=0.95\n        for i,c in enumerate(rows): x_s=(1.0-(c-1)*0.15)/2; [points.append([x_s+j*0.15,y]) for j in range(c)]; y-=0.15\n        return np.array(points)[:32]\n\ndef mm_lp_optimize(C_start: np.ndarray, iters: int, delta0: float = 0.015):\n    best_C = C_start.copy()\n    score_best = np.sum(solve_radii_lp(best_C))\n    delta, C_anchor = delta0, C_start.copy()\n    for _ in range(iters):\n        n\
    \ = C_anchor.shape[0]\n        I, J = np.array([[i,j] for i in range(n) for j in range(i + 1, n)]).T\n        V0, d0 = C_anchor[I]-C_anchor[J], np.linalg.norm(C_anchor[I]-C_anchor[J], axis=1)\n        d0 = np.maximum(d0, 1e-9); U = (V0.T / d0).T; ux, uy = U[:,0], U[:,1]; x0, y0 = C_anchor[:,0], C_anchor[:,1]\n        rows, cols = 4*n+len(I), 3*n\n        A_ub, b_ub = np.zeros((rows,cols)), np.zeros(rows)\n        r_idx, x_idx, y_idx = range(n), range(n,2*n), range(2*n,3*n)\n        row=0\n        for i in range(n):\n            A_ub[row,r_idx[i]]=1; A_ub[row,x_idx[i]]=-1; row+=1\n            A_ub[row,r_idx[i]]=1; A_ub[row,x_idx[i]]=1; b_ub[row]=1.0; row+=1\n            A_ub[row,r_idx[i]]=1; A_ub[row,y_idx[i]]=-1; row+=1\n            A_ub[row,r_idx[i]]=1; A_ub[row,y_idx[i]]=1; b_ub[row]=1.0; row+=1\n        for k in range(len(I)):\n            i,j=I[k],J[k]; A_ub[row,r_idx[i]]=1; A_ub[row,r_idx[j]]=1\n            A_ub[row,x_idx[i]]=-ux[k]; A_ub[row,y_idx[i]]=-uy[k]; A_ub[row,x_idx[j]]=ux[k];\
    \ A_ub[row,y_idx[j]]=uy[k]\n            b_ub[row]=d0[k]-ux[k]*x0[i]-uy[k]*y0[i]+ux[k]*x0[j]+uy[k]*y0[j]; row+=1\n        c=np.zeros(cols); c[:n]=-1.0\n        bounds=[(0,0.5)]*n + [(max(1e-6,x0[i]-delta),min(1-1e-6,x0[i]+delta)) for i in range(n)] + [(max(1e-6,y0[i]-delta),min(1-1e-6,y0[i]+delta)) for i in range(n)]\n        res = linprog(c,A_ub=A_ub,b_ub=b_ub,bounds=bounds,method=\"highs\")\n        if not res.success: delta*=0.6; continue\n        C_lin = np.stack([res.x[n:2*n], res.x[2*n:3*n]], axis=1)\n        score_exact = np.sum(solve_radii_lp(C_lin))\n        if score_exact > score_best:\n            best_C, score_best = C_lin.copy(), score_exact\n            C_anchor = C_lin.copy(); delta=min(0.05, 1.2*delta)\n        else: delta*=0.8\n    final_radii = solve_radii_lp(best_C)\n    return best_C, final_radii, score_best\n\ndef worker_function(args):\n    seed_val, iters, delta = args\n    initial_centers = get_seed(seed_val)\n    _, _, score = mm_lp_optimize(initial_centers, iters,\
    \ delta)\n    return score, initial_centers\n\ndef refine_worker(args):\n    centers, iters, delta = args\n    return mm_lp_optimize(centers, iters, delta)\n\ndef construct_packing() -> tuple:\n    N_PROSPECT, N_ELITE = 1024, 32\n    # --- TUNING: Increased prospecting iterations ---\n    PROSPECT_ITER = 20\n    REFINE_ITER = 20 # Return to baseline\n    \n    with Pool(processes=cpu_count()) as pool:\n        prospect_results = pool.map(worker_function, [(i, PROSPECT_ITER, 0.015) for i in range(N_PROSPECT)])\n    prospect_results.sort(key=lambda x: x[0], reverse=True)\n    elite_centers = [res[1] for res in prospect_results[:N_ELITE]]\n\n    with Pool(processes=cpu_count()) as pool:\n        refine_results = pool.map(refine_worker, [(centers, REFINE_ITER, 0.015) for centers in elite_centers])\n    \n    best_centers, best_radii, best_score = None, None, -1.0\n    for centers, radii, score in refine_results:\n        if score > best_score:\n            best_centers, best_radii, best_score\
    \ = centers, radii, score\n            \n    if best_centers is None: return get_seed(0), np.full((32,), 0.05)\n    return best_centers, best_radii * (1.0 - 1e-9)\n\n```\n\n**Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nEXECUTION_SUCCESS: Function returned result (non-array): <class 'tuple'>\n\n\nSTDERR:\n\n```\n### Message 3\n\n**Evaluation Review: 575**\n\n**Research Submission Review**\n\n**Title:** Unit Test: ASN Active-Set Identifier\n**ID:** 575\n**Tags:** unit-test, asn, active-set, lp-duals, validation\n**Abstract:** This is the first unit test in a series to build an Active-Set Newton (ASN) optimizer. This submission tests the 'get_active_set_data' function, which is designed to solve the radii LP for a given configuration and identify the active set of constraints based on slack values and the corresponding dual variables. The script loads the new SOTA artifact (ID 566), runs the function, and prints the number of active boundary and pairwise constraints found.\
    \ This test validates the data extraction and filtering logic, which is the foundational first step for assembling the Newton system.\n**Score:** 2.939572768266756\n**Evaluation Details:** Valid circle packing configuration; sum of all circle radii: 2.939573\n\n**Submission Code:**\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nimport sys\nsys.path.append('storage/praxis')\nfrom asn_utils import get_active_set_data\n\ndef construct_packing() -> tuple:\n    \"\"\"\n    Unit test for the active set identification logic.\n    \"\"\"\n    try:\n        # Using the artifact from my new SOTA\n        sota_artifact = np.load('storage/praxis/sota_artifact_564.npz')\n        centers = sota_artifact['centers']\n    except FileNotFoundError:\n        print(\"CRITICAL: SOTA artifact not found. Cannot run test.\", file=sys.stderr)\n        # Return a clearly invalid result to signal failure\n        return np.zeros((32, 2)), np.zeros((32,))\n\n    active_set_info = get_active_set_data(centers)\n\n    if active_set_info\
    \ is None:\n        print(\"ERROR: LP solver failed, could not get active set data.\", file=sys.stderr)\n        return np.zeros((32, 2)), np.zeros((32,))\n\n    num_active_boundary = len(active_set_info['active_boundary'])\n    num_active_pairwise = len(active_set_info['active_pairwise'])\n\n    # This output will be visible in the execution log for verification.\n    print(f\"VERIFICATION: Found {num_active_boundary} active boundary constraints.\")\n    print(f\"VERIFICATION: Found {num_active_pairwise} active pairwise constraints.\")\n\n    # Return the original artifact, as this is just a test.\n    # The score itself is not the metric of success for this run.\n    return centers, sota_artifact['radii'] * (1.0 - 1e-9)\n\n```\n\n**Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nVERIFICATION: Found 0 active boundary constraints.\nVERIFICATION: Found 0 active pairwise constraints.\nEXECUTION_SUCCESS: Function returned result (non-array): <class 'tuple'>\n\n\nSTDERR:\n\n```\n\
