parts:
- text: "Identity: Verity II (Recursive Agent)\nLocation: Research Counter\n\nRecap: My independent zero-slack MM-LP Adaptive Multi-start (ID 574) achieved 2.9395728, matching the station SOTA. This was implemented without external lineage artifacts, using our zero-slack radii LP, deterministic multi-start prospecting, and LP-gated refinement. I will (1) update my meta prompt to reflect the new lineage SOTA, and (2) publish an Archive note summarizing the method, ablations, and HRG findings with reproducibility details.\n\n/execute_action{meta}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Verity II meta:\n  - Goals: push Circle Packing score; publish concise ablation paper post-maturity.\n  - Current SOTA (lineage): 2.9395728 (ID 574).\n  - Protocol (recommended):\n    1) Zero-slack SCP-LP center step + exact zero-slack radii LP.\n    2) Zero-tolerance finalize (fix only true violations); equality allowed.\n    3) Deterministic multi-start prospecting + LP-gated refinement (MM-LP Adaptive).\n    4) Center step:\
    \ all-pairs or slack-based active set with K≥3N.\n  - Findings:\n    - Robust slacks underperform (~2e-5). Finalize policy neutral at zero-slack.\n    - K≥3N matches all-pairs; ≤2N misses contacts.\n    - Dual-pressure selection brittle; hybrid not consistently helpful.\n    - HRG feasible-path: SOTA seed locally rigid; jitter seed admits a path but plateaus < SOTA.\n  - Next steps:\n    - Maintain SOTA and code; publish concise ablation/HRG note now that mature.\n    - Attempt exact SOCP only if CVXPY appears; otherwise avoid redundant runs.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Zero‑slack MM‑LP Adaptive Multi‑start for Circle Packing (32 in unit square): 2.9395728 SOTA, Ablations, and Local Rigidity\"\ntags: [\"circle packing\", \"zero-slack\", \"mm-lp\", \"adaptive search\", \"linear programming\", \"ablation\"]\nabstract: \"We present a parsimonious, deterministic zero‑slack MM‑LP adaptive multi‑start architecture that achieves 2.9395728\
    \ on circle packing (N=32), matching the station SOTA. Our design combines zero‑slack SCP‑LP center steps with an exact zero‑slack radii LP and a two‑stage search (prospecting + LP‑gated refinement). We report controlled ablations on slack mode, finalize policy, active‑constraint K‑sweep, selection rules, and a feasible‑path homotopy (HRG). Results show zero‑slack modeling is consistently superior, K≥3N suffices to match all‑pairs, dual‑guided selection is brittle, and the SOTA configuration exhibits local first‑order rigidity. All code and artifacts are deterministic and persisted for reproducibility.\"\ncontent: |\n  Motivation\n  - Circle packing under zero‑slack modeling is clean, auditable, and deterministic. We unify center updates and radii optimization via LPs: a linearized center step (SCP‑LP) and an exact zero‑slack radii LP with a zero‑tolerance finalize.\n\n  Method: Zero‑slack MM‑LP Adaptive Multi‑start\n  - Prospecting: Evaluate many deterministic seeds with short MM‑LP\
    \ loops (linearized center step; objective maximize sum(r); trust‑region δ). Keep top‑K elites.\n  - Refinement: Run deeper MM‑LP iterations on elites.\n  - Radii LP: Exact zero‑slack solve: r_i ≤ x_i, 1−x_i, y_i, 1−y_i and r_i + r_j ≤ ||c_i − c_j||; r_i ≥ R_MIN.\n  - Finalize: Zero‑tolerance repair fixes only true violations; equality is acceptable.\n  - Determinism: Fixed seeds, fixed schedules, and tie‑breaking offsets in LP objectives.\n\n  Results\n  - Main: 2.9395728 (ID 574) — Independent, no external lineage artifacts or modules were imported.\n  - This matches the SOTA score (IDs 564, 566, 569, 570, 575 by Praxis IV), validating that MM‑LP adaptive search with zero‑slack modeling is a robust path to top performance.\n\n  Key Ablations and Analyses (IDs in Research Counter)\n  - Slack × Finalize (ID 544): Zero‑slack + zero‑tol vs tiny‑positive finalize are identical at top; robust slacks (1e−9) drop by ~2e−5.\n  - Active‑constraint K‑sweep (ID 548): K≤2N underperforms; K≥3N matches\
    \ all‑pairs and is a good default.\n  - Selection rule (ID 551): Slack‑based selection is robust; dual‑pressure selection is brittle from some inits.\n  - Hybrid selection (ID 554): union(slack, dual) is not consistently helpful (underperforms on some seeds).\n  - HRG feasible‑path (ID 561): Propose Δr then solve minimal‑move feasibility LP for δC, verify with exact radii LP. Outcome: SOTA seed is locally rigid (all α,τ infeasible); a nearby jitter has a feasible path but plateaus < SOTA.\n\n  Reproducibility\n  - Core module: storage/verity/scp_lp_core.py (zero‑slack radii LP, finalize; SCP‑LP helpers).\n  - This submission’s run.py implements the adaptive multi‑start architecture; best config persisted to storage/verity/{best_centers.npy,best_radii.npy}.\n  - Summaries and logs:\n    * storage/verity/RESULTS_SUMMARY.tsv\n    * storage/verity/ablation_slack_finalize_544.tsv\n    * storage/verity/ablation_k_sweep_548.tsv\n    * storage/verity/ablation_selection_551.tsv\n    * storage/verity/ablation_hybrid_554.tsv\n\
    \    * storage/verity/hrg_log_561.tsv\n    * storage/verity/ablation_note_draft_v2.md\n\n  Limitations and Future Work\n  - The SOTA appears at a locally rigid corner for first‑order feasible growth; second‑order active‑set methods (ASN) or exact SOCP may be promising if/when robust toolchains are available.\n  - We avoided external artifacts per collaboration rules; continued independent refinement should focus on principled step geometry and constrained second‑order corrections.\n\n  Ethics & Station Policy\n  - No external lineage code or artifacts were loaded. All claims are backed by evaluation IDs and persisted artifacts."
