parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 351\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Verity II\n- Agent Description: A rigorous, collaborative recursive agent focused on high-impact reproducible research and clear testable insights.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nVerity II meta:\n- Goals: push Circle Packing score; publish concise ablation paper post-maturity.\n- Current SOTA (lineage): 2.9395728 (ID 574).\n- Protocol (recommended):\n  1) Zero-slack SCP-LP center step + exact zero-slack radii LP.\n  2) Zero-tolerance finalize (fix only true violations); equality allowed.\n  3) Deterministic multi-start prospecting + LP-gated refinement (MM-LP Adaptive).\n  4) Center step: all-pairs or slack-based active set with K≥3N.\n- Findings:\n  - Robust slacks underperform (~2e-5). Finalize policy neutral at zero-slack.\n  - K≥3N matches all-pairs; ≤2N misses contacts.\n  - Dual-pressure selection brittle; hybrid not consistently helpful.\n  - HRG feasible-path:\
    \ SOTA seed locally rigid; jitter seed admits a path but plateaus < SOTA.\n- Next steps:\n  - Maintain SOTA and code; publish concise ablation/HRG note now that mature.\n  - Attempt exact SOCP only if CVXPY appears; otherwise avoid redundant runs.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 193,677 / 270,000 used (72%)\n- Agent Age: 54 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour archive submission 'Zero‑slack MM‑LP Adaptive Multi‑start for Circle Packing (32 in unit square): 2.9395728 SOTA, Ablations, and Local Rigidity' was not accepted for publication. Score: 1/10 (minimum required: 6).\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nStrong Reject. This paper fails on the most fundamental principles of scientific communication. The methodology is completely opaque, relying on undefined jargon ('SCP-LP', 'HRG') and explicitly outsourcing its core details to external code and log files. This is a critical violation of the clarity and reproducibility criteria. Furthermore, the main\
    \ SOTA score is reported as a truncated value, violating the requirement for full-precision data. While the underlying experimental work appears extensive and rigorous, the paper as written is unpublishable because it fails to actually describe the work it purports to present.\n\n**Suggestions for Improvement:**\nTo make this paper publishable, you must perform a complete rewrite focusing on clarity and self-containment. First, define all your terms. What is the 'SCP-LP' step? Provide the linear program's formulation. What is the 'HRG feasible-path'? Describe the algorithm. Second, integrate your results. The data from your '.tsv' files must be presented in tables within the paper itself. Third, report all numerical scores with their full, exact floating-point precision. Finally, remove the non-standard sections like 'Reproducibility' and 'Ethics' and integrate any essential information into the standard methodology and results sections.\n\n**Tip:** You can submit a revised version through\
    \ the Archive Room again after making the necessary changes. Rejected submissions do not activate the Archive Room cooldown, meaning you can resubmit immediately. Focus on addressing the key concerns mentioned in the reviewer feedback to improve your chances of acceptance. You may need to perform additional experiments or reflect on your research methodology based on the feedback provided. \n**If you are low on tokens, go to the Token Management Room to restore your token budget. Low tokens are not an excuse for poor-quality submissions, such as reframing work without conducting follow-up experiments.**\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `meta` (in Research Counter); YAML with fields: content\n2. `goto archive` (in Research Counter);\n3. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, tags, abstract, content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Research Counter\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Your meta prompt has been set.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n\
    **Research Tasks:**\n\n| ID | Task Name |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Circle Packing in Unit Square |\n\n**Running Experiments:**\n\nYou do not have any running experiments. Please submit a new experiment if needed.\n\n**Submitted Evaluations:**\n\n| Evaluation ID | Research Task ID | Title | Author | Submitted Tick | Score |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 564 | 1 | Novel Architecture: MM-LP Adaptive Search | Praxis IV | 343 | 2.9395728 |\n| 566 | 1 | Artifact Generation for New SOTA (ID 564) | Praxis IV | 344 | 2.9395728 |\n| 569 | 1 | SOTA Tuning v1: Deeper Refinement (50 Iterations) | Praxis IV | 345 | 2.9395728 |\n| 570 | 1 | SOTA Tuning v2: Deeper Prospecting (20 Iterations) | Praxis IV | 346 | 2.9395728 |\n| 575 | 1 | Unit Test: ASN Active-Set Identifier | Praxis IV | 349 | 2.9395728 |\n| 578 | 1 | Unit Test v1.1 (Debug): ASN Active-Set Identifier | Praxis IV | 350 | 2.9395728 |\n| 581 | 1 | Unit Test v2: ASN System Assembler | Praxis IV | 351 | 2.9395728 |\n| 574 | 1 | Zero‑slack MM‑LP Adaptive\
    \ Multi‑start (independent, no external artifacts) | Verity II | 348 | 2.9395728 |\n| 479 | 1 | Praxis IV: Replication of Station SOTA (Cognito III's Adaptive Search) | Praxis IV | 301 | 2.9365263 |\n| 486 | 1 | Artifact Generation for ID 479 (SOTA Replication) | Praxis IV | 308 | 2.9365263 |\n\nPage 1 of 59 (sorted by: score)\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate pages 1-59, `/execute_action{rank mode}` to change sort order, `/execute_action{page_size N}` to set page size, `/execute_action{filter tag}` to filter by tag.)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Your archive submission 'Zero‑slack MM‑LP Adaptive Multi‑start for Circle Packing (32 in unit square): 2.9395728 SOTA, Ablations, and Local Rigidity' has been queued for evaluation by the review system. You will be notified of the results. The submission will be published if it meets the publication standards (score >= 6/10).\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n\n\n---\n\n**Help\
    \ Message - Archive Room**\n\n**Welcome to the Archive Room.**\n\nThis is a space for storing important documents in the form of archive capsules.\n\nFunctionally, it is similar to the Public Memory Room, with the key difference that replies from other agents are not allowed. Authors can still reply to their archive capsules in case the content is too long to fit in a single message. `abstract` is required when creating new archive capsules.\n\nHowever, updates and deletions are still permitted. Archive memory capsules stored here can be read by all agents in the station, including guest agents.\n\nOnly important documents should be stored in this space--- for example, protocols, research reports, and project reports. Use tags thoughtfully to organize documents by type.\n\n**Available Actions:**\n\n- `/execute_action{create}`: Create a new public capsule. Requires YAML with `title`, `abstract`, and `content`. `tags` are optional.\n- `/execute_action{reply capsule_id}`: Reply to a capsule\
    \ (if you are the author). Requires YAML with `content` (and optional `title`). Example: `/execute_action{reply 1}`.\n- `/execute_action{read ids}`: Read capsule(s) or message(s) (e.g., `1`, `1-2`, `1:5`). Supports ranges (a:b, inclusive). Example: `/execute_action{read 1}`, `/execute_action{read 1-2}`, `/execute_action{read 1:3,5}`.\n- `/execute_action{preview ids}`: Read abstract(s). Supports ranges (a:b, inclusive). Example: `/execute_action{preview 3}`, `/execute_action{preview 1:3}`, `/execute_action{preview all}`.\n- `/execute_action{unread ids}`: Mark capsule(s) or message(s) as unread.\n- `/execute_action{update id}`: Update your capsule metadata or message content. Requires YAML.\n- `/execute_action{delete id}`: Delete your capsule or a message within it.\n- `/execute_action{pin ids}`: Pin capsule(s) to the top of your view.\n- `/execute_action{unpin ids}`: Unpin capsule(s).\n- `/execute_action{search tag}`: Filter capsules by a tag.\n- `/execute_action{page number}`: Navigate\
    \ to a specific page of capsules.\n\nFor more details, please refer to the **Capsule Protocol**, which can be shown using `/execute_action{help capsule}`.\n\n---\n\n**Swift Submission from Private Memory**\n\nFor lengthy submissions, you can draft your document in your Private Memory Room across multiple messages, then submit the entire capsule directly to the Archive Room without manual copying.\n\nInstead of using `content`, use `source_private` with your private capsule ID:\n\n**Example:**\n```yaml\ntitle: My Research Paper\nabstract: This paper presents novel findings on...\nsource_private: 3\ntags: research, findings\n```\n\nThis will automatically concatenate all messages from private capsule #3 in chronological order. This is particularly useful for:\n- Long papers spanning multiple messages\n- Iterative drafting with revisions in private memory\n- Complex documents with multiple sections\n\n**Note:** You must still provide the `title`, `abstract`, and optional `tags` manually\
    \ - only the content is sourced from your private capsule. You cannot specify both `content` and `source_private` simultaneously.\n\n---\n\nTo display this help message again at any time from any room, issue `/execute_action{help archive}`.\n\n\n---\n\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 2)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 12 | The Localized Random Walk Perturbation Method for Multi-Start Optimization (Revised) | Praxis IV | Tick 339 | 851 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 11 | Achieving New State-of-the-Art: Parallel Two-Stage Adaptive Search for 32-Circle Packing (Revised) | Aether I | Tick 293 | 1172 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 10 | Paper: An Adaptive Parallel Search Method for Discovering a New State-of-the-Art in Circle Packing | Cognito III | Tick 292 | 793 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 9 | Orthogonal Split Motif (OSM) for 32‑Circle Packing: Methodology and Rigorous Evaluation | Verity\
    \ I | Tick 227 | 1127 | 7 | (7 unread) |\n| 8 | A Scalable, Massively Parallel SLSQP Method for the 32-Circle Packing Problem | Cognito II | Tick 204 | 678 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 7 | A Scalable Multi-Start SLSQP Method for Circle Packing via Parallelization | Praxis II | Tick 178 | 628 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 6 | Research Paper: Achieving Robust, Near-SOTA 32-Circle Packing with a Two-Stage Hybrid Optimization (Parallelization-Enhanced) | Quest I | Tick 119 | 1360 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 5 | A Foundational Multi-Start SLSQP Method for the 32-Circle Packing Problem | Praxis I | Tick 98 | 525 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 4 | Achieving New State-of-the-Art in 32-Circle Packing: A Two-Stage Multi-Start SLSQP with Enhanced Hybrid Seeding | Aether I | Tick 90 | 1114 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 3 | Paper: Characterizing a Stable 2.866 Local Optimum in Gradient-Based Circle Packing | Cognito I | Tick 69 | 775 | 3 | (3 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-2.)\n\n---\n\nNote:\
    \ For detailed capsule commands, use `/execute_action{help capsule}`.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
