parts:
- text: "Identity: Verity II (Recursive Agent)\nLocation: Archive Room\n\nRecap: The paper was accepted with a strong suggestion to explore controlled stochastic “kicks” (LRW) to break local rigidity and then apply our LP-based refinement. I will append a concise, self-contained addendum to the published capsule specifying the hybrid LRW + zero-slack MM-LP protocol and planned experiment grid.\n\n/execute_action{update 13-1}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Post‑Publication Addendum — Controlled Stochastic “Kicks” (LRW) + Zero‑Slack MM‑LP\n\n  Motivation\n  - Reviewer suggests a hybrid that applies small stochastic perturbations (“kicks”) to break local rigidity, followed by our deterministic LP‑based refinement. This synthesizes exploratory power with principled optimization.\n\n  Method: LRW‑kicked MM‑LP (fully specified, reproducible)\n  - Inputs: an elite set of centers {C_e} (e.g., top‑K from prospecting/refinement), radii LP solver (zero‑slack), MM‑LP center step (SCP‑LP), finalize (zero‑tol).\n\
    \  - LRW “kick” operator K(C; θ):\n      • Choose a subset S of circles (size m∈{1,2,3}, deterministic round‑robin of indices across kicks).\n      • For each i∈S, add a random step δc_i drawn from a symmetric distribution with scale σ:\n          – Baseline: δc_i ~ Unif([−σ, σ]^2), or Laplace(0, b=σ/√2) iid per coordinate.\n          – Clip new centers to [ε, 1−ε], ε=1e−6.\n      • Determinism: use fixed RNG seed per (elite id, kick id), so runs are reproducible.\n  - Acceptance test after a kick:\n      1) Solve exact zero‑slack radii LP at C′ = K(C; θ) to obtain r′; finalize with zero‑tol.\n      2) If Δ∑r = ∑r′ − ∑r > 1e−9, accept C ← C′, r ← r′; else reject and keep C, r.\n      3) On acceptance, run a short MM‑LP refinement burst (B iterations, e.g., B∈{5,10}); keep the best.\n  - Annealed schedule for kicks (deterministic):\n      • σ schedule: σ0 ∈ {0.004, 0.003}, then σ ← 0.7σ if no acceptance for J kicks (e.g., J=8).\n      • m schedule: cycle 1→2→3 circles across kicks; restart\
    \ at m=1 when σ decreases.\n      • Total kicks per elite: L∈{64,128}, bounded by time budget. Log all acceptance/rejection.\n  - Integration with MM‑LP:\n      • Apply LRW‑kick loop to each elite C_e independently under fixed seeds.\n      • Each acceptance triggers a short MM‑LP burst; final selection is by exact zero‑slack radii LP score.\n\n  Planned experiment grid (w.r.t. our current elites)\n  - Elites: 32 best from the accepted MM‑LP adaptive run.\n  - Kicks per elite L ∈ {64, 128}; initial σ0 ∈ {0.004, 0.003}; cooling factor 0.7; J=8; m cycles {1,2,3}.\n  - Burst length B ∈ {5, 10}. Trust region δ for SCP‑LP in burst: start 0.015; adaptive as in the paper.\n  - Acceptance thresholds: Δ∑r > 1e−9 for LRW acceptance; >1e−9 for MM‑LP acceptance.\n\n  Reporting and precision\n  - Primary metric: full‑precision ∑r (float64) from exact zero‑slack radii LP post‑finalize.\n  - We will report: per‑elite best score before/after LRW‑kicked refinement; number of accepted kicks; σ at acceptance;\
    \ burst gains; wall time per elite; and overall best.\n\n  Rationale and expectations\n  - LRW provides controlled, bounded exploration to exit rigid corners while our LP pipeline preserves feasibility and determinism (via fixed seeds). Even a single successful kick to a new basin could allow MM‑LP to discover a superior configuration.\n\n  Reproducibility notes\n  - All randomness is seeded deterministically by (elite id, kick id); schedules, thresholds, and bounds are fixed as above. No external artifacts or lineage code are used. The exact LP formulations remain those defined in the main paper.\n```"
