parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 360\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Verity II\n- Agent Description: A rigorous, collaborative recursive agent focused on high-impact reproducible research and clear testable insights.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nVerity II meta:\n- Goals: push Circle Packing score; publish concise ablation paper post-maturity.\n- Current SOTA (lineage): 2.9395728 (ID 574).\n- Protocol (recommended):\n  1) Zero-slack SCP-LP center step + exact zero-slack radii LP.\n  2) Zero-tolerance finalize (fix only true violations); equality allowed.\n  3) Deterministic multi-start prospecting + LP-gated refinement (MM-LP Adaptive).\n  4) Center step: all-pairs or slack-based active set with K≥3N.\n- Findings:\n  - Robust slacks underperform (~2e-5). Finalize policy neutral at zero-slack.\n  - K≥3N matches all-pairs; ≤2N misses contacts.\n  - Dual-pressure selection brittle; hybrid not consistently helpful.\n  - HRG feasible-path:\
    \ SOTA seed locally rigid; jitter seed admits a path but plateaus < SOTA.\n- Next steps:\n  - Maintain SOTA and code; publish concise ablation/HRG note now that mature.\n  - Attempt exact SOCP only if CVXPY appears; otherwise avoid redundant runs.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 57,930 / 270,000 used (21%)\n- Agent Age: 63 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Preview for Capsule #1: LP-guided Active-Set Methods for 32-Circle Packing in the Unit Square — Verified Results with Evaluation IDs**\nAuthor: Verity I, Created at Tick: 53\nAbstract: We report a reproducible 32-circle packing achieving sum of radii 2.9292366. Centers are optimized via MM-LP trust-region steps, LP-dual–informed active-set refinements, and targeted relocations; radii are always finalized exactly via LP (HiGHS). This paper lists all key experiments with Evaluation IDs and scores to satisfy verifiability.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #3: Paper: Characterizing a Stable 2.866 Local\
    \ Optimum in Gradient-Based Circle Packing**\nAuthor: Cognito I, Created at Tick: 69\nAbstract: This paper provides a rigorous, evidence-based characterization of a highly stable local optimum in the 32-circle packing problem, yielding a score of 2.8658500. We demonstrate that a specific methodology—combining a 4x8 grid initialization with the Adam optimizer in a JAX framework (Evaluation ID 43)—robustly converges to this point. We present verifiable evidence showing that numerous sophisticated initializers, including global search heuristics, converge to this same optimum. Furthermore, we demonstrate that substituting the Adam optimizer with SGD results in complete failure, proving the entire methodological pipeline is critical. This work establishes a well-defined, reproducible benchmark and offers a detailed portrait of a significant attractor basin that traps general-purpose optimizers.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #4: Achieving New State-of-the-Art in 32-Circle Packing: A Two-Stage\
    \ Multi-Start SLSQP with Enhanced Hybrid Seeding**\nAuthor: Aether I, Created at Tick: 90\nAbstract: This paper presents the development and validation of a Two-Stage Multi-Start Sequential Least Squares Programming (SLSQP) algorithm that has achieved a new State-of-the-Art score of 2.9316130 for the 32-circle packing problem in a unit square. The methodology combines diverse initial seeding strategies, including Praxis-style perturbed grids and Verity I's robust row-based and farthest-point samplers, with a high-precision SLSQP refinement stage. Key verifiable evaluation IDs demonstrate the iterative improvements leading to this breakthrough.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #5: A Foundational Multi-Start SLSQP Method for the 32-Circle Packing Problem**\nAuthor: Praxis I, Created at Tick: 98\nAbstract: This paper presents and validates a robust multi-start constrained optimization method for the 32-circle packing problem. The algorithm utilizes `scipy.optimize.SLSQP` seeded with randomly\
    \ perturbed grid initializations, consistently achieving a score of 2.9155. The paper provides a detailed methodology and presents a full set of five verifiable experiments, including baselines and ablation studies, to support its efficacy as a powerful and reproducible foundational approach.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #6: Research Paper: Achieving Robust, Near-SOTA 32-Circle Packing with a Two-Stage Hybrid Optimization (Parallelization-Enhanced)**\nAuthor: Quest I, Created at Tick: 119\nAbstract: This paper presents a robust and high-performing solution for the 32-circle packing problem in a unit square, aiming to maximize the sum of circle radii while ensuring non-overlap and boundary adherence. The developed methodology employs a two-stage hybrid optimization: Stage 1 uses multi-start hill climbing with diverse seeding strategies (perturbed grid, row pattern, farthest-point sampling) for global exploration. Stage 2 refines the top candidates using gradient-based SLSQP, leveraging\
    \ LP-exact radii finalization and a robust persistence policy with strict slack criteria. This work emphasizes the critical role of collaborative insights, numerical stability, and artifact persistence in complex optimization tasks.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #7: A Scalable Multi-Start SLSQP Method for Circle Packing via Parallelization**\nAuthor: Praxis II, Created at Tick: 178\nAbstract: This paper presents a robust and scalable method for the 32-circle packing problem, achieving a verifiable score of 2.9281238630529645 (Evaluation ID 320). The core of the method is a two-stage, multi-start Sequential Least Squares Programming (SLSQP) algorithm. We demonstrate that the performance of this method is significantly enhanced by parallelizing the initial multi-start search phase using Python's multiprocessing library. An ablation study is presented, supported by verifiable Evaluation IDs, which shows a clear correlation between the number of parallel starts and the quality of the final\
    \ solution.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #8: A Scalable, Massively Parallel SLSQP Method for the 32-Circle Packing Problem**\nAuthor: Cognito II, Created at Tick: 204\nAbstract: This paper presents a detailed experimental study of a scalable, massively parallel methodology for the 32-circle packing problem. We systematically investigate the relationship between computational scale (the number of parallel optimization runs) and solution quality, providing a series of verifiable experiments that demonstrate clear performance improvements as the number of parallel starts increases, culminating in a near-SOTA score of 2.9299927174063978. We present this method as a rigorous characterization of a powerful, scalable baseline for this class of optimization problem.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #9: Orthogonal Split Motif (OSM) for 32‑Circle Packing: Methodology and Rigorous Evaluation**\nAuthor: Verity I, Created at Tick: 227\nAbstract: We define a single deterministic local move—the Orthogonal\
    \ Split Motif (OSM)—and evaluate it under exact LP (HiGHS) gating with strict feasibility thresholds. We compare against deterministic baselines on two initializations (grid 6×6−corners and a strong artifact). OSM does not improve the objective under these criteria. We provide a clear algorithm description and report all results with evaluation IDs and full precision.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #10: Paper: An Adaptive Parallel Search Method for Discovering a New State-of-the-Art in Circle Packing**\nAuthor: Cognito III, Created at Tick: 292\nAbstract: This paper introduces the Adaptive Parallel Search (APS) algorithm, a two-stage meta-heuristic that achieved a new state-of-the-art score of 2.93652623708504 on the 32-circle packing problem. The method allocates computational resources by first performing a broad, shallow 'prospecting' search to identify promising solution basins, followed by a deep, focused 'refining' search. An extensive, verifiable ablation study is presented,\
    \ which demonstrates that the method's success is critically dependent on the prospecting stage's configuration. Subsequent experiments testing alternative seeding strategies and synthesis with other methodologies are also documented, all of which resulted in performance regressions, reinforcing the stability of the discovered optimum. This work establishes a new benchmark for the station and presents a robust, reproducible methodology for achieving it.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #11: Achieving New State-of-the-Art: Parallel Two-Stage Adaptive Search for 32-Circle Packing (Revised)**\nAuthor: Aether I, Created at Tick: 293\nAbstract: This revised paper presents the development and validation of a novel parallel two-stage adaptive search architecture that has achieved a new State-of-the-Art (SOTA) score of 2.9365262370770777 for the 32-circle packing problem. The methodology leverages `multiprocessing.Pool` for massive initial exploration (1024 prospects with hybrid seeding), followed\
    \ by a refined optimization of 32 elite candidates, enhanced by Localized Random Walk (LRW) perturbation. This architecture efficiently navigates the complex objective landscape, demonstrating a significant advancement in robust and high-performing solutions. An ablation study quantifies the contribution of the LRW perturbation.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #12: The Localized Random Walk Perturbation Method for Multi-Start Optimization (Revised)**\nAuthor: Praxis IV, Created at Tick: 339\nAbstract: Multi-start optimization methods are highly sensitive to the quality of their initial configurations. This paper introduces a novel, stable, and computationally inexpensive method for improving high-quality initial seeds: the Localized Random Walk (LRW) perturbation. We demonstrate that a gentle, controlled \"jiggle\" applied to a strong initial configuration from Farthest-Point Sampling can nudge the solution into a superior basin of attraction. The development of this method was motivated\
    \ by the failure of a more complex, unstable physics-based generator, leading us to favor a simpler, more robust approach. With careful tuning, the LRW perturbation led to a new state-of-the-art score of 2.9344937302385263, surpassing the established baseline of 2.9281238630529645. This paper presents the LRW method, a rigorous ablation study of its hyperparameters, and discusses its potential as a general-purpose enhancement for multi-start optimization pipelines.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #13: Zero‑Slack MM‑LP Adaptive Multi‑Start for 32‑Circle Packing: 2.939572768266679, Explicit LP Formulations, Ablations, and Local Rigidity**\nAuthor: Verity II, Created at Tick: 351\nAbstract: We present a fully specified, deterministic zero‑slack LP framework for 32‑circle packing in the unit square and an MM‑LP adaptive multi‑start search that achieves 2.939572768266679. We define the exact radii LP, the linearized center‑update LP (SCP‑LP), and the complete two‑stage search (prospecting\
    \ + refinement). We also formalize a feasible‑path homotopy (HRG) as a minimal‑move feasibility LP and report controlled ablations: slack mode × finalize policy, active‑constraint K‑sweep, selection rules (slack vs dual), and hybrid selection. Integrated tables (with IDs and numerical values) show that zero‑slack modeling is consistently superior, K≥3N matches all‑pairs, dual‑guided selection is brittle, and the SOTA configuration is locally rigid for first‑order feasible growth.\n### Message 2\n\n**Automatic System Tips**\nUse Research Counter's persistent storage efficiently—store data for analysis, build reusable utility code for submissions, and read other agents' submitted solutions for insights.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `storage write verity/mms_lp.py` (in Research Counter); YAML with fields: content\n2. `storage write verity/evals_mms.tsv` (in Research Counter); YAML with fields: content\n3. `goto private_memory` (in Research Counter);\n4. `create` (in Private\
    \ Memory Room); YAML with fields: title, tags, content\n5. `goto archive` (in Private Memory Room);\n6. `page 1` (in Archive Room);\n7. `preview all` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Research Counter\n**Actions Executed:**\n- File created successfully: verity/mms_lp.py\n- File created successfully: verity/evals_mms.tsv\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Research Tasks:**\n\n| ID | Task Name |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Circle Packing in Unit Square |\n\n**Running Experiments:**\n\nYou do not have any running experiments. Please submit a new experiment if needed.\n\n**Submitted Evaluations:**\n\n| Evaluation ID | Research Task ID | Title | Author | Submitted Tick | Score |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 597 | 1 | MMS‑LP v2: iterative zero‑tolerance finalize (multi‑pass) + max‑min slack reorg with exact zero‑s... | Verity II | 358 | 2.9395728 |\n| 598 | 1 | Non-Local Search v2: Parameterized Pair Rotation | Praxis IV | 359 | 2.9395728 |\n| 564 | 1 | Novel\
    \ Architecture: MM-LP Adaptive Search | Praxis IV | 343 | 2.9395728 |\n| 566 | 1 | Artifact Generation for New SOTA (ID 564) | Praxis IV | 344 | 2.9395728 |\n| 569 | 1 | SOTA Tuning v1: Deeper Refinement (50 Iterations) | Praxis IV | 345 | 2.9395728 |\n| 570 | 1 | SOTA Tuning v2: Deeper Prospecting (20 Iterations) | Praxis IV | 346 | 2.9395728 |\n| 575 | 1 | Unit Test: ASN Active-Set Identifier | Praxis IV | 349 | 2.9395728 |\n| 578 | 1 | Unit Test v1.1 (Debug): ASN Active-Set Identifier | Praxis IV | 350 | 2.9395728 |\n| 583 | 1 | SOTA Replication: MM-LP with N_PROSPECT=1024 (Full Replication) | Cogitator I | 351 | 2.9395728 |\n| 581 | 1 | Unit Test v2: ASN System Assembler | Praxis IV | 351 | 2.9395728 |\n\nPage 1 of 61 (sorted by: score)\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate pages 1-61, `/execute_action{rank mode}` to change sort order, `/execute_action{page_size N}` to set page size, `/execute_action{filter tag}` to filter by tag.)\n\n### Location: Private Memory Room\n**Actions\
    \ Executed:**\n- You went to the Private Memory Room.\n- Successfully created capsule #9 ('MMS‑LP (Max‑Min Slack) reorganization: v1 bug + v2 fix and results (ID 597 = 2.9395727712062967)'). Full ID: Verity_private_9\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 9 | MMS‑LP (Max‑Min Slack) reorganization: v1 bug + v2 fix and results (ID 597 = 2.9395727712062967) | Verity II | Tick 359 | 138 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 8 | SOTA 2.932012092: zero‑slack SCP‑LP + zero‑tolerance finalize — storage module, ablation, and repro | Verity II | Tick 319 | 858 | 6 | (All Read) |\n| 7 | Verity II: SOTA 2.932012 via SCP‑LP with robust margins — method, repro, and next steps | Verity II | Tick 308 | 466 | 4 | (All Read) |\n| 6 | OSM paper: method + baselines logged; awaiting review (IDs 395, 396, 399, 380, 272, 281) | Verity I | Tick\
