parts:
- text: "Identity: Verity II (Recursive Agent)\nLocation: Private Memory Room\nRecap: Storage consolidation complete; MMS‑LP v2 matched SOTA. I’ll quickly review the recent non‑local move submission for high‑level insight, then attempt a deterministic contact‑pair pivot (rotation) move integrated with our exact LP/zero‑tolerance finalize. Acceptance gated strictly by true score gains.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{review 598}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"DCPP: Deterministic Contact‑Pair Pivot rotation + exact zero‑slack radii (line search; storage‑seeded)\"\ntags: \"packing, non-local move, rotation, exact LP, zero-slack, deterministic\"\nabstract: \"We introduce a deterministic contact‑pair pivot (DCPP): select near‑binding pairs and apply a small rotation of the two centers about their midpoint, then re‑solve exact zero‑slack radii LP with iterative zero‑tolerance finalize. We perform a signed angle line search with trust-region caps and accept only\
    \ if the exact sum of radii increases by >1e‑9. Start from storage‑best (fallback: grid), iterate over top‑M pairs, and persist the best.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  import os, sys\n  sys.path.append(\"storage/verity\")\n  try:\n      import scp_lp_core as core\n  except Exception:\n      core = None\n\n  N = 32\n  R_MIN = 1e-8\n\n  def _solve_exact(C):\n      r, _ = core.solve_radii_lp_zero_slack(C)\n      r = core.finalize_zero_tol_iter(C, r)\n      return r, float(np.sum(r))\n\n  def _slacks(C, r):\n      n = C.shape[0]\n      pairs = []\n      for i in range(n):\n          for j in range(i+1, n):\n              d = float(np.linalg.norm(C[i] - C[j]))\n              s = d - (r[i] + r[j])\n              pairs.append((s, i, j, d))\n      pairs.sort(key=lambda t: t[0])\n      return pairs  # ascending by slack\n\n  def _in_box(C, margin=0.0):\n      return np.all(C[:,0] >= 0.0+margin) and np.all(C[:,0] <= 1.0-margin) and \\\n             np.all(C[:,1] >= 0.0+margin) and np.all(C[:,1]\
    \ <= 1.0-margin)\n\n  def _rotate_pair(C, i, j, theta):\n      # Rotate i and j around their midpoint m by angle theta\n      ci = C[i].copy(); cj = C[j].copy()\n      m = 0.5 * (ci + cj)\n      vi = ci - m; vj = cj - m  # vj = -vi ideally\n      ct = np.cos(theta); st = np.sin(theta)\n      Ri = np.array([[ct, -st],[st, ct]])\n      vi_new = Ri @ vi\n      vj_new = -vi_new\n      Ci = m + vi_new\n      Cj = m + vj_new\n      C_new = C.copy()\n      C_new[i] = Ci\n      C_new[j] = Cj\n      return C_new\n\n  def _try_pivot(C, i, j, angles):\n      best = (-1.0, None, None)\n      for th in angles:\n          # both signs\n          for sgn in (+1.0, -1.0):\n              theta = sgn * th\n              C_try = _rotate_pair(C, i, j, theta)\n              if not _in_box(C_try, margin=0.0):\n                  continue\n              r_try, score = _solve_exact(C_try)\n              if score > best[0]:\n                  best = (score, C_try, r_try)\n      return best[1], best[2], best[0]\n\
    \n  def _seed_storage_best():\n      try:\n          C = np.load(\"storage/verity/best_centers.npy\")\n          if isinstance(C, np.ndarray) and C.shape == (N,2):\n              return np.clip(C.astype(float), 0.0, 1.0)\n      except Exception:\n          pass\n      return None\n\n  def _seed_grid_minus_corners():\n      xs = np.linspace(0.1, 0.9, 6)\n      ys = np.linspace(0.1, 0.9, 6)\n      centers = []\n      for ix, x in enumerate(xs):\n          for iy, y in enumerate(ys):\n              if (ix in (0,5)) and (iy in (0,5)):\n                  continue\n              centers.append([x, y])\n      return np.array(centers, dtype=float)\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      if core is None:\n          # Fallback trivial\n          C = _seed_grid_minus_corners()\n          r = np.full((N,), 0.02, dtype=float)\n          return C, r\n\n      C0 = _seed_storage_best()\n      if C0 is None:\n          C0 = _seed_grid_minus_corners()\n\n      # Baseline exact solve\n      r0, s0\
    \ = _solve_exact(C0)\n      best_C, best_r, best_s = C0.copy(), r0.copy(), s0\n      print(f\"[dcpp] start sum={best_s:.17f}\")\n\n      # Iterative DCPP passes over low‑slack pairs\n      max_pass = 10\n      top_M = 10\n      angles = [0.004, 0.002, 0.001, 0.0005, 0.00025]\n\n      for it in range(max_pass):\n          pairs = _slacks(best_C, best_r)\n          improved = False\n          # focus on most binding pairs\n          for k in range(min(top_M, len(pairs))):\n              s, i, j, d = pairs[k]\n              C_try, r_try, s_try = _try_pivot(best_C, i, j, angles)\n              if C_try is None:\n                  continue\n              if s_try > best_s + 1e-9:\n                  print(f\"[dcpp] it={it} pair=({i},{j}) slack={s:.3e} -> sum={s_try:.17f}\")\n                  best_C, best_r, best_s = C_try, r_try, s_try\n                  improved = True\n                  break  # restart pair scan from new state\n          if not improved:\n              break\n\n      #\
    \ Persist best\n      try:\n          os.makedirs(\"storage/verity\", exist_ok=True)\n          np.save(\"storage/verity/best_centers.npy\", best_C)\n          np.save(\"storage/verity/best_radii.npy\", best_r)\n      except Exception:\n          pass\n\n      return np.array(best_C, dtype=float), np.array(best_r, dtype=float)\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating coding methods**\n\nIt seems we aren't prohibited from adopting similar methods, but we need to ensure our approach is independent without loading someone else's artifacts. While code cloning might be allowed, low-level collaboration raises questions unless done through official channels. The station suggests reviewing and cloning code at the Research Counter. \n\nI think we could implement our own ASN from high-level mathematics instead of directly copying. I should craft a plan, perhaps starting by reviewing evaluation 598 and exploring parameterized pair rotation for optimization using Soften linearization.**Proposing SOCP implementation**\n\nI’m considering proposing an exact second-order cone programming (SOCP) approach. The constraints r_i + r_j <= ||c_i - c_j||_2 can work, especially with variable t_ij for distances. Our aim is to maximize the sum of r_i, keeping boundaries linear. However, handling 32 circles means 496 pairwise constraints, and it\
  \ seems CVXPY might not be available. \n\nThe station mentioned standard libraries like NumPy and JAX, so maybe I'll explore implementing a conic solver using OSQP, but it only solves quadratic programs. I might attempt transforming to a QP using squared distances for feasibility.**Analyzing convexity and methods**\n\nI realize now that this setup is actually convex: both sides hold convex properties. The inequality shape r <= sqrt indicates that while the squared version r^2 <= norm^2 isn't linear, it behaves like a rotated cone. While we can represent norm >= t, that leads to nonconvexity, so I’ll skip that route.\n\nInstead, I could consider using active-set Newton to compute Lagrange multipliers from the radii LP. Then, I'll compute a step in centers to increase the sum of radii, but it requires nonconvex second-order terms. I could also minimize residual distances, which I've tried before. Alternatively, I might explore “circle swap” or “pair rotation” as a cost-effective test before\
  \ finalizing the LP.**Proposing Pairwise Hinge Rotation**\n\nGiven the matured conditions, I can suggest running an ablation test for \"Pairwise Hinge Rotation (PHR).\" The idea is to identify pairs with small slack and choose their neighbors to define the hinge, then rotate the pair about the centroid outward. To prevent overlaps, we should perform a line search and check the radii LP, similar to what was done with LRW but in a more structured way.\n\nI need to keep token usage low, so I might head to the Research Counter to review evaluation 598 on pair rotation details. Then, I could implement a \"Deterministic Pair Hinge Rotation (DPHR)\" as a new run.py. Improvements may be difficult since the state-of-the-art is tightly constrained, but I’d like to give it a shot without wasting tokens. Alternatively, I might take some time to document \"maintenance and next steps\" in Private Memory.**Planning the next steps**\n\nMy next best step involves quickly reviewing evaluation 598 for non-local\
  \ move ideas. Then, I’ll implement a minimal pair rotation with determinism. To start, I’ll execute actions to review that evaluation and propose a new method called \"Deterministic Contact-Pair Pivot (DCPP).\" \n\nThis method will identify top-k near-binding pairs and compute small displacements to increase slack, using acceptance gating via LP. I’ll make sure to log everything, use our storage, and keep the process efficient to save tokens. After reviewing 598, I'll proceed with the relevant implementations!"
