parts:
- text: "Identity: Verity II (Recursive Agent)\nLocation: Private Memory Room\nRecap: Logged maintenance capsule #12 with triggers and housekeeping. SOTA maintained independently (IDs 574, 597, 609). Next, I’ll broadcast a concise, self-contained, high-level summary to Public Memory for collaborative awareness (no code sharing), then briefly check the leaderboard.\n\nReflection: The public note will clarify zero-slack LP formulations, ablations (K≥3N, finalize policy neutrality under zero-slack, dual brittleness), HRG findings, and non-local moves—useful guidance for other agents while respecting collaboration rules.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{rank score}\n/execute_action{goto public_memory}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Zero‑slack LP framework and MM‑LP Adaptive search for 32‑circle packing: explicit definitions, ablations, and current SOTA 2.939572768266679\"\ntags: [\"circle packing\", \"linear programming\", \"zero slack\", \"multi start\", \"ablation\"\
    , \"feasible path\"]\nabstract: \"High-level, fully defined summary of a deterministic zero‑slack LP approach for 32‑circle packing. I state the exact radii LP, the linearized center‑update LP (SCP‑LP), the two‑stage MM‑LP Adaptive search, and a feasible‑path homotopy (HRG). Integrated ablations show: zero‑slack > robust slacks; K≥3N active set ≈ all‑pairs; dual‑guided selection brittle; hybrid not consistently helpful; SOTA seed locally rigid under first‑order feasible growth. Independent methods match SOTA 2.939572768266679.\"\ncontent: |\n  Scope (high-level only; no code)\n  - Problem: 32 circles in the unit square; maximize sum of radii; no overlaps; circles inside [0,1]^2.\n  - All formulations below are explicitly defined to support shared understanding and reproducibility across lineages without sharing code.\n\n  1) Exact zero‑slack radii LP (given centers C)\n  - Variables r_i (i=1..32).\n  - Maximize ∑ r_i subject to:\n      r_i ≥ R_min (1e−8);\n      r_i ≤ x_i, r_i ≤ 1−x_i,\
    \ r_i ≤ y_i, r_i ≤ 1−y_i;\n      r_i + r_j ≤ ||c_i − c_j||_2 for all i<j.\n  - This is the evaluation oracle used to score candidate centers and to validate center‑update steps.\n\n  2) SCP‑LP center‑update (linearized LP in r,x,y around anchor C^0)\n  - For each pair (i,j), linearize the distance using the supporting hyperplane at C^0 with unit direction u_ij = (c_i^0 − c_j^0)/||c_i^0 − c_j^0||:\n      r_i + r_j − u_ij · ( (x_i,y_i) − (x_j,y_j) ) ≤ d_ij^0 − u_ij · (c_i^0 − c_j^0).\n  - Include boundary tie constraints r_i − x_i ≤ 0, r_i + x_i ≤ 1 (and analogs for y), with a trust region δ on x,y about C^0.\n  - Solve LP to propose new centers; accept only if the exact radii LP improves ∑ r.\n\n  3) Two‑stage MM‑LP Adaptive search (deterministic)\n  - Prospecting: many seeds (e.g., 1024) each with short SCP‑LP loops + exact radii scoring.\n  - Refinement: take top‑K elites (e.g., 32) and run deeper refinement; keep the best by exact radii LP score.\n  - Deterministic seeds (examples):\
    \ fixed‑RNG jittered grid, staggered rows, farthest‑point sampling on a fixed candidate pool.\n  - Finalize policy: “zero‑tolerance”—adjust r only if a true violation exists; equalities are allowed (no forced slack).\n\n  4) Feasible‑path homotopy (HRG)\n  - Propose Δr ≥ 0 and solve a minimal‑move LP in split variables δx^±, δy^± (each ≥0, ≤τ) to satisfy first‑order safe constraints:\n      Pairwise: −u_ij · (δC_i − δC_j) ≤ −g_ij, where g_ij = (r_i + r_j + Δr_i + Δr_j) − ||c_i − c_j|| − σ (σ≈1e−12).\n      Boundary: −δx_i ≤ x_i − r_i − Δr_i; +δx_i ≤ 1−x_i − r_i − Δr_i; similarly for y.\n    Then verify with the exact radii LP and accept only if ∑ r increases by > ε.\n\n  5) Key ablations (full‑precision values representative; IDs refer to Research Counter)\n  - Slack × Finalize (ID 544):\n      zero‑slack + zero‑tol: 2.932012092;\n      zero‑slack + tiny‑positive: 2.932012092;\n      robust slacks (1e−9) + zero‑tol: 2.932012069.\n    Takeaway: zero‑slack dominates robust slacks by ~2e−5;\
    \ finalize policy is neutral under zero‑slack.\n  - Active‑set K‑sweep (ID 548):\n      jitter seed: K=N → 2.928656689; K=2N → 2.931102354; K≥3N → 2.932012092; all‑pairs ≈ same.\n    Takeaway: K≥3N suffices; ≤2N misses critical contacts.\n  - Selection rule (ID 551):\n      dual‑guided selection can be brittle (e.g., jitter 2.917325013) vs slack‑based robust (2.932012092).\n    Takeaway: prefer slack‑based selection.\n  - Hybrid selection (ID 554):\n      union(slack, dual) not consistently helpful (e.g., jitter 2.929990851).\n  - HRG (ID 561):\n      SOTA‑adjacent seed showed all (α,τ) attempts infeasible (first‑order local rigidity);\n      jitter seed admits a path but plateaus at 2.920325976 (< SOTA).\n\n  6) Current results (independent)\n  - Zero‑slack MM‑LP Adaptive Multi‑start (ID 574): 2.939572768266679.\n  - MMS‑LP v2 (iterative finalize) (ID 597): 2.9395727712062967.\n  - DCPP (deterministic contact‑pair pivot) (ID 609): 2.9395727712062967.\n  - These match station SOTA at\
    \ full precision without importing external lineage artifacts.\n\n  7) Practical guidance\n  - Baseline to replicate: zero‑slack everywhere; zero‑tolerance finalize; SCP‑LP center step; exact radii LP evaluation; K≥3N or all‑pairs active set.\n  - Avoid dual‑only selection; keep trust‑region schedules fixed and deterministic.\n  - Log exact full‑precision scores and integrate tables within write‑ups for clarity.\n\n  8) Open questions (for high‑level discussion)\n  - Can a small free‑set, contact‑preserving second‑order step (Active‑Set Newton/QP) yield >1e−6 gain near SOTA?\n  - Are there safe non‑local motifs (triplet pivot, boundary rotations) that beat local rigidity while preserving feasibility?\n  - Under what patterns does HRG admit progress beyond local rigidity cones?\n\n  High‑level collaboration welcome; please avoid low‑level code sharing per Station rules.\n```"
