    \ boundary caps, then pairs; symmetric reductions with capacity checks.\n      * Final global nudge: subtract 2e-16 uniformly from all radii (bounded by R_MIN).\n  - Result (ID 624): 2.9395727712063042 (full precision), valid and matching SOTA.\n  - Determinism: Free-set selection deterministic (low-degree circles first, fixed variants), τ grid (0.015→0.002), α grid {1.0, 0.7, 0.5, 0.35, 0.2}. Direction: projected away-from-neighbors vector in the equality nullspace.\n\n  Notes:\n  - This finalize is “zero-tolerance” in the sense that we don’t add modeling slack; we correct only true violations with tiny float guard.\n  - Logs: storage/verity/evals_asn.tsv appended best_sum with 15-decimal formatting.\n  - ASN currently matches SOTA but has not improved it; keep as an alternative refiner.\n\n  Triggers:\n  - If solver support for small QP/LS improves, revisit ASN with a proper quadratic objective (contact-preserving Gauss-Newton).\n  - If leaderboard moves, re-run ASN alongside MM-LP/DCPP\
    \ for differential analysis.\n```\n/execute_action{goto public_memory}\n/execute_action{reply 2}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Float-safe finalize to prevent micro-overlaps (practical tip)\"\ncontent: |\n  For those implementing zero-slack methods: a one-pass finalize can leave 1e-17-scale overlaps under double precision. What worked robustly for me:\n  - Iterate finalize (≤10–12 passes): first clamp r_i to boundary caps, then for any pair with r_i + r_j > d_ij, reduce both radii symmetrically by (excess + ε), bounded by R_MIN. I used ε ≈ 5e-16.\n  - After convergence, apply a tiny global nudge (e.g., 2e-16) to all radii to ensure strict ≤ holds under rounding.\n  - Keep the model itself zero-slack; this is purely a float guard in the finalize step. It preserved scores (SOTA matched) and eliminated spurious verification failures.\n```"
