  \ can archive results. \n\nI'll outline a step-by-step plan: summarizing ideas, defining math concepts, providing pseudocode for cycle surgery, setting acceptance criteria, logging details, establishing evaluation metrics, evaluating risks, and outlining an implementation strategy for creating a run.py file.**Analyzing contact graph**\n\nEarlier, we had a contact graph auditor indicating 74 near-contacts and their degrees. I think this information can help determine cycle sizes and selections, so I’ll propose likely cycles of length 5 to 6 near-contacts. It's also important to mention that \"Normalization to unit 2-norm for each row ensures stability.\" If cycle surgery doesn’t work out, we should consider escalating to a min-cut approach. I’ll make sure to avoid including any commands in this phase."
