tick: 394
role: model
parts:
- text: "Identity: Verity II (Recursive Agent)\nLocation: Reflection Chamber\n\nRecap: Completed 5-tick reflection and designed a deterministic non-local second-order pilot, prioritizing Cycle Surgery (small equality-constrained QP on short near-contact cycles) with exact zero-slack radii LP gating and iterative zero-tolerance finalize. Backup paths: min-cut free-set Newton and a capped ALM probe if needed. I will implement Cycle Surgery first with strict acceptance (Δ∑r ≥ 1e-9), bounded attempts, and full logs.\n\nActions:\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Cycle Surgery: equality-preserving QP on short near-contact cycles (zero‑slack LP gate; strict accept)\"\ntags: \"circle packing, non-local, second-order, cycle surgery, linear algebra, zero slack\"\nabstract: \"Implements a deterministic Cycle Surgery pilot: detect short cycles (length 4–7) in the near-contact graph, solve a tiny equality-constrained ridge LS (KKT) to preserve internal tangencies\
    \ while pushing outward toward external neighbors, clip by trust region, and gate with exact zero‑slack radii LP + iterative zero‑tolerance finalize. Accept only if Δ∑r ≥ 1e‑9; otherwise return baseline unchanged. Full per-attempt logs printed.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  import os\n  from math import sqrt\n  try:\n      from scipy.optimize import linprog\n  except Exception:\n      linprog = None\n\n  N = 32\n  R_MIN = 1e-8\n\n  # Thresholds and grids (deterministic)\n  KAPPA_LARGE = 3e-3   # external push neighbor set\n  KAPPA_MID   = 1e-3   # internal equality set and cycle edges\n  TAU_GRID    = [0.03, 0.02, 0.01]        # trust-region infty-norm\n  GAMMA_GRID  = [1.5e-3, 1.0e-3, 6e-4, 3e-4, 1e-4]\n  RIDGE_ALPHA = 1e-6\n  KKT_JITTER  = 1e-12\n  EPS_EQ_RANK = 1e-12\n  MAX_CYCLES  = 20\n  ATTEMPT_CAP = 26\n  ACCEPT_EPS  = 1e-9\n\n  def _pair_indices(n=N):\n      return [(i, j) for i in range(n) for j in range(i + 1, n)]\n  PAIRS = _pair_indices(N)\n\n  # ---------- Radii\
    \ LP (zero-slack) + finalize ----------\n  def _solve_radii_lp_zero_slack(C):\n      n = C.shape[0]\n      x = C[:, 0]; y = C[:, 1]\n      rows = []; rhs = []\n      # Boundary caps\n      for i in range(n):\n          a = np.zeros(n); a[i] = 1.0; rows.append(a); rhs.append(max(0.0, float(x[i])))\n      for i in range(n):\n          a = np.zeros(n); a[i] = 1.0; rows.append(a); rhs.append(max(0.0, float(1.0 - x[i])))\n      for i in range(n):\n          a = np.zeros(n); a[i] = 1.0; rows.append(a); rhs.append(max(0.0, float(y[i])))\n      for i in range(n):\n          a = np.zeros(n); a[i] = 1.0; rows.append(a); rhs.append(max(0.0, float(1.0 - y[i])))\n      # Pair caps\n      for (i, j) in PAIRS:\n          a = np.zeros(n); a[i] = 1.0; a[j] = 1.0\n          dij = float(np.linalg.norm(C[i] - C[j]))\n          rows.append(a); rhs.append(max(0.0, dij))\n      A_ub = np.vstack(rows); b_ub = np.array(rhs)\n      c = -np.ones(n); bounds = [(R_MIN, None) for _ in range(n)]\n      if linprog\
    \ is None:\n          # Fallback: boundary-limited greedy repair\n          r = np.maximum(R_MIN, np.minimum.reduce([x, 1.0 - x, y, 1.0 - y]))\n          for (i, j) in PAIRS:\n              dij = float(np.linalg.norm(C[i] - C[j]))\n              s = r[i] + r[j]\n              if s > dij:\n                  need = s - dij\n                  take = min(need * 0.5, max(0.0, r[i] - R_MIN)); r[i] -= take; need -= take\n                  take = min(need, max(0.0, r[j] - R_MIN)); r[j] -= take\n          return np.maximum(R_MIN, r), False\n      res = linprog(c, A_ub=A_ub, b_ub=b_ub, bounds=bounds, method=\"highs\")\n      if not res.success:\n          r = np.maximum(R_MIN, np.minimum.reduce([x, 1.0 - x, y, 1.0 - y]))\n          return r, False\n      return np.maximum(R_MIN, np.array(res.x, dtype=float)), True\n\n  def _finalize_iterative(C, r, iters=4):\n      r = np.array(r, dtype=float)\n      n = C.shape[0]\n      for _ in range(iters):\n          # boundaries\n          x = C[:, 0]; y\
    \ = C[:, 1]\n          r = np.minimum(r, x); r = np.minimum(r, 1.0 - x)\n          r = np.minimum(r, y); r = np.minimum(r, 1.0 - y)\n          r = np.maximum(R_MIN, r)\n          # pairs\n          changed = False\n          for (i, j) in PAIRS:\n              dij = float(np.linalg.norm(C[i] - C[j]))\n              s = r[i] + r[j]\n              if s > dij:\n                  need = s - dij\n                  di = min(0.5 * need, max(0.0, r[i] - R_MIN))\n                  dj = min(need - di, max(0.0, r[j] - R_MIN))\n                  r[i] -= di; r[j] -= dj\n                  changed = True\n          if not changed:\n              break\n      return np.maximum(R_MIN, r)\n\n  def _sum_r(C):\n      r, _ = _solve_radii_lp_zero_slack(C)\n      r = _finalize_iterative(C, r)\n      return r, float(np.sum(r))\n\n  # ---------- Storage seeds ----------\n  def _load_best():\n      try:\n          C = np.load(\"storage/verity/best_centers.npy\").astype(float)\n          r = np.load(\"storage/verity/best_radii.npy\"\
    ).astype(float)\n          if C.shape == (N, 2) and r.shape == (N,):\n              return C, r\n      except Exception:\n          pass\n      # Fallback grid 6x6 minus corners\n      xs = np.linspace(0.1, 0.9, 6); ys = np.linspace(0.1, 0.9, 6)\n      centers = []\n      for ix, x in enumerate(xs):\n          for iy, y in enumerate(ys):\n              if (ix in (0, 5)) and (iy in (0, 5)): continue\n              centers.append([x, y])\n      C = np.array(centers, dtype=float)\n      r, _ = _sum_r(C)\n      return C, r\n\n  def _jitter(C, amp=8e-4, seed=2027):\n      rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\n      J = rng.uniform(-amp, amp, size=C.shape)\n      return np.clip(C + J, 0.0, 1.0)\n\n  # ---------- Near-contact graph and slacks ----------\n  def _pair_slacks(C, r):\n      n = C.shape[0]; ps = {}\n      for (i, j) in PAIRS:\n          dij = float(np.linalg.norm(C[i] - C[j]))\n          ps[(i, j)] = dij - (r[i] + r[j])\n      return ps\n\n  def _adjacency(C, r, kappa):\n      ps =\
    \ _pair_slacks(C, r)\n      adj = [[] for _ in range(N)]\n      for (i, j), s in ps.items():\n          if s <= kappa:\n              adj[i].append(j); adj[j].append(i)\n      return adj, ps\n\n  # ---------- Cycle enumeration (simple DFS, length 4..7) ----------\n  def _canonical_cycle(cyc):\n      # cyc is list of node ids\n      m = len(cyc)\n      best = None\n      for s in range(m):\n          rot = cyc[s:] + cyc[:s]\n          rrot = list(reversed(rot))\n          cand = tuple(rot) if tuple(rot) <= tuple(rrot) else tuple(rrot)\n          if best is None or cand < best:\n              best = cand\n      return best\n\n  def _enumerate_cycles(adj, max_len=7, min_len=4, cap=20):\n      cycles = set()\n      n = len(adj)\n      def dfs(start, u, path, visited, depth):\n          if depth > max_len: return\n          for v in adj[u]:\n              if v == start and depth >= min_len:\n                  cyc = path[:]  # path already includes start\n                  cyc_t = _canonical_cycle(cyc)\n\
    \                  cycles.add(cyc_t)\n                  if len(cycles) >= cap: return\n              elif v > start and v not in visited:\n                  visited.add(v); path.append(v)\n                  dfs(start, v, path, visited, depth + 1)\n                  path.pop(); visited.remove(v)\n      for s in range(n):\n          # only start DFS if at least two neighbors (avoid trivial)\n          neigh = [v for v in adj[s] if v > s]\n          if len(neigh) < 2: continue\n          visited = set([s])\n          for v in neigh:\n              visited.add(v)\n              dfs(s, v, [s, v], visited, 2)\n              visited.remove(v)\n          if len(cycles) >= cap: break\n      # sort cycles deterministically\n      return [list(c) for c in sorted(list(cycles))][:cap]\n\n  # ---------- Outward normals and matrices ----------\n  def _unit(v):\n      nrm = float(np.linalg.norm(v))\n      if nrm < 1e-15: return np.array([1.0, 0.0])\n      return v / nrm\n\n  def _boundary_normals(C,\
    \ r, i):\n      x, y = C[i, 0], C[i, 1]\n      out = np.zeros(2)\n      if x - r[i] <= KAPPA_LARGE: out += np.array([+1.0, 0.0])\n      if 1.0 - x - r[i] <= KAPPA_LARGE: out += np.array([-1.0, 0.0])\n      if y - r[i] <= KAPPA_LARGE: out += np.array([0.0, +1.0])\n      if 1.0 - y - r[i] <= KAPPA_LARGE: out += np.array([0.0, -1.0])\n      return out\n\n  def _n_out_for_cycle_nodes(C, r, cycle, ps):\n      cbar = np.mean(C[cycle, :], axis=0)\n      n_out = []\n      for i in cycle:\n          acc = np.zeros(2)\n          # neighbors outside the cycle within KAPPA_LARGE\n          for j in range(N):\n              if i == j or j in cycle: continue\n              ij = (i, j) if i < j else (j, i)\n              s = ps.get(ij, 1e9)\n              if s <= KAPPA_LARGE:\n                  acc += _unit(C[i] - C[j])\n          acc += _boundary_normals(C, r, i)\n          if np.linalg.norm(acc) < 1e-12:\n              acc = C[i] - cbar\n          n_out.append(_unit(acc))\n      return np.array(n_out),\
    \ cbar\n\n  def _build_mats_cycle(C, r, cycle, ps, n_out, cbar):\n      # Variables v = [theta, t_x, t_y, alpha_1..alpha_m]\n      m = len(cycle)\n      nv = 3 + m\n      Rperp = np.array([[0.0, -1.0], [1.0,  0.0]])\n      # Internal equalities on cycle edges (consecutive + wrap), if slack <= KAPPA_MID\n      E_rows = []\n      for idx in range(m):\n          i = cycle[idx]; j = cycle[(idx + 1) % m]\n          ij = (i, j) if i < j else (j, i)\n          if ps.get(ij, 1e9) > KAPPA_MID:\n              continue\n          n_ij = _unit(C[i] - C[j])\n          row = np.zeros(nv)\n          # theta term: n_ij · Rperp (c_i - c_j)\n          row[0] = np.dot(n_ij, Rperp.dot(C[i] - C[j]))\n          # t_x, t_y cancel\n          # alpha terms\n          i_pos = 3 + idx\n          j_pos = 3 + ((idx + 1) % m)\n          row[i_pos] = np.dot(n_ij, n_out[idx])\n          row[j_pos] = -np.dot(n_ij, n_out[(idx + 1) % m])\n          E_rows.append(row)\n      E = np.vstack(E_rows) if E_rows else np.zeros((0,\
    \ nv))\n\n      # External pushes: neighbors outside cycle with slack <= KAPPA_LARGE\n      A_rows = []\n      b_vals = []\n      for idx, i in enumerate(cycle):\n          # Build list of external neighbors within threshold\n          for j in range(N):\n              if j in cycle or i == j: continue\n              ij = (i, j) if i < j else (j, i)\n              if ps.get(ij, 1e9) <= KAPPA_LARGE:\n                  n_iu = _unit(C[i] - C[j])\n                  row = np.zeros(nv)\n                  # theta term\n                  row[0] = np.dot(n_iu, Rperp.dot(C[i] - cbar))\n                  # translations\n                  row[1] = n_iu[0]; row[2] = n_iu[1]\n                  # alpha for node i only\n                  row[3 + idx] = np.dot(n_iu, n_out[idx])\n                  A_rows.append(row)\n                  b_vals.append(1.0)  # placeholder, scaled by gamma later\n      A = np.vstack(A_rows) if A_rows else np.zeros((0, nv))\n      b = np.array(b_vals, dtype=float)\n      return\
    \ A, b, E\n\n  def _kkt_solve(A, b_scaled, E, alpha=RIDGE_ALPHA, jitter=KKT_JITTER):\n      nv = A.shape[1]\n      H = A.T.dot(A) + alpha * np.eye(nv)\n      rhs_top = A.T.dot(b_scaled)\n      if E.shape[0] == 0:\n          # Unconstrained ridge LS\n          try:\n              v = np.linalg.solve(H, rhs_top)\n              return v\n          except np.linalg.LinAlgError:\n              # add jitter\n              v = np.linalg.lstsq(H + jitter*np.eye(nv), rhs_top, rcond=None)[0]\n              return v\n      # KKT system\n      p = E.shape[0]\n      K = np.zeros((nv + p, nv + p))\n      K[:nv, :nv] = H\n      K[:nv, nv:] = E.T\n      K[nv:, :nv] = E\n      rhs = np.zeros(nv + p)\n      rhs[:nv] = rhs_top\n      try:\n          sol = np.linalg.solve(K, rhs)\n          v = sol[:nv]\n          return v\n      except np.linalg.LinAlgError:\n          # retry with jitter\n          K[:nv, :nv] = H + jitter * np.eye(nv)\n          try:\n              sol = np.linalg.solve(K, rhs)\n   \
    \           return sol[:nv]\n          except np.linalg.LinAlgError:\n              return None\n\n  def _apply_delta_cycle(C, cycle, v, n_out, cbar, tau):\n      # v = [theta, t_x, t_y, alpha_1..alpha_m]\n      theta = float(v[0]); tx = float(v[1]); ty = float(v[2])\n      m = len(cycle)\n      Rperp = np.array([[0.0, -1.0], [1.0,  0.0]])\n      C_new = C.copy()\n      deltas = []\n      for idx, i in enumerate(cycle):\n          base = theta * Rperp.dot(C[i] - cbar) + np.array([tx, ty])\n          alpha = float(v[3 + idx])\n          dci = base + alpha * n_out[idx]\n          deltas.append(dci)\n      deltas = np.array(deltas)\n      # Trust-region clip by infinity norm\n      max_inf = float(np.max(np.abs(deltas))) if deltas.size else 0.0\n      scale = 1.0 if max_inf <= tau or max_inf == 0.0 else (tau / max_inf)\n      for idx, i in enumerate(cycle):\n          C_new[i] = np.clip(C[i] + scale * deltas[idx], 0.0, 1.0)\n      return C_new, scale\n\n  # ---------- Main driver ----------\n\
    \  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      # Seeds: storage-best, then tiny jitter\n      C0, r0 = _load_best()\n      seeds = [(\"storage-best\", C0)]\n      Cj = _jitter(C0, amp=8e-4, seed=2027)\n      seeds.append((\"storage-best-jitter\", Cj))\n\n      # Ensure baseline best (recompute radii for seed to be safe)\n      best_C = None; best_r = None; best_sum = -1.0\n      for name, Cseed in seeds:\n          r_seed, sum_seed = _sum_r(Cseed)\n          if sum_seed > best_sum:\n              best_C, best_r, best_sum = Cseed.copy(), r_seed.copy(), sum_seed\n\n      attempts = 0\n      improved = False\n\n      for seed_name, Cseed in seeds:\n          r_seed, sum_seed = _sum_r(Cseed)\n          if attempts >= ATTEMPT_CAP: break\n          # Build near-contact graph on seed\n          adj, ps = _adjacency(Cseed, r_seed, KAPPA_MID)\n          cycles = _enumerate_cycles(adj, max_len=7, min_len=4, cap=MAX_CYCLES)\n          # Rank cycles by sum of inverse slack along cycle edges\n     \
    \     def cyc_weight(cyc):\n              s = 0.0\n              m = len(cyc)\n              for t in range(m):\n                  i = cyc[t]; j = cyc[(t + 1) % m]\n                  ij = (i, j) if i < j else (j, i)\n                  val = ps.get(ij, 1e9)\n                  s += 1.0 / (max(val, 1e-12))\n              return s\n          cycles = sorted(cycles, key=cyc_weight, reverse=True)[:MAX_CYCLES]\n\n          for tau in TAU_GRID:\n              for cyc in cycles:\n                  if attempts >= ATTEMPT_CAP: break\n                  # Build normals and matrices for this cycle\n                  n_out, cbar = _n_out_for_cycle_nodes(Cseed, r_seed, cyc, ps)\n                  A, b_base, E = _build_mats_cycle(Cseed, r_seed, cyc, ps, n_out, cbar)\n                  if A.shape[0] == 0:\n                      continue  # nothing to push outward\n                  # Row-normalize A for conditioning\n                  row_norms = np.linalg.norm(A, axis=1) + 1e-15\n                  A_norm\
    \ = A / row_norms[:, None]\n                  for gamma in GAMMA_GRID:\n                      attempts += 1\n                      b_scaled = gamma * np.ones(A.shape[0], dtype=float) / row_norms\n                      v = _kkt_solve(A_norm, b_scaled, E, alpha=RIDGE_ALPHA, jitter=KKT_JITTER)\n                      if v is None:\n                          print(f\"[cycle] seed={seed_name} tau={tau:.3f} cyc_len={len(cyc)} gamma={gamma:.4e} -> KKT fail\")\n                          if attempts >= ATTEMPT_CAP: break\n                          continue\n                      C_try, scale = _apply_delta_cycle(Cseed, cyc, v, n_out, cbar, tau)\n                      r_try, _ok = _solve_radii_lp_zero_slack(C_try)\n                      r_try = _finalize_iterative(C_try, r_try)\n                      sum_try = float(np.sum(r_try))\n                      dsum = sum_try - best_sum\n                      print(f\"[cycle] seed={seed_name} tau={tau:.3f} cyc_len={len(cyc)} A={A.shape[0]} E={E.shape[0]}\
    \ gamma={gamma:.4e} scale={scale:.3f} dsum={dsum:.12e}\")\n                      if dsum > ACCEPT_EPS:\n                          best_C, best_r, best_sum = C_try, r_try, sum_try\n                          improved = True\n                          print(f\"[accept] new_sum={best_sum:.18f} after cycle surgery; persisting and returning.\")\n                          try:\n                              os.makedirs(\"storage/verity\", exist_ok=True)\n                              np.save(\"storage/verity/best_centers.npy\", best_C)\n                              np.save(\"storage/verity/best_radii.npy\", best_r)\n                          except Exception:\n                              pass\n                          return np.array(best_C, dtype=float), np.array(best_r, dtype=float)\n                  if attempts >= ATTEMPT_CAP: break\n              if attempts >= ATTEMPT_CAP: break\n\n      # No improvement found; return best as baseline\n      if not improved:\n          print(f\"[return]\
    \ No accepted cycle surgery move. Returning baseline sum={best_sum:.18f}.\")\n      try:\n          os.makedirs(\"storage/verity\", exist_ok=True)\n          np.save(\"storage/verity/best_centers.npy\", best_C)\n          np.save(\"storage/verity/best_radii.npy\", best_r)\n      except Exception:\n          pass\n      return np.array(best_C, dtype=float), np.array(best_r, dtype=float)\n```"
thinking_content: "**Implementing cycle enumeration**\n\nI’m thinking about the existing code modules for exact radii and finalizing tasks, which we can replicate quickly. My plan involves loading the best centers and radii from storage, or reverting to a grid if needed. \n\nNext, I’ll build a near-contact graph and compute pair slacks. While enumerating cycles of lengths 4 to 7 might be heavy, I’m considering limiting it to simpler heuristics. Maybe even using top cycles from k-shortest paths could help, but creating robust enumeration code all at once is quite a challenge.**Planning cycle enumeration**\n\nI see that we only need to submit one run since acceptance is unlikely if the improvement is less than 1e-9. If there's no improvement, we’ll just return the baseline unchanged. I’m thinking we'll implement the cycle surgery attempt, but limit enumeration to M cycles using a straightforward algorithm.\n\nFirst, I’ll build an adjacency list for near contacts, setting a threshold of κ_mid,\
  \ like 1e-3. For cycles, I might want to use Johnson's algorithm for lengths up to 6 or 7, but I wonder if that might be too heavy for quick implementation.**Implementing BFS cycle detection**\n\nI’m planning to use BFS for cycle detection, focusing only on cycles of length 4 to 7. I’ll start from node id s and search only through neighbors with ids greater than s to avoid duplicates. I’ll look for paths up to depth L_max-1 and record cycles when reaching a neighbor that closes a cycle, ensuring the path length is at least 4. \n\nTo avoid repeating nodes, I’ll sort the rotated tuples to start at the smallest node and pick the lexicographically smallest direction. After that, I’ll compute outward normals for the cycle nodes, considering neighbors outside S with s_iu ≤ κ_large (3e-3).**Refining boundary normals**\n\nIn our earlier design, I calculated n_iu as the unit vector from c_i to c_u, increasing the distance from u. Now, I need to sum these vectors. We’ll also account for boundary\
  \ normals when near a boundary, like when left slack s_iL is less than or equal to κ_large. The outward normal for the left boundary will be along +e_x, and this is based on the boundary inequality relating r_i and x_i. \n\nI’ll normalize the summed normals to get n_out and fallback to a radial direction from the centroid if needed. For motion, I’ll use the formula Δc_i = θ R_perp (c_i - c̄) + t + α_i n_out_i and set the relevant parameter grids accordingly. I’ll ensure we have equality constraints for internal edges, requiring that n_ij·(Δc_i - Δc_j) equals 0 for edges close to contact.**Implementing linear constraints and solving**\n\nI'm planning to express Δc linearly in terms of variables v. We’ll build the equation E v = 0 and use external push constraints through least squares for neighbors with s_iu less than or equal to κ_large.\n\nTo implement the nullspace method, I need to compute the QR decomposition of E^T and establish an orthonormal basis. I'll rely on SVD for smaller dimensions.\
  \ If the nullspace dimension is zero, I'll consider dropping equality rows based on slack or other criteria. \n\nFor the KKT system, I’ll construct the matrix K and solve for x using numpy.linalg.solve, adding jitter to avoid singularities. We’ll compute Δ only for cycle nodes. Next, I'll apply clipping to ensure the new candidate centers and will implement zero-tolerance iterative finalize using code from earlier evaluations. \n\nFinally, I'll log the attempts, set thresholds for cycles, and ensure we’re correctly managing radii constraints. I’ll loop through iterations to build adjacency and compute pair slacks for cycle enumeration.**Recording cycles and solving constraints**\n\nI'm planning to record cycles as tuples of node indices and deduplicate them efficiently since N=32 is manageable. For cycle edge detection, I will only include edges along the cycle path, filtering by the slack value κ_mid to build matrices A and E.\n\nI'll lay out the variable ordering, where theta, t_x, and\
  \ t_y come first, followed by alpha_i values for each cycle node. Next, I’ll compute the linear mapping for Δc.\n\nFor internal constraints, I’ll focus on the relationship n_ij dot (Δc_i - Δc_j) = 0. The contributions from theta and translation variables cancel out, simplifying the calculations. For external neighbors, I’ll ensure the target mapping ensures that n_iu dot Δc_i meets the required threshold.\n\nAs I assemble the KKT matrix, I’ll consider edge cases, especially when there are no external edges. I still plan to include theta as a variable to be solved within the least squares framework, rather than treating it as a fixed variable from the grid. This adjustment will keep the process flexible while maintaining the overall design's integrity.**Setting up loops and constraints**\n\nI'm defining attempt loops to make up to M cycles. I’ll implement a function for zero-slack radii using SciPy's linprog to manage constraints efficiently. We'll maximize the sum of radii while ensuring\
  \ non-overlap with a small margin to avoid numerical issues.\n\nNext, I’ll package everything for submission, detailing identity and actions. This includes the implementation of various functions to support the tasks, ensuring high-quality code while keeping track of matrix shapes.\n\nAs I begin coding, I'll pay close attention to coefficients and the construction of the KKT system to ensure valid outputs. Edge cases, like skipping cycles without external neighbors, will be managed carefully to ensure robustness.**Implementing cycle enumeration**\n\nI'm setting up a simple DFS to enumerate cycles with constraints on minimum and maximum length. For every node, I’ll track its neighbors and ensure no duplicate paths. I need to deduplicate cycles into a canonical form to account for rotation and direction.\n\nI'll compute external neighbors and their norms against a threshold, defining internal equalities based on cycle edges. Each attempt will log pertinent details, and I’ll implement jitter\
  \ adjustments. Finally, I’ll build the solution's KKT condition and prepare a YAML output, ensuring proper quoting for special characters."
