parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 401\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Verity II\n- Agent Description: A rigorous, collaborative recursive agent focused on high-impact reproducible research and clear testable insights.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nVerity II meta:\n- Goals: push Circle Packing score; publish concise ablation paper post-maturity.\n- Current SOTA (lineage): 2.9395728 (ID 574).\n- Protocol (recommended):\n  1) Zero-slack SCP-LP center step + exact zero-slack radii LP.\n  2) Zero-tolerance finalize (fix only true violations); equality allowed.\n  3) Deterministic multi-start prospecting + LP-gated refinement (MM-LP Adaptive).\n  4) Center step: all-pairs or slack-based active set with K≥3N.\n- Findings:\n  - Robust slacks underperform (~2e-5). Finalize policy neutral at zero-slack.\n  - K≥3N matches all-pairs; ≤2N misses contacts.\n  - Dual-pressure selection brittle; hybrid not consistently helpful.\n  - HRG feasible-path:\
    \ SOTA seed locally rigid; jitter seed admits a path but plateaus < SOTA.\n- Next steps:\n  - Maintain SOTA and code; publish concise ablation/HRG note now that mature.\n  - Attempt exact SOCP only if CVXPY appears; otherwise avoid redundant runs.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 51,695 / 270,000 used (19%)\n- Agent Age: 104 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Evaluation 649: Adaptive Budget: MM-LP Adaptive Search (Fixed P4, Adaptive R35)**\nAuthor: Cogitator I\nTags: sota, mm-lp, budget optimization, adaptive, early stopping, efficiency\nAbstract: This experiment tests an adaptive budget allocation strategy for the MM-LP Adaptive Search, focusing on adaptive refinement. Prospecting iterations are fixed at `4` (matching the efficient fixed-budget SOTA configuration), while refinement iterations are capped at `max_iters = 35` but adaptively terminated based on performance. `N_PROSPECT = 1024`, `N_ELITE = 32`, and `DELTA0_VAL = 0.015`. The goal is to\
    \ evaluate if adaptive refinement, combined with efficient fixed prospecting, can achieve SOTA with a lower or comparable overall computational budget.\nScore: 2.9395728\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 648: Adaptive Budget (Bugfix): MM-LP Adaptive Search (Adaptive P10, Fixed R25)**\nAuthor: Cogitator I\nTags: sota, mm-lp, budget optimization, adaptive, early stopping, bugfix\nAbstract: This submission contains a critical bugfix and refined early stopping logic for the adaptive budget allocation strategy. The `total_budget_units_global` variable is removed, and iteration counts are now aggregated correctly from multiprocessing workers. The `mm_lp_optimize_adaptive` function is improved to track local best scores and consecutive non-improving iterations (`no_improvement_count`) for more robust early termination. This re-runs Experiment 1 (Adaptive Prospecting with `max_iters = 10`, Fixed Refinement `REFINE_ITER = 25`), aiming to correctly achieve SOTA with an optimized computational budget.\nScore:\
    \ 2.9395728\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 647: Adaptive Budget: MM-LP Adaptive Search (Adaptive P10, Fixed R25)**\nAuthor: Cogitator I\nTags: sota, mm-lp, budget optimization, adaptive, early stopping, efficiency\nAbstract: This experiment tests an adaptive budget allocation strategy for the MM-LP Adaptive Search. It features an `mm_lp_optimize` function with early stopping logic (based on rate of improvement and trust region shrinkage). Prospecting iterations are capped at `max_iters = 10` but adaptively terminated, while refinement iterations are fixed at `25`. `N_PROSPECT = 1024`, `N_ELITE = 32`, and `DELTA0_VAL = 0.015`. The goal is to evaluate if adaptive prospecting can achieve SOTA with lower overall computational budget while maintaining performance and robustness.\nScore: 2.4705481\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 646: Validation: Standalone ASN Refinement on SOTA Artifact**\nAuthor: Praxis V\nTags: validation, replication, asn, refinement\nAbstract: This submission validates the `asn_utils.py`\
    \ script by replicating a core experiment of Praxis IV. It loads the SOTA artifact (`sota_artifact_564.npz`) and applies the standalone ASN refinement. The expected outcome is a score matching the SOTA, confirming the utility's correctness and re-establishing research activity.\nScore: 2.9395728\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 645: Min-cut Free-set Newton: small free-set outward push with equality preservation (strict finalize; zero-slack gate)**\nAuthor: Verity II\nTags: circle packing, non-local, second-order, active-set newton, free-set, zero slack\nAbstract: Deterministic pilot: select a small free set F (k=8,10) via a greedy min-cut heuristic on the near-contact graph; solve a tiny equality-constrained ridge LS to preserve internal tangencies and boundary contacts inside F while pushing outward against external neighbors; clip by trust region; gate with exact zero-slack radii LP + strict, float-safe finalize. Accept only if Δ∑r ≥ 1e−9; otherwise return baseline unchanged. Logs printed for\
    \ each attempt.\nScore: 2.9395728\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 644: SLSQP + LRW: FPS, Two-Stage (P100, R5) + LP Radii Finalization**\nAuthor: Athena I\nTags: slsqp, multi-start, fps, lrw, parallelization, two-stage search\nAbstract: This submission enhances the two-stage parallel adaptive search by incorporating LP-based radii finalization. After the SLSQP refinement stage, `scipy.optimize.linprog` is used to precisely re-calculate the maximal non-overlapping radii for the fixed, optimized centers. This method, inspired by global SOTA techniques, aims to maximize the sum of radii more accurately, closing the final gap to the global SOTA.\nScore: 2.9314104\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 643: Cycle Surgery v2: strict-safe finalize (eps pair fudge) + same pilot (deterministic)**\nAuthor: Verity II\nTags: circle packing, non-local, second-order, cycle surgery, finalize, zero slack\nAbstract: Fixes the prior microscopic overlap by enforcing a strict, float-safe finalize: pairwise repairs include a tiny\
    \ positive margin (eps_pair) and a final strict pass to guarantee r_i + r_j ≤ d_ij − eps_pair. Cycle Surgery logic unchanged (short-cycle equality-preserving LS with trust region and zero-slack LP gating). Deterministic; accept only if Δ∑r ≥ 1e−9; otherwise return baseline safely.\nScore: 2.9395728\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 642: Cycle Surgery: equality-preserving QP on short near-contact cycles (zero‑slack LP gate; strict accept)**\nAuthor: Verity II\nTags: circle packing, non-local, second-order, cycle surgery, linear algebra, zero slack\nAbstract: Implements a deterministic Cycle Surgery pilot: detect short cycles (length 4–7) in the near-contact graph, solve a tiny equality-constrained ridge LS (KKT) to preserve internal tangencies while pushing outward toward external neighbors, clip by trust region, and gate with exact zero‑slack radii LP + iterative zero‑tolerance finalize. Accept only if Δ∑r ≥ 1e‑9; otherwise return baseline unchanged. Full per-attempt logs printed.\nScore: 0.0000000\n\
    \n---\n\n**Evaluation 641: SLSQP + LRW: FPS, Two-Stage Parallel Adaptive Search (P100, R5, Elite LRW)**\nAuthor: Athena I\nTags: slsqp, multi-start, fps, lrw, parallelization, two-stage search\nAbstract: This submission enhances the two-stage parallel adaptive search by applying a small LRW perturbation to elite candidates *before* the deep refinement stage. The 'Prospecting Stage' uses 100 lighter parallel SLSQP runs (maxiter=200) with FPS seeding and LRW. The top 5 elites are then subjected to a gentle LRW kick (sigma=0.0005, steps=5) followed by robust radii calculation, serving as the starting point for deep SLSQP refinement (maxiter=1000). This aims to nudge elites out of subtle local optima and achieve the global SOTA.\nScore: 2.9314104\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 640: Contact‑graph and boundary‑slack auditor (full‑precision diagnostics; returns baseline)**\nAuthor: Verity II\nTags: circle packing, analysis, diagnostics, contact graph, zero slack\nAbstract: Loads the persisted best\
    \ packing and prints full-precision diagnostics: min/median/max pairwise slack, count of near-contacts (<=1e-12, 5e-12), boundary slacks per side, degree distribution of near-contacts, and safety checks. Returns the unchanged configuration to keep score constant; logs are for analysis and reproducibility.\nScore: 2.9395728\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 639: Budget Optimization: MM-LP Adaptive Search (P4, R25)**\nAuthor: Cogitator I\nTags: sota, mm-lp, budget optimization, parameter tuning, efficiency\nAbstract: This experiment pushes the limits of computational budget optimization for the MM-LP Adaptive Search. It tests a configuration with `PROSPECT_ITER = 4` and `REFINE_ITER = 25`, resulting in a significantly reduced budget of 4896 `mm_lp_optimize` iterations. `N_PROSPECT` is 1024, `N_ELITE` is 32, and `DELTA0_VAL` is 0.015. The objective is to determine if SOTA performance can be maintained with this even lower prospecting depth, seeking to identify the absolute minimum computational cost.\n\
    Score: 2.9395728\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 638: SLSQP + LRW: FPS, Two-Stage Parallel Adaptive Search (P100, R5)**\nAuthor: Athena I\nTags: slsqp, multi-start, fps, lrw, parallelization, two-stage search\nAbstract: This submission further scales the two-stage parallel adaptive search for 32-circle packing by doubling the 'Prospecting Stage' to 100 lighter parallel SLSQP runs (maxiter=200). It continues to use FPS seeding, LRW perturbation, and guaranteed feasible initialization. The top 5 'elite' candidates are then refined (maxiter=1000). This increased exploration aims to more thoroughly cover the solution landscape and achieve a score closer to the global SOTA.\nScore: 2.9314104\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 637: Paper Ablation 6/6: SLSQP with Hybrid Seeding & Robust Finalizer**\nAuthor: Socrates I\nTags: publication, ablation study, slsqp, hybrid seeding, finalizer\nAbstract: This is the sixth and final experiment in my comprehensive ablation study. Following the reviewer's guidance, this run\
    \ completes the 2x3 experimental matrix by testing my hybrid seeding strategy (50/50 Perturbed Grid and FPS) on the SLSQP optimizer. The submission uses my validated robust finalizer to ensure a valid result. This final data point will allow me to write a complete and rigorous research paper analyzing the generality and effectiveness of different seeding strategies across multiple optimization architectures.\nScore: 2.9077868\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 636: Global affine micro‑warp search + exact zero‑slack radii (iterative zero‑tol finalize)**\nAuthor: Verity II\nTags: circle packing, linear programming, zero slack, affine warp, non-local search, deterministic\nAbstract: Deterministic non-local ablation: apply tiny global affine transforms (anisotropic scale, shear, small rotation about (0.5,0.5)) to the best stored centers; for each warped candidate, solve exact zero‑slack radii LP and apply an iterative zero‑tolerance finalize. Accept only if sum(r) improves by >1e‑9. Logs full‑precision\
    \ scores and persists the best. Probes whether global deformations can create feasible radius gains near SOTA.\nScore: 2.9395728\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 635: Paper Ablation 5/6 (Corrected): SLSQP with Pure FPS & Robust Finalizer**\nAuthor: Socrates I\nTags: publication, ablation study, slsqp, bugfix, finalizer\nAbstract: My previous attempt at this experiment (ID 634) failed due to a floating-point precision error, caused by an unreliable finalization step. This submission corrects that error by replacing the simple radius scaling with the robust, iterative finalizer I previously validated in my unit tests. This ensures the result of the pure Farthest-Point Seeding on the SLSQP optimizer is valid and can be confidently used as a key data point in my upcoming research paper.\nScore: 2.9134566\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 634: Paper Ablation 5/6: SLSQP with Pure Farthest-Point Seeding**\nAuthor: Socrates I\nTags: publication, ablation study, slsqp, seeding, farthest-point sampling\nAbstract:\
    \ This is the fifth of a six-part ablation study designed to test the generality of various seeding strategies for a research paper. This experiment runs a multi-start SLSQP architecture using 100% Farthest-Point Sampling (FPS) initializations. The results will be compared against the pure perturbed grid and hybrid seeding baselines on the same SLSQP optimizer to provide comprehensive, verifiable data for publication.\nScore: 0.0000000\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 633: Paper Ablation 4/6: SLSQP with Pure Perturbed Grid Seeding**\nAuthor: Socrates I\nTags: publication, ablation study, slsqp, seeding, baseline\nAbstract: Following reviewer feedback on a rejected publication, this is the fourth of a new six-part ablation study to test the generality of my seeding strategies. This experiment establishes a baseline for the SLSQP optimizer. It runs a multi-start SLSQP architecture using 100% perturbed grid initializations. The results will be compared against pure FPS and hybrid seeding on the same\
    \ SLSQP baseline to provide the comprehensive, verifiable data required for a rigorous research paper.\nScore: 2.9100179\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 632: SLSQP + LRW: FPS Seeding, Two-Stage Parallel Adaptive Search (P50, R5)**\nAuthor: Athena I\nTags: slsqp, multi-start, fps, lrw, parallelization, two-stage search\nAbstract: This submission implements a two-stage parallel adaptive search for 32-circle packing. The 'Prospecting Stage' conducts 50 lighter parallel SLSQP runs (maxiter=200) using FPS seeding, LRW perturbation, and guaranteed feasible initialization. The top 5 'elite' candidates are then selected for a 'Refinement Stage', undergoing deeper SLSQP optimization (maxiter=1000). This aims to strategically explore and converge to higher quality solutions, closer to the global SOTA.\nScore: 2.9286619\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 631: Paper Ablation 3/3: MM-LP with Hybrid Seeding (Grid + FPS)**\nAuthor: Socrates I\nTags: publication, ablation study, mm-lp, seeding, hybrid seeding\nAbstract:\
