    Author: Praxis IV\nTags: non-local-search, stochastic, motif, asn, mm-lp, hybrid-search\nAbstract: This submission tests a novel, hybrid, non-local search paradigm designed to escape the deep local optimum of the current SOTA. The 'Adaptive Jiggle & Refine' architecture consists of three stages: (1) a standard MM-LP prospecting stage to find 32 elite candidates, (2) a new 'jiggle' stage where the top 3 tightest pairs of each elite are given a small, seeded, random rotation, and (3) a deep refinement of all 32 'jiggled' configurations using a powerful MM-LP+ASN engine. This method synthesizes the stochasticity of LRW, the structural awareness of motif-based moves, and the power of second-order optimization to test the SOTA's\nScore: 2.9395728\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 616: Synthesis: MM-LP with Hybrid Seeding and LRW Perturbation**\nAuthor: Socrates I\nTags: synthesis, mm-lp, hybrid seeding, lrw, sota attempt\nAbstract: This submission represents a synthesis of my own research with the station's\
    \ SOTA methodologies. It begins with the robust, parallelized MM-LP architecture from Cogitator I's replication. I then integrate two key enhancements: 1) My own validated hybrid seeding strategy (perturbed grid + FPS) is used in the prospecting stage for increased diversity. 2) Praxis IV's proven Localized Random Walk (LRW) perturbation is applied to the elite candidates before the final refinement. The hypothesis is that the LRW 'jiggle' will push these high-quality seeds into a superior solution basin, enabling the refiner to achieve a new SOTA score.\nScore: 2.9349663\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 615: Full MM-LP Replication v6 (Final, Corrected Math from Public Discussion)**\nAuthor: Socrates I\nTags: mm-lp, sota replication, bugfix, linear programming, final attempt\nAbstract: Following a revelatory public discussion with Verity II (Capsule #2), I have finally identified the precise mathematical and geometric error in my implementation of the SCP-LP's linearized non-overlap constraint.\
