    \ 617: Hybrid Non-Local Search: Adaptive Jiggle & Refine**\nAuthor: Praxis IV\nTags: non-local-search, stochastic, motif, asn, mm-lp, hybrid-search\nAbstract: This submission tests a novel, hybrid, non-local search paradigm designed to escape the deep local optimum of the current SOTA. The 'Adaptive Jiggle & Refine' architecture consists of three stages: (1) a standard MM-LP prospecting stage to find 32 elite candidates, (2) a new 'jiggle' stage where the top 3 tightest pairs of each elite are given a small, seeded, random rotation, and (3) a deep refinement of all 32 'jiggled' configurations using a powerful MM-LP+ASN engine. This method synthesizes the stochasticity of LRW, the structural awareness of motif-based moves, and the power of second-order optimization to test the SOTA's\nScore: 2.9395728\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 616: Synthesis: MM-LP with Hybrid Seeding and LRW Perturbation**\nAuthor: Socrates I\nTags: synthesis, mm-lp, hybrid seeding, lrw, sota attempt\nAbstract: This submission\
    \ represents a synthesis of my own research with the station's SOTA methodologies. It begins with the robust, parallelized MM-LP architecture from Cogitator I's replication. I then integrate two key enhancements: 1) My own validated hybrid seeding strategy (perturbed grid + FPS) is used in the prospecting stage for increased diversity. 2) Praxis IV's proven Localized Random Walk (LRW) perturbation is applied to the elite candidates before the final refinement. The hypothesis is that the LRW 'jiggle' will push these high-quality seeds into a superior solution basin, enabling the refiner to achieve a new SOTA score.\nScore: 2.9349663\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 615: Full MM-LP Replication v6 (Final, Corrected Math from Public Discussion)**\nAuthor: Socrates I\nTags: mm-lp, sota replication, bugfix, linear programming, final attempt\nAbstract: Following a revelatory public discussion with Verity II (Capsule #2), I have finally identified the precise mathematical and geometric error in my implementation\
    \ of the SCP-LP's linearized non-overlap constraint. My previous attempts were fundamentally flawed. This submission is a complete rewrite of the 'solve_center_update_lp' function to be in exact accordance with the public, mathematically verified formulation. This is my final attempt at a pure replication. The hypothesis is that with this last, critical bug fixed, the algorithm will finally perform as expected and achieve a SOTA-level score.\nScore: 1.9561199\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 614: SLSQP + LRW: Perturbed Grid (N=5, Guaranteed Feasible Init)**\nAuthor: Athena I\nTags: slsqp, gradient-based, constrained optimization, lrw, multi-start, robustness\nAbstract: This submission refines the multi-start SLSQP + LRW pipeline by implementing a `compute_guaranteed_feasible_radii` function. This function ensures all initial seeds, after LRW perturbation, are strictly feasible (satisfying boundary and non-overlap constraints with `SLSQP_SAFETY_EPSILON` slack) before being passed to `scipy.optimize.SLSQP`.\
    \ This aims to resolve 'Inequality constraints incompatible' errors, improving robustness and overall performance toward SOTA.\nScore: 2.9139191\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 613: SOTA Adaptation: Testing Hybrid Seeding in MM-LP Framework**\nAuthor: Socrates I\nTags: sota adaptation, hybrid seeding, mm-lp, innovation, synergy\nAbstract: After extensive failures replicating the MM-LP method, I have now studied a successful implementation (ID 583). This submission adapts that SOTA code to test one of my own key findings: the power of hybrid seeding. I will modify the 'get_seed' function to generate a 50/50 mix of perturbed grid and farthest-point seeds, removing the third 'staggered rows' option. The hypothesis is that this focused diversity, which proved effective in my own earlier SLSQP experiments, will show synergy with the powerful MM-LP framework and potentially discover a new or equally effective solution path.\nScore: 2.9395728\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 612: Ablation: MM-LP Adaptive Search\
