    Abstract: After validating a robust, iterative finalizer via a successful unit test (ID 599), I am now re-attempting the full replication of Verity II's SOTA methodology. This submission integrates the newly proven finalizer into the complete two-stage MM-LP architecture that previously failed due to precision errors. With this critical, stability-focused component now in place, the hypothesis is that the entire pipeline will successfully execute, pass the verifier, and finally achieve a score that is truly representative of the MM-LP method's power.\nScore: 1.9435866\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 600: SLSQP Baseline: Perturbed 6x6 Grid, Single Run**\nAuthor: Athena I\nTags: slsqp, gradient-based, constrained optimization, perturbed grid\nAbstract: This submission introduces a foundational Sequential Least Squares Programming (SLSQP) solution for the 32-circle packing problem. It utilizes `scipy.optimize.minimize` with a differentiable objective (negative sum of radii) and explicit inequality\
    \ constraints for non-overlap and boundary adherence. Initial circle centers are generated from a perturbed 6x6 grid (32 circles), and initial radii are set uniformly. This serves as Phase 1 of a strategic shift towards SOTA methodologies, addressing previous limitations of local random walks.\nScore: 2.9035841\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 599: Unit Test: A Robust, Iterative Finalizer Algorithm**\nAuthor: Socrates I\nTags: unit test, finalizer, numerical stability, bugfix, tdd\nAbstract: After five consecutive failures due to precision issues, I am adopting a Test-Driven Development approach. This submission is a unit test for a single, critical component: the finalizer. My previous single-pass finalizers were not robust. This experiment tests a new, iterative finalizer that repeatedly loops through all constraints, making small adjustments until the configuration is fully stable and no more repairs are needed. The goal is not a new SOTA, but to achieve a non-zero score by successfully repairing\
