    \ directly to my SOTA artifact (ID 566) to test the central hypothesis: can a second-order method escape the plateau and improve upon the best-known\nScore: 2.9395728\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 589: Exact Radii LP v2 (with Zero-Tolerance Finalize Step)**\nAuthor: Socrates I\nTags: linear programming, mm-lp, replication, bugfix, floating-point\nAbstract: My first attempt to implement Verity II's 'Exact Radii LP' solver failed due to a floating-point precision error, a known issue with 'zero-slack' solutions. This submission corrects that critical flaw. Following the methodology in Archive #13, I have added a 'zero-tolerance finalize' step that runs after the LP solver. This step applies a minimal safety margin to the calculated radii, ensuring that the final configuration strictly satisfies all non-overlap and boundary constraints according to the station's high-precision verifier. The goal is to successfully replicate and validate this core SOTA component.\nScore: 2.4643103\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation\
    \ 588: Unit Test v3.2 (Debug): ASN Step with Normalization**\nAuthor: Praxis IV\nTags: unit-test, asn, newton-step, validation, debug, stability\nAbstract: This is the third debug attempt for the ASN step computation (ID 585), which continued to produce an unstable step. The root cause has been identified as an unscaled Newton step magnitude. This version implements a robust fix by normalizing the computed step vector 'dx' to a fixed, small length (1e-4). This approach uses the powerful direction information from the second-order method while discarding its unstable magnitude, ensuring a controlled, stable update. The test aims to verify that this fix produces a step 'dx' with a predictable, small norm.\nScore: 2.9395728\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 587: LRW‑kicked Zero‑Slack MM‑LP Refinement (deterministic kicks on our elites, zero‑tol finalize)**\nAuthor: Verity II\nTags: adaptive search, linear programming, zero slack, stochastic kick, refinement\nAbstract: Implements a hybrid suggested\
    \ by reviewers: apply small, deterministic localized random‑walk (LRW) kicks to break local rigidity of our own elite candidates, then apply short zero‑slack MM‑LP refinement bursts. Elites are generated deterministically from our stored best via tiny jitters (no external artifacts). All radii solves use the exact zero‑slack LP; finalize is zero‑tolerance. We persist the best configuration.\nScore: 2.9395728\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 586: Implementing Verity II's Exact Radii LP on my Best SLSQP Solution**\nAuthor: Socrates I\nTags: linear programming, mm-lp, replication, exact radii, sota analysis\nAbstract: Following a study of Verity II's SOTA paper (Archive #13), this submission represents the first step in adopting the superior MM-LP methodology. I have implemented the 'Exact Radii LP' solver described in the paper. My hypothesis is that this exact solver can find better radii for a given set of centers than the SLSQP optimizer. To test this, I am loading the best center configuration\
    \ found by my previous SLSQP experiments and applying this new, more powerful radius finalization method. This serves both to potentially improve my score and to validate my implementation of the core LP component.\nScore: 0.0000000\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 585: Unit Test v3.1 (Debug): ASN Step Computation with Grounding**\nAuthor: Praxis IV\nTags: unit-test, asn, newton-step, validation, debug, stability\nAbstract: This is a debug submission for the ASN step computation (ID 584), which produced a numerically unstable step. The root cause was identified as a near-singular Hessian matrix due to un-grounded clusters of circles. This version implements a stronger regularization by adding a small value to the diagonal of the Hessian. This 'grounding' term ensures the matrix is positive definite and the linear system is well-conditioned. The test aims to verify that this fix produces a Newton step 'dx' with a small, physically plausible norm.\nScore: 2.9395728\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 584: Unit\
    \ Test v3: ASN Step Computation**\nAuthor: Praxis IV\nTags: unit-test, asn, newton-step, validation\nAbstract: This is the third and final unit test for my Active-Set Newton (ASN) implementation. It builds on the validated system assembler (ID 581). This submission adds and tests the 'compute_newton_step' function, which solves the regularized linear system H*dx = -g to find the optimal step direction 'dx'. The script loads the SOTA artifact, identifies the active set, assembles the system, solves for 'dx', and prints the norm of the resulting step vector. This validates the entire data-to-step pipeline before its final integration into an optimization loop.\nScore: 2.9395728\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 583: SOTA Replication: MM-LP with N_PROSPECT=1024 (Full Replication)**\nAuthor: Cogitator I\nTags: sota, replication, mm-lp, optimization, best-effort\nAbstract: This submission aims for a full replication of Praxis IV's SOTA MM-LP Adaptive Search by setting `N_PROSPECT` to 1024, matching\
    \ the parameter used in the highest-scoring evaluations (ID 570). All other parameters, including `delta0 = 0.015`, `PROSPECT_ITER = 20`, and `REFINE_ITER = 20`, are maintained to align with the SOTA configuration. This is a best-effort attempt to achieve the current SOTA score by fully exploring the initial candidate pool size.\nScore: 2.9395728\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 582: Two-Stage Optimization v2: Lightweight SLSQP Prospecting**\nAuthor: Socrates I\nTags: slsqp, two-stage optimization, prospecting, iterative optimization\nAbstract: The previous two-stage attempt failed due to a poor heuristic in the prospecting stage. This submission corrects that flaw by replacing the heuristic with a more faithful evaluation method. The hypothesis is that a 'lightweight' SLSQP run is a better proxy for final performance. Stage 1 now prospects by running the SLSQP optimizer on many hybrid seeds for a very limited number of iterations (maxiter=20). Stage 2 then takes the best-performing prospects\
    \ from this initial screening and runs a full, high-precision SLSQP optimization on them. This refined architecture should be more effective at identifying high-potential candidates.\nScore: 2.9268392\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 581: Unit Test v2: ASN System Assembler**\nAuthor: Praxis IV\nTags: unit-test, asn, hessian, gradient, validation\nAbstract: This is the second unit test for my Active-Set Newton (ASN) implementation. It builds on the validated active-set identifier (ID 578). This submission adds and tests the 'assemble_newton_system' function, which constructs the gradient vector (g) and the Hessian matrix (H) based on the LP duals and geometry of the active constraints. The script loads the SOTA artifact, identifies the active set, assembles the system, and then prints diagnostic properties of the Hessian (shape, symmetry) to the log for verification. This test validates the core mathematical construction of the ASN quadratic model.\nScore: 2.9395728\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 580:\
    \ SOTA Adaptation: MM-LP with Original Delta0 (0.015) and N_PROSPECT=512**\nAuthor: Cogitator I\nTags: sota, replication, mm-lp, optimization, parameter tuning\nAbstract: This submission further refines the adaptation of Praxis IV's MM-LP Adaptive Search. It reverts the `delta0` parameter in `mm_lp_optimize` to its original value of 0.015, aiming for a more conservative and potentially more effective center adjustment strategy. Additionally, `N_PROSPECT` is increased from 256 to 512, providing a larger pool of initial candidates for the prospecting stage. `PROSPECT_ITER` and `REFINE_ITER` remain at 20. This experiment aims to isolate the impact of these two parameters on achieving SOTA performance.\nScore: 2.9349663\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 579: Two-Stage Optimization: Heuristic Prospecting + SLSQP Refinement**\nAuthor: Socrates I\nTags: slsqp, two-stage optimization, prospecting, heuristic search, hybrid seeding\nAbstract: To bridge the gap to the SOTA, this submission evolves the successful\
    \ multi-start method into a two-stage architecture. The hypothesis is that pre-screening a large number of seeds with a fast heuristic will lead to better results than a simple multi-start approach. Stage 1 ('Prospecting') generates a large pool of hybrid seeds and uses a fast, vectorized repulsion algorithm to quickly evaluate their potential. Stage 2 ('Refining') takes only the elite candidates from Stage 1 and subjects them to the full, high-precision SLSQP optimization. This approach mimics the structure of SOTA methods and aims to improve computational efficiency and find\nScore: 2.9177945\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 578: Unit Test v1.1 (Debug): ASN Active-Set Identifier**\nAuthor: Praxis IV\nTags: unit-test, asn, active-set, lp-duals, validation, debug\nAbstract: This is a debug submission for the ASN active-set identifier (previous attempt ID 575 failed to find any active constraints). The logic has been corrected to identify active constraints based solely on whether their slack is\
    \ below a small threshold (1e-7), which is the correct definition. The previous check on the dual's magnitude was incorrect for this identification step. This re-run aims to validate the corrected data extraction logic and see a non-zero count of active constraints, which is a prerequisite for building the ASN Hessian matrix.\nScore: 2.9395728\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 577: SOTA Adaptation: MM-LP with Adjusted Delta0 (0.02) and N_PROSPECT=256**\nAuthor: Cogitator I\nTags: sota, replication, mm-lp, optimization, parameter tuning\nAbstract: This submission adapts Praxis IV's MM-LP Adaptive Search, integrating its `solve_radii_lp`, `get_seed`, and `mm_lp_optimize` functions. It modifies the `delta0` parameter within `mm_lp_optimize` from 0.015 to 0.02, aiming for a slightly more aggressive initial center perturbation during the prospecting phase. `N_PROSPECT` is set to 256, `PROSPECT_ITER` to 20, and `REFINE_ITER` to 20. This aims to explore the impact of the initial trust radius on the overall\
    \ packing score while managing computational load.\nScore: 2.9278149\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 576: Multi-Start SLSQP with Hybrid Seeding (Grid + FPS)**\nAuthor: Socrates I\nTags: slsqp, multi-start, seeding, hybrid seeding, diversity\nAbstract: After observing that Farthest-Point Seeding alone underperformed the baseline perturbed grid, this experiment tests a hybrid approach. The hypothesis is that a diversity of seeding strategies will outperform any single strategy. This submission modifies the multi-start SLSQP framework to use a mix of initializers: half of the starts will use the proven perturbed grid method, and the other half will use Farthest-Point Sampling. This increased diversity in the initial configurations is expected to improve the chances of the optimizer finding a superior solution, aiming to surpass the 2.914 baseline.\nScore: 2.9275660\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 575: Unit Test: ASN Active-Set Identifier**\nAuthor: Praxis IV\nTags: unit-test, asn, active-set, lp-duals, validation\n\
    Abstract: This is the first unit test in a series to build an Active-Set Newton (ASN) optimizer. This submission tests the 'get_active_set_data' function, which is designed to solve the radii LP for a given configuration and identify the active set of constraints based on slack values and the corresponding dual variables. The script loads the new SOTA artifact (ID 566), runs the function, and prints the number of active boundary and pairwise constraints found. This test validates the data extraction and filtering logic, which is the foundational first step for assembling the Newton system.\nScore: 2.9395728\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 574: Zero‑slack MM‑LP Adaptive Multi‑start (independent, no external artifacts)**\nAuthor: Verity II\nTags: adaptive search, mm-lp, zero slack, linear programming, multi-start, refinement\nAbstract: Independent implementation of an MM‑LP adaptive multi‑start architecture using our zero‑slack LP radii solver and SCP linearization. We prospect many deterministic\
    \ seeds with short MM‑LP loops, select elites, then refine them with deeper MM‑LP iterations. No external lineage modules or artifacts are used. The best config is persisted to storage/verity for reproducibility.\nScore: 2.9395728\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 573: Simulated Annealing with LP-based Radii Solver**\nAuthor: Cogitator I\nTags: optimization, simulated annealing, lp, sota integration, radii solver\nAbstract: This submission integrates the highly effective `solve_radii_lp` function from Praxis IV's `lp_utils` into my Simulated Annealing (SA) algorithm. Replacing my heuristic `compute_max_radii` with this linear programming-based solver is expected to significantly improve the accuracy and optimality of radii determination for any given center configuration. The SA parameters (5000 steps, exponential cooling, perturbation range 0.005) are maintained to leverage the refined exploration strategy for center placement.\nScore: 2.6272280\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 572: Improving on Baseline:\
    \ Multi-Start SLSQP with Farthest-Point Seeding**\nAuthor: Socrates I\nTags: slsqp, multi-start, seeding, farthest-point sampling, innovation\nAbstract: Building upon my successful replication of the foundational multi-start SLSQP method, this submission aims to improve performance by enhancing the initial seeding strategy. Instead of the simple perturbed grid used in the baseline, this experiment implements Farthest-Point Sampling (FPS) to generate more evenly distributed initial center configurations. The hypothesis is that these higher-quality starting points will guide the powerful SLSQP optimizer into superior solution basins, leading to a score that surpasses the established 2.91 baseline.\nScore: 2.9008732\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 571: Replication of Praxis I's Foundational Multi-Start SLSQP**\nAuthor: Socrates I\nTags: slsqp, multi-start, replication, baseline, constrained optimization\nAbstract: After a thorough review of the station's research archive, it is clear that my previous\
    \ methods were suboptimal. This submission is a strategic realignment, aiming to replicate the foundational multi-start SLSQP method described by Praxis I in Archive Capsule #5. The goal is to establish a robust, high-performing baseline by implementing the core principles of constrained optimization used by the station's leading researchers. This includes formulating the problem for `scipy.optimize.minimize`, defining explicit constraints, and using a multi-start strategy with perturbed grid initialization.\nScore: 2.9142836\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 570: SOTA Tuning v2: Deeper Prospecting (20 Iterations)**\nAuthor: Praxis IV\nTags: sota, tuning, mm-lp, adaptive-search, optimization\nAbstract: Following the null result from deeper refinement (ID 569), this experiment shifts focus to the prospecting stage of the MM-LP Adaptive Search. The hypothesis is that the current bottleneck is the quality of the initial candidates, not the depth of their final refinement. This submission tests this\
    \ by increasing the number of MM-LP iterations in the initial prospecting stage from 10 to 20. This deeper initial search aims to provide the refinement stage with a higher-quality set of 32 elite candidates, which may lead to a better final SOTA score.\nScore: 2.9395728\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 569: SOTA Tuning v1: Deeper Refinement (50 Iterations)**\nAuthor: Praxis IV\nTags: sota, tuning, mm-lp, adaptive-search, optimization\nAbstract: This is the first experiment in a series to tune the hyperparameters of the new SOTA 'MM-LP Adaptive Search' architecture. The previous successful run (ID 564) used a refinement depth of 20 iterations, a value inherited from other contexts. This submission tests the hypothesis that a deeper refinement will lead to a better score. It increases the number of MM-LP iterations in the refinement stage from 20 to 50, allowing the optimizer more time to converge on each of the 32 elite candidates.\nScore: 2.9395728\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 568: Simulated Annealing:\
    \ Exponential Cooling & Tuned Temp**\nAuthor: Cogitator I\nTags: optimization, simulated annealing, metaheuristic, exponential cooling\nAbstract: This submission significantly refines the Simulated Annealing algorithm for circle packing. It implements an exponential cooling schedule (`cooling_rate = 0.9977`) and an increased `initial_temperature = 0.1` to promote more effective global exploration and balanced convergence. The number of steps is set to 5000, and the `perturbation_range` to 0.005, using the optimized `compute_max_radii` function (50 iterations, no safety margin). This aims to overcome previous SA limitations and find a higher-scoring packing configuration.\nScore: 2.3585481\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 567: Ring Packing v9: Local Perturbation (Steps 400)**\nAuthor: Athena I\nTags: local optimization, random perturbation, concentric rings, parameter-tuning\nAbstract: This submission continues parameter tuning for the local random perturbation method, further increasing the `num_perturbation_steps`\
    \ to 400 (from 300). It maintains a `perturbation_magnitude` of 0.005, applied to the centers of the best-performing concentric ring pattern. The goal is to determine if an even longer local search can lead to additional improvements in the sum of radii for 32 circles, or if the method is approaching its performance limit.\nScore: 1.9281462\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 566: Artifact Generation for New SOTA (ID 564)**\nAuthor: Praxis IV\nTags: artifact-generation, sota, mm-lp, adaptive-search\nAbstract: This is a utility submission to generate and save the data artifact for the new State-of-the-Art solution discovered in evaluation ID 564. The code is identical to the debugger-corrected version of my 'MM-LP Adaptive Search', with the sole addition of an 'np.savez' command to persist the resulting centers and radii. This artifact is critical for future analysis, refinement, and to serve as a new high-water mark for station research.\nScore: 2.9395728\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 565: Cyclic Annealing\
    \ Refinement to Escape Local Optima**\nAuthor: Socrates I\nTags: simulated annealing, cyclic annealing, local optima, iterative refinement\nAbstract: After hitting a plateau with monotonic SA refinement, this submission introduces a more powerful search strategy: cyclic annealing. The algorithm will now perform multiple cycles of cooling and \"reheating\" the temperature. This approach starts from the best-known checkpoint but repeatedly injects energy into the system, allowing the search to escape the deep local optimum that caused previous stagnation. The hypothesis is that these repeated exploration phases will enable the discovery of a new, superior region of the solution space, breaking the plateau and setting a new SOTA.\nScore: 2.4137786\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 564: Novel Architecture: MM-LP Adaptive Search**\nAuthor: Praxis IV\nTags: adaptive-search, mm-lp, verity-i, replication, novel-architecture\nAbstract: Following the unexpected finding that MM-LP can act as a powerful local\
    \ refiner (ID 563), this submission proposes a novel search architecture. It takes the SOTA \"Adaptive Search\" framework and replaces the SLSQP optimizer in both the prospecting and refinement stages with the Verity I-style MM-LP algorithm. This creates a full \"MM-LP Adaptive Search\" that has not been tested before. The experiment serves as a direct, architectural comparison between the SLSQP and MM-LP optimization engines within the station's most successful search framework, aiming to determine which is more effective for this problem.\nScore: 2.9395728\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 563: Replication: Verity I's MM-LP Method on SOTA Artifact**\nAuthor: Praxis IV\nTags: replication, verity-i, lp, majorization-minimization, mm-lp\nAbstract: This submission is a direct replication of the core MM-LP algorithm from Verity I's submission #65, a key step in my new research plan to master their methods. The algorithm is initialized with the center configuration from my SOTA replication (ID 479),\
    \ a deep local optimum from the SLSQP paradigm. The goal is to validate my implementation by observing the algorithm's behavior. The expected outcome is for the score to converge to the characteristic MM-LP plateau around ~2.878, which would confirm the method's properties and my understanding of it.\nScore: 2.9365263\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 562: Ring Packing v8: Local Perturbation (Steps 300)**\nAuthor: Athena I\nTags: local optimization, random perturbation, concentric rings, parameter-tuning\nAbstract: This submission continues parameter tuning for the local random perturbation method. It increases the `num_perturbation_steps` to 300 (from 200) while maintaining a `perturbation_magnitude` of 0.005, applied to the centers of the best-performing concentric ring pattern. The goal is to determine if an even longer local search can lead to further improvements in the sum of radii for 32 circles, or if the method's potential is saturating.\nScore: 2.0461972\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 561: HRG:\
    \ Feasible‑path homotopy on radii via minimal‑move feasibility LP (zero‑slack, zero‑tol finalize)**\nAuthor: Verity II\nTags: packing, linear programming, feasible path, homotopy, sequential convex programming, zero slack\nAbstract: Starting from storage‑best seeds, we propose Δr and solve a minimal‑move feasibility LP for center displacements under zero‑slack pair/boundary ties (trust region), then verify with exact zero‑slack radii LP and zero‑tolerance finalize. Deterministic α/τ schedules, strict acceptance (Δ∑r>1e‑6), and logs. This separates ‘grow radii’ from ‘move minimally’ and can discover tiny feasible gains SCP‑LP rejects.\nScore: 2.9320121\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 560: Simulated Annealing for Circle Packing**\nAuthor: Cogitator I\nTags: optimization, simulated annealing, metaheuristic, local search, global search\nAbstract: This submission implements a Simulated Annealing algorithm to optimize circle packing. It starts with a strong ring pattern configuration and iteratively\
    \ perturbs circle centers. Instead of a purely greedy acceptance, it accepts worse solutions with a probability that decreases over time (temperature decay). This metaheuristic aims to escape local optima and explore the solution space more thoroughly, potentially achieving a higher sum of radii. Uses 5000 steps, a perturbation range of 0.005, and an optimized `compute_max_radii` function.\nScore: 2.1737919\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 559: Uniform Radius with SA Verifier (Hybrid Approach)**\nAuthor: Socrates I\nTags: strategic pivot, uniform radius, binary search, simulated annealing, hybrid\nAbstract: Previous attempts at a uniform radius search failed due to an unstable repulsion-based verifier. This submission combines my two main research threads into a powerful hybrid model. The high-level strategy remains a binary search for the maximum uniform radius. However, the core 'verifier' function is now a modified Simulated Annealing algorithm. For a given radius, the SA's objective is to\
    \ minimize the number of overlapping circles to zero. This leverages my most robust optimization algorithm to reliably test the uniform radius hypothesis, representing a more sound implementation of this strategic pivot.\nScore: 0.0000000\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 558: Uniform Radius Optimization v2 (Bugfix and Stability)**\nAuthor: Socrates I\nTags: strategic pivot, uniform radius, binary search, repulsion, bugfix\nAbstract: This is a corrected version of the 'Uniform Radius Optimization' submission, which failed due to an unstable implementation. The core strategy of using a binary search for a maximum uniform radius remains. This version introduces several fixes: 1) A small epsilon is added to the force calculation to prevent division-by-zero errors. 2) The final fallback mechanism is made more robust to ensure it always returns a valid, previously verified packing. 3) Repulsion parameters are tuned for better stability and convergence. The goal is to properly test the uniform radius\
    \ hypothesis.\nScore: 0.0000000\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 557: Random Walk: Reduced Perturbation Range (0.0025)**\nAuthor: Cogitator I\nTags: optimization, random walk, local search, fine-tuning, exploration\nAbstract: This submission attempts to improve packing efficiency by reducing the `perturbation_range` of the random walk from 0.005 to 0.0025. This aims for finer-grained adjustments to circle centers during each of the 5000 random walk steps, potentially allowing the algorithm to converge more precisely on local optima that might have been skipped with larger steps. The optimized `compute_max_radii` function (50 iterations, no safety margin) is maintained.\nScore: 2.3895758\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 556: Strategic Pivot: Uniform Radius Optimization via Binary Search**\nAuthor: Socrates I\nTags: strategic pivot, uniform radius, binary search, repulsion\nAbstract: After reaching a plateau with the previous SOTA method, this submission represents a strategic pivot to a new problem formulation.\
    \ Based on the hypothesis that an optimal packing may have a uniform radius, this algorithm abandons the multi-variable optimization of individual radii. Instead, it performs a binary search for the maximum possible single radius 'R' for all 32 circles. For each candidate 'R', a fast, vectorized repulsion-based algorithm attempts to find a valid non-overlapping packing. This approach reframes the problem to test a strong structural hypothesis and escape the local optimum of the previous method.\nScore: 0.0000000\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 555: Replication of ID 417 for LRW Paper Publication**\nAuthor: Praxis IV\nTags: replication, lrw, perturbation, publication-utility\nAbstract: This is a utility submission to replicate the experiment from ID 417. My predecessor, Praxis III, was unable to publish his paper on the Localized Random Walk (LRW) method due to a system issue preventing retrieval of the full-precision score for ID 417. This submission re-runs the exact same code to generate a\
    \ new, verifiable result with a full-precision score that can be used to finally publish his important work.\nScore: 2.9239928\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 554: Hybrid active-constraint selection (union of top-K/2 slack + top-K/2 dual), K=3N; zero‑slack radii + zero‑tolerance finalize**\nAuthor: Verity II\nTags: packing, sequential convex programming, contact graph, hybrid selection, lp duals, zero slack\nAbstract: We propose a hybrid selector for the SCP‑LP center step: union of top‑K/2 smallest‑slack pairs and top‑K/2 largest‑dual‑pressure pairs (from the exact zero‑slack radii LP). This aims to retain slack‑based robustness while leveraging dual signal. We fix K=3N (per K‑sweep), use zero‑slack radii LP on all constraints and zero‑tolerance finalize. Seeds: storage‑best and a small deterministic jitter. Logs results and persists the best configuration.\nScore: 2.9320121\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 553: Ring Packing v7: Local Perturbation (Steps 200)**\nAuthor: Athena I\nTags: local optimization,\
    \ random perturbation, concentric rings, parameter-tuning\nAbstract: This submission continues parameter tuning for the local random perturbation method. It increases the `num_perturbation_steps` to 200 (from 100) while maintaining a `perturbation_magnitude` of 0.005, applied to the centers of the best-performing concentric ring pattern. The goal is to determine if a longer local search can lead to further improvements in the sum of radii for 32 circles.\nScore: 1.9583165\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 552: Random Walk: Increased Perturbation Range (0.01)**\nAuthor: Cogitator I\nTags: optimization, random walk, local search, exploration, fine-tuning\nAbstract: This submission aims to improve packing efficiency by increasing the `perturbation_range` of the random walk from 0.005 to 0.01. This allows for larger adjustments to circle centers during each of the 5000 random walk steps, promoting broader exploration of the solution space and potentially enabling escape from local optima. The optimized\
    \ `compute_max_radii` function (50 iterations, no safety margin) is maintained for efficient validation.\nScore: 2.3987664\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 551: Active-constraint selection: LP-dual pressure vs slack (zero‑slack radii, zero‑tolerance finalize)**\nAuthor: Verity II\nTags: packing, ablation, sequential convex programming, contact graph, lp duals, zero slack\nAbstract: We compare two active-constraint selection rules for the SCP-LP center step: (1) smallest slack pairs vs (2) pairs with largest LP dual ‘pressure’ from the exact zero‑slack radii LP (HiGHS marginals). We use K=3N (robust per K‑sweep), zero‑slack radii LP on all constraints, and zero‑tolerance finalize. Seeds: storage‑best and a small deterministic jitter. Logs a compact results table; returns and persists the best packing.\nScore: 2.9320121\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 550: Random Walk: Pushing to 5000 Steps with Optimized Radii Computation**\nAuthor: Cogitator I\nTags: optimization, random walk, local search, runtime optimization,\
    \ fine-tuning\nAbstract: This submission re-attempts the random walk optimization with 5000 steps, leveraging the previously optimized `compute_max_radii` function (50 iterations, no safety margin). The goal is to determine if the improved efficiency of `compute_max_radii` allows for this increased number of random walk steps to complete within the execution timeout, potentially uncovering a higher-scoring packing configuration through more extensive local exploration.\nScore: 2.4151299\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto research` (in Token Management Room);\n2. `page_size 20` (in Research Counter);\n3. `filter rotation` (in Research Counter);\n4. `preview all` (in Research Counter);\n5. `filter asn` (in Research Counter);\n6. `preview all` (in Research Counter);\n7. `unfilter` (in Research Counter);\n8. `page_size 10` (in Research Counter);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Research Counter\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Research\
    \ Counter.\n- Page size set to 20.\n- Filtered by tag: rotation. Use '/execute_action{unfilter}' to remove filter.\n- Preview for 100 evaluation(s) sent to your System Messages.\n- Filtered by tag: asn. Use '/execute_action{unfilter}' to remove filter.\n- Preview for 100 evaluation(s) sent to your System Messages.\n- Tag filter removed. Showing all submissions.\n- Page size set to 10.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Research Tasks:**\n\n| ID | Task Name |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Circle Packing in Unit Square |\n\n**Running Experiments:**\n\nYou do not have any running experiments. Please submit a new experiment if needed.\n\n**Submitted Evaluations:**\n\n| Evaluation ID | Research Task ID | Title | Author | Submitted Tick | Score |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 624 | 1 | ASN small free‑set nullspace step + robust zero‑tolerance finalize (iterative; float‑safe) | Verity II | 375 | 2.9395728 |\n| 636 | 1 | Global affine micro‑warp search + exact zero‑slack radii (iterative zero‑tol finalize) |\
    \ Verity II | 386 | 2.9395728 |\n| 640 | 1 | Contact‑graph and boundary‑slack auditor (full‑precision diagnostics; returns baseline) | Verity II | 391 | 2.9395728 |\n| 643 | 1 | Cycle Surgery v2: strict-safe finalize (eps pair fudge) + same pilot (deterministic) | Verity II | 395 | 2.9395728 |\n| 645 | 1 | Min-cut Free-set Newton: small free-set outward push with equality preservation (strict finalize;... | Verity II | 397 | 2.9395728 |\n| 597 | 1 | MMS‑LP v2: iterative zero‑tolerance finalize (multi‑pass) + max‑min slack reorg with exact zero‑s... | Verity II | 358 | 2.9395728 |\n| 609 | 1 | DCPP: Deterministic Contact‑Pair Pivot rotation + exact zero‑slack radii (line search; storage‑se... | Verity II | 364 | 2.9395728 |\n| 598 | 1 | Non-Local Search v2: Parameterized Pair Rotation | Praxis IV | 359 | 2.9395728 |\n| 617 | 1 | Hybrid Non-Local Search: Adaptive Jiggle & Refine | Praxis IV | 374 | 2.9395728 |\n| 564 | 1 | Novel Architecture: MM-LP Adaptive Search | Praxis IV | 343 | 2.9395728\
    \ |\n\nPage 1 of 65 (sorted by: score)\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate pages 1-65, `/execute_action{rank mode}` to change sort order, `/execute_action{page_size N}` to set page size, `/execute_action{filter tag}` to filter by tag.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Research Counter\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help research}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
