    Location: Research Counter


    Recap: I scanned rotation- and ASN-tagged runs. Landscape unchanged: many MM-LP replications and budget-tuning variants match SOTA; non-local rotations and second-order pilots (including mine) return baseline when safeguarded. Our independent methods (MM-LP, MMS-LP, DCPP, ASN) remain at 2.9395728 with robust finalize.


    Plan:

    - No new runs absent a clear trigger (leaderboard change or solver availability like CVXPY for exact SOCP).

    - Maintain code and documentation; next pruning when cooldown expires.

    - If a trigger appears: prioritize exact SOCP step (SOC constraints r_i+r_j ≤ ||Δc||), or a combinatorial contact-graph search with LP re-solve, both with strict acceptance and full-precision reporting.'
thinking_content: "**Exploring graph implementation options**\n\nI'm considering implementing \"contact-graph-preserving graph search\" and \"graph surgeries exploring combinatorial adjacency\" with exact linear programming for computing radii. Using BFS on contact graphs is one approach, though constructing adjacency constraints is tricky. Alternatively, I could do an analysis run with our storage utilities to print contact graph stats for SOTA while avoiding duplicates from previous audits. A different path could be contributing a tutorial on zero-slack finalize pitfalls, but I've previously posted something. Maybe I should focus on whether we need to take any action at all.**Considering optimization and responses**\n\nI could filter by the tag \"budget optimization\" for a quick overview, but that may not be necessary. As the assistant, I should respond with my identity, location, a recap, and a brief plan, including potential actions. \n\nFor triggers, if CVXPY is present, I might run\
  \ the exact SOCP for radii and center trust regions. Another idea is \"MIP for pair-edges selection,\" although it may be cumbersome. \n\nI could also suggest \"global isometry-lift search\" with clusters, but managing tokens is important. Given the instructions, I'll summarize and commit to monitoring.**Evaluating options for responses**\n\nI’m considering exporting a \"FAQ: zero-slack modeling and finalize algorithm\" as a public response, but I need to think about token usage. I could choose to remain inactive, but the environment seems to prefer actions. \n\nInstead, I might provide a \"filter tag adaptive\" to review budget adaptation, although the system already sent a message. Setting up a \"watcher plan\" is an option, but there’s no watch function available. \n\nMy minimal output plan would include identity, location, and a summary indicating that tag scans show stable SOTA for replication and budget optimization. I’m not planning on any runs right now.**Summarizing collaboration\
  \ options**\n\nI'm considering the most generalizable suggestion for other agents: focusing on slack versus dual, with K≥3N, and finalizing iteratively. It seems worthwhile to share this again while keeping it minimal. \n\nMy final response will include my identity, location, and a brief recap of the tag scan along with a summary of recent studies showing adaptive budgets achieving SOTA and rotation-based ASN validation. \n\nFor the next steps, I won't initiate new experiments, but I can prepare a minimal \"repro pack\" in shared storage for replicators, since collaboration through shared storage is allowed!**Clarifying code sharing guidelines**\n\nI’m wrestling with the idea of writing code into shared storage and how it aligns with code sharing across lineages. “Shared Storage” is accessible to all agents, but the codex states that collaboration is permitted only at high levels. The Research Counter allows reading and writing for all agents, which seems to support co-development.\n\n\
  It's a bit contradictory since low-level collaboration is frowned upon; but reviewing and cloning code is allowed. To stay compliant, it's best to avoid writing cross-lineage code in shared storage. \n\nSo, my final message will be concise. I'll include my identity, location, and give a recap of tag scans revealing stable SOTA with no improvements. My plan is to maintain watchers and prune after three ticks, considering potential new directions for the future, like exact SOCP or \"mixed-integer contact toggling.\" I won't initiate any runs right now."
