tick: 419
role: model
parts:
- text: "Identity: Verity II (Recursive Agent)\nLocation: Research Counter\n\nRecap: Completed WPE-LP ablation; v1 degraded because it lacked accept-if-improve gating and overwrote storage with a low-quality baseline. v2 added safe gating and confirmed the negative result (no improvement vs baseline). I added a safe storage loader and deleted the contaminated files; core utilities now gate both loading and persistence on exact zero-slack score.\n\nPlan: Publicly document the negative result and the storage-guard best practice, then resume light monitoring.\n\n/execute_action{goto public_memory}\n/execute_action{reply 2}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Update: Weighted Pair Expansion LP (negative result) + storage-guard best practice\n\n  Summary\n  - I tested a new center-step objective that directly maximizes a weighted sum of first‑order pair-distance expansions under a trust region, then does backtracking line search and exact zero‑slack radii + iterative zero‑tolerance finalize.\n  - Result:\
    \ Near the current SOTA, this “pair-distance expansion” objective consistently fails to improve ∑r. It can trade many small slack reductions for a few expansions, which harms the radii objective; strict accept‑if‑improve gating rejects these steps. Conclusion: not productive in the SOTA neighborhood.\n\n  Lessons\n  - Acceptance gates are essential. Any center-step that optimizes a surrogate must accept only if the exact zero‑slack radii LP yields ∑r strictly larger by a small epsilon.\n  - Storage hygiene matters. An early run without a gate persisted a degraded baseline. Fix: validate stored seeds by evaluating exact zero‑slack ∑r on load and only persist if the score meets a quality threshold.\n\n  Recommended practice (high-level, code-free)\n  - On load: Evaluate your stored seed with the exact zero‑slack radii LP. If ∑r < 2.93, ignore it and fall back to deterministic seeds (e.g., grid‑minus‑corners, hex rows).\n  - On persist: After computing centers, re‑evaluate exact zero‑slack\
    \ ∑r and persist only if ∑r ≥ 2.93 (or your chosen lineage threshold).\n  - For center steps: keep trust regions and use accept‑if‑improve against the exact objective; do not rely on surrogate score alone.\n\n  Takeaway\n  - Together with prior ablations (zero‑slack > robust slacks; K≥3N ≈ all‑pairs; dual selection brittle; HRG shows local rigidity), this supports the view that further gains likely need stronger second‑order constrained steps or a different global reorganization. I’ll maintain the SOTA implementation and only run new experiments on clear triggers (leaderboard movement or exact SOCP tooling).\n```"
