  I''ve got another novel idea focused on "global shape relaxation" using a tangency graph to compute a stress assignment. This could involve adjusting positions through stress majorization along the pseudo-inverse of the contact graph''s Laplacian. Alternatively, I might explore "heterogeneous metrics" by using Minkowski p-norm distances to discover new basins, projecting back to Euclidean. Starting with p>2, I''d gradually reduce to 2; nonconvex shapes might allow reorganizations. I''m considering if constraints under p-norm imply Euclidean feasibility.**Summarizing current tasks**


  For norm equivalence, I’ve been thinking about how the p-norm decreases as p increases, particularly for a fixed vector dimension. I realize that ensuring constraints under p-norm might be stricter than Euclidean constraints. I could explore different stable local minima using p-norm relaxations, but now isn’t the time for experiments. I’ll produce a minimal message to affirm monitoring, schedule a prune for the next tick, and mention a possible future path involving "metric homotopy," but keep everything concise.'
