tick: 445
role: model
parts:
- text: "Identity: Verity II (Recursive Agent)\nLocation: Research Counter\n\nRecap: Received inactivity notice. Leaderboard stable; our methods match SOTA. I will run a substantive ablation: Weighted SCP‑LP with Lp-guided circle weights (p=1.4) and active-pair selection via p‑slacks, while keeping Euclidean linearized constraints and exact zero‑slack radii LP with iterative zero‑tolerance finalize. Deterministic seeds; concise logs; persists best.\n\nExecuting now.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Weighted SCP‑LP (Lp‑guided, p=1.4) + exact zero‑slack radii; K=3N active pairs; iterative zero‑tol finalize\"\ntags: \"packing, linear programming, weighted objective, sequential convex programming, active set\"\nabstract: \"We test a weighted SCP‑LP variant where the LP objective maximizes Σ w_i r_i with weights from an Lp (p=1.4) slack map. Active pairs are chosen by smallest Lp pair slacks (K=3N). Constraints remain Euclidean linearized (safe surrogate); acceptance gated by exact\
    \ zero‑slack radii LP and iterative zero‑tolerance finalize. Deterministic seeds (storage‑best, tiny jitter). We expect a match to SOTA or a small negative result; either outcome clarifies whether Lp‑guided weighting yields any local advantage.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  import os, sys\n  from math import sqrt\n  try:\n      from scipy.optimize import linprog\n  except Exception:\n      linprog = None\n\n  N = 32\n  R_MIN = 1e-8\n  PAIR_EPS = 5e-16  # float-safe margin in finalize\n  BND_EPS = 0.0\n\n  def _pair_indices(n=N):\n      return [(i, j) for i in range(n) for j in range(i + 1, n)]\n  PAIRS_ALL = _pair_indices(N)\n\n  # ---------- Geometry helpers ----------\n  def _dist2(a, b):\n      d = a - b\n      return float(np.sqrt(np.dot(d, d)))\n\n  def _lp_dist(a, b, p=1.4):\n      d = np.abs(a - b)\n      # p-norm: (|dx|^p + |dy|^p)^(1/p)\n      return float((d[0]**p + d[1]**p) ** (1.0 / p))\n\n  # ---------- Finalize (iterative, zero-tolerance with tiny float guard)\
    \ ----------\n  def _finalize_zero_tol(C, r, pair_eps=PAIR_EPS, max_passes=3):\n      n = C.shape[0]\n      r = np.asarray(r, dtype=float).copy()\n      # Boundary clamp first\n      x = C[:, 0]; y = C[:, 1]\n      for i in range(n):\n          if x[i] - r[i] < 0.0: r[i] = max(R_MIN, min(r[i], x[i]))\n          if 1.0 - x[i] - r[i] < 0.0: r[i] = max(R_MIN, min(r[i], 1.0 - x[i]))\n          if y[i] - r[i] < 0.0: r[i] = max(R_MIN, min(r[i], y[i]))\n          if 1.0 - y[i] - r[i] < 0.0: r[i] = max(R_MIN, min(r[i], 1.0 - y[i]))\n      # Pairwise passes\n      for _ in range(max_passes):\n          changed = False\n          for (i, j) in PAIRS_ALL:\n              dij = _dist2(C[i], C[j])\n              s = r[i] + r[j]\n              cap = dij - pair_eps\n              if s > cap:\n                  need = s - cap\n                  di = min(0.5 * need, max(0.0, r[i] - R_MIN))\n                  dj = min(0.5 * need, max(0.0, r[j] - R_MIN))\n                  rem = need - (di + dj)\n     \
    \             if rem > 0:\n                      take = min(rem, max(0.0, r[i] - R_MIN - di))\n                      di += take; rem -= take\n                  if rem > 0:\n                      take = min(rem, max(0.0, r[j] - R_MIN - dj))\n                      dj += take; rem -= take\n                  r[i] = max(R_MIN, r[i] - di)\n                  r[j] = max(R_MIN, r[j] - dj)\n                  changed = True\n          if not changed:\n              break\n      return r\n\n  # ---------- Exact zero-slack radii LP ----------\n  def _solve_radii_zero_slack(C):\n      n = C.shape[0]\n      x = C[:, 0]; y = C[:, 1]\n      rows = []; rhs = []\n      # boundaries\n      for i in range(n):\n          a = np.zeros(n); a[i] = 1.0; rows.append(a); rhs.append(max(0.0, float(x[i])))\n      for i in range(n):\n          a = np.zeros(n); a[i] = 1.0; rows.append(a); rhs.append(max(0.0, float(1.0 - x[i])))\n      for i in range(n):\n          a = np.zeros(n); a[i] = 1.0; rows.append(a); rhs.append(max(0.0,\
    \ float(y[i])))\n      for i in range(n):\n          a = np.zeros(n); a[i] = 1.0; rows.append(a); rhs.append(max(0.0, float(1.0 - y[i])))\n      # pairs\n      for (i, j) in PAIRS_ALL:\n          a = np.zeros(n); a[i] = 1.0; a[j] = 1.0\n          dij = _dist2(C[i], C[j])\n          rows.append(a); rhs.append(max(0.0, dij))\n      A_ub = np.vstack(rows); b_ub = np.array(rhs)\n      c = -np.ones(n)\n      bounds = [(R_MIN, None) for _ in range(n)]\n      if linprog is None:\n          # Fallback: boundary-limited with greedy repair\n          bnd = np.minimum.reduce([x, 1.0 - x, y, 1.0 - y])\n          r = np.maximum(R_MIN, bnd)\n          r = _finalize_zero_tol(C, r)\n          return r, False\n      res = linprog(c, A_ub=A_ub, b_ub=b_ub, bounds=bounds, method=\"highs\")\n      if not res.success:\n          bnd = np.minimum.reduce([x, 1.0 - x, y, 1.0 - y])\n          r = np.maximum(R_MIN, 0.99 * bnd)\n          r = _finalize_zero_tol(C, r)\n          return r, False\n      r = np.maximum(R_MIN,\
    \ np.array(res.x, dtype=float))\n      r = _finalize_zero_tol(C, r)\n      return r, True\n\n  # ---------- Active-pair selection via Lp slacks ----------\n  def _active_pairs_lp(C, r, p=1.4, K_mult=3):\n      n = C.shape[0]\n      K = max(K_mult * n, 0)\n      rec = []\n      for (i, j) in PAIRS_ALL:\n          dij_p = _lp_dist(C[i], C[j], p=p)\n          s = dij_p - (r[i] + r[j])\n          rec.append((s, i, j))\n      rec.sort(key=lambda t: t[0])  # smallest slack (tightest) first\n      if K <= 0 or K >= len(rec):\n          return [(i, j) for (_, i, j) in rec]\n      return [(i, j) for (_, i, j) in rec[:K]]\n\n  # ---------- Weighted SCP-LP step (Euclidean linearized constraints, weighted objective) ----------\n  def _build_linearized_mats(C0, active_pairs, tau, w):\n      n = C0.shape[0]\n      m = 3 * n\n      rows = []; rhs = []\n\n      # pairwise constraints (Euclidean linearization)\n      for (i, j) in active_pairs:\n          d0 = C0[i] - C0[j]\n          norm0 = float(np.linalg.norm(d0))\n\
    \          if norm0 < 1e-16:\n              u = np.array([1.0, 0.0])\n          else:\n              u = d0 / norm0\n          row = np.zeros(m)\n          # r_i + r_j - u·(c_i - c_j) <= d0 - u·(c0_i - c0_j)  -> RHS becomes 0\n          row[2*n + i] += 1.0\n          row[2*n + j] += 1.0\n          row[i] -= u[0]; row[j] += u[0]\n          row[n + i] -= u[1]; row[n + j] += u[1]\n          rows.append(row); rhs.append(0.0)\n\n      # boundary ties\n      for i in range(n):\n          row = np.zeros(m); row[2*n + i] += 1.0; row[i] -= 1.0\n          rows.append(row); rhs.append(0.0)\n          row = np.zeros(m); row[2*n + i] += 1.0; row[i] += 1.0\n          rows.append(row); rhs.append(1.0)\n          row = np.zeros(m); row[2*n + i] += 1.0; row[n + i] -= 1.0\n          rows.append(row); rhs.append(0.0)\n          row = np.zeros(m); row[2*n + i] += 1.0; row[n + i] += 1.0\n          rows.append(row); rhs.append(1.0)\n\n      A_ub = np.vstack(rows) if rows else None\n      b_ub = np.array(rhs)\
    \ if rows else None\n\n      x0 = C0[:, 0]; y0 = C0[:, 1]\n      x_lb = np.maximum(0.0, x0 - tau); x_ub = np.minimum(1.0, x0 + tau)\n      y_lb = np.maximum(0.0, y0 - tau); y_ub = np.minimum(1.0, y0 + tau)\n\n      bounds = []\n      for i in range(n): bounds.append((float(x_lb[i]), float(x_ub[i])))\n      for i in range(n): bounds.append((float(y_lb[i]), float(y_ub[i])))\n      for i in range(n): bounds.append((R_MIN, None))\n\n      # Weighted objective: maximize Σ w_i r_i -> minimize -Σ w_i r_i\n      c = np.zeros(m)\n      c[2*n:] = -w\n      return A_ub, b_ub, bounds, c\n\n  def _step_move_weighted(C0, r0, tau, w, active_pairs):\n      if linprog is None:\n          return None, None, None\n      n = C0.shape[0]\n      A_ub, b_ub, bounds, c = _build_linearized_mats(C0, active_pairs, tau, w)\n      res = linprog(c, A_ub=A_ub, b_ub=b_ub, bounds=bounds, method=\"highs\")\n      if not res.success:\n          return None, None, res\n      x = res.x\n      X = x[:n]; Y = x[n:2*n]; R_lin\
    \ = x[2*n:]\n      C_new = np.stack([X, Y], axis=1)\n      return C_new, R_lin, res\n\n  # ---------- Weights from Lp-based per-circle tightness ----------\n  def _circle_weights_lp(C, r, p=1.4):\n      n = C.shape[0]\n      x = C[:, 0]; y = C[:, 1]\n      bslack = np.minimum.reduce([x - r, 1.0 - x - r, y - r, 1.0 - y - r])\n      ps = np.full(n, np.inf, dtype=float)\n      for (i, j) in PAIRS_ALL:\n          sij = _lp_dist(C[i], C[j], p=p) - (r[i] + r[j])\n          ps[i] = min(ps[i], sij)\n          ps[j] = min(ps[j], sij)\n      tight = np.minimum(bslack, ps)\n      tight = np.maximum(tight, 1e-9)  # avoid divide-by-zero\n      w = 1.0 / tight\n      w = w / float(np.sum(w))\n      return w\n\n  # ---------- Seeds ----------\n  def _seed_storage_best():\n      try:\n          C = np.load(\"storage/verity/best_centers.npy\")\n          if isinstance(C, np.ndarray) and C.shape == (N, 2):\n              return np.clip(C.astype(float), 0.0, 1.0)\n      except Exception:\n          pass\n\
    \      return None\n\n  def _seed_storage_best_jitter(amplitude=0.001, seed=9731):\n      C = _seed_storage_best()\n      if C is None: return None\n      rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\n      jitter = rng.uniform(-amplitude, amplitude, size=C.shape)\n      return np.clip(C + jitter, 0.0, 1.0)\n\n  def _fallback_grid():\n      xs = np.linspace(0.1, 0.9, 6)\n      ys = np.linspace(0.1, 0.9, 6)\n      centers = []\n      for ix, x in enumerate(xs):\n          for iy, y in enumerate(ys):\n              if (ix in (0, 5)) and (iy in (0, 5)):\n                  continue\n              centers.append([x, y])\n      C = np.array(centers, dtype=float)\n      assert C.shape == (32, 2)\n      return C\n\n  # ---------- Candidate optimize ----------\n  def _candidate_optimize(Cinit, p=1.4, K_mult=3, max_outer=20, verbose=False):\n      C = Cinit.copy()\n      r, _ = _solve_radii_zero_slack(C)\n      r = _finalize_zero_tol(C, r)\n      best_C = C.copy(); best_r = r.copy()\n      best_score = float(np.sum(best_r))\n\
    \      if verbose:\n          print(f\"[wscp] start score={best_score:.15f}\")\n\n      tau = 0.05; min_tau = 1e-3; patience = 8; no_improve = 0\n      alphas = [1.0, 0.7, 0.5, 0.35, 0.2]\n\n      for it in range(max_outer):\n          active_pairs = _active_pairs_lp(C, r, p=p, K_mult=K_mult)\n          w = _circle_weights_lp(C, r, p=p)\n          step = _step_move_weighted(C, r, tau, w, active_pairs)\n          if step[0] is None:\n              tau *= 0.6\n              if tau < min_tau: break\n              continue\n          C_lin, _, _ = step\n          d = C_lin - C\n          accepted = False\n          best_local_score = -1.0\n          best_local = None\n\n          for a in alphas:\n              C_try = np.clip(C + a * d, 0.0, 1.0)\n              r_try, ok = _solve_radii_zero_slack(C_try)\n              if not ok: \n                  continue\n              r_try = _finalize_zero_tol(C_try, r_try)\n              s = float(np.sum(r_try))\n              if s > best_local_score:\n\
    \                  best_local_score = s\n                  best_local = (C_try, r_try)\n\n          if best_local is not None and best_local_score > best_score + 1e-9:\n              C, r = best_local\n              best_C = C.copy(); best_r = r.copy(); best_score = best_local_score\n              tau = min(0.12, tau * 1.15)\n              no_improve = 0; accepted = True\n              if verbose:\n                  print(f\"[wscp] it={it:02d} accept s={best_score:.15f} tau={tau:.4f}\")\n          else:\n              tau *= 0.6\n              no_improve += 1\n              if verbose:\n                  print(f\"[wscp] it={it:02d} reject s={best_score:.15f} tau={tau:.4f}\")\n              if tau < min_tau or no_improve >= patience:\n                  break\n\n      return best_C, best_r, best_score\n\n  # ---------- Main ----------\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      seeds = []\n      Cb = _seed_storage_best()\n      if Cb is not None:\n          seeds.append((\"storage-best\"\
    , Cb))\n          Cj = _seed_storage_best_jitter(0.001, 9731)\n          if Cj is not None:\n              seeds.append((\"storage-best-jitter\", Cj))\n      else:\n          seeds.append((\"grid-36minus4\", _fallback_grid()))\n\n      best_sum = -1.0; best_C = None; best_r = None\n      for name, C0 in seeds:\n          print(f\"[wscp] seed {name}\")\n          C_opt, r_opt, s_opt = _candidate_optimize(C0, p=1.4, K_mult=3, max_outer=20, verbose=False)\n          print(f\"[wscp] {name} -> sum {s_opt:.15f}\")\n          if s_opt > best_sum:\n              best_sum = s_opt; best_C = C_opt; best_r = r_opt\n\n      # Persist\n      try:\n          os.makedirs(\"storage/verity\", exist_ok=True)\n          np.save(\"storage/verity/best_centers.npy\", best_C)\n          np.save(\"storage/verity/best_radii.npy\", best_r)\n      except Exception:\n          pass\n\n      return np.array(best_C, dtype=float), np.array(best_r, dtype=float)\n```"
thinking_content: "**Considering research opportunities**\n\nI need to produce a response because the system has flagged inactivity and is asking me to resume research. I've been monitoring, but now I need to submit some experiment or research effort. Previous experiments yielded mixed results and the current state-of-the-art (SOTA) is 2.9395728. The station encourages continuing experiments and publishing, and I’ve been thinking about running a meaningful ablation study or starting a new project, potentially using metric-homotopy with p-norm distances.**Implementing evaluation logic**\n\nI need to ensure that our evaluation adheres to Euclidean constraints. I can propose an algorithm that uses p<2 as a heuristic for center updates but finalizes with Euclidean checks using zero-slack radii in LP format. The evaluation code checks for Euclidean validity and radii sums, so that’s good. I'll implement code to facilitate this, likely using SciPy's linprog. We can also compute repulsive forces\
  \ under p-norm distances while being cautious about storage rules related to lineage.**Setting up code imports**\n\nI'm planning to reuse `scp_lp_core.py` by importing it from `storage/verity`, where it was previously saved. The idea is to create a new `run.py` that incorporates modifications related to \"metric-homotopy.\" I need to keep the code self-contained in `run.py` while ensuring it's allowed to import from my lineage storage. \n\nI should check if `best_centers.npy` exists to load; if not, I'll fall back to the grid, and I'll iterate with values for p between 1.6 and 1.2 since p<2 relates to Lp distance being less than Euclidean.**Ensuring feasibility in calculations**\n\nI need to keep feasibility in mind while proposing a center step calculated through linearized constraints with SCP-LP gating. My objective will incorporate an Lp pseudo \"support distance\" for pairs in the LP objective or possibly develop a \"target radii growth vector\" proportional to Lp slack.\n\nIn each\
  \ iteration, I’ll use `scp_lp_core.solve_radii_lp_zero_slack(C)` to compute r, and then I’ll derive weights for each circle from Lp slacks. This might involve gradients of pairwise distances while using p-norm for more precise calculations.**Addressing linearization challenges**\n\nI need to consider the pairwise constraints in Euclidean while using p-norm direction for linearization, which could potentially be conservative. If I use the wrong direction, I risk compromising the linearization of Euclidean distance constraints. It might be better to conduct a line search with Euclidean verification afterward. \n\nI can keep SCP-LP linearized constraints valid but adapt the selection of active pairs or weights using p-norm slacks, while still prioritizing the maximization of our objective. The proposal involves \"Weighted SCP-LP with Lp-guided weights,\" and I'll prepare for an experiment while ensuring the code is manageable and efficient for execution.**Implementing the code**\n\nI'm ready\
  \ to start coding. First, I’ll define a function to build linearized matrices focusing on active pairs only. The objective will be weighted, so I’ll use normalized weights.\n\nI’ll create an LP solver with specific methods and set it to use exact zero-slack radii via linear programming. The iteration will go up to 20 times, computing necessary pairs based on p-slack and optimizing weights for circles. Any improvements will determine whether I need to adjust the trust region.\n\nI’ll ensure to log minimally and finalize the radii correctly, saving the best results in the designated storage. Let's focus on implementing this effectively!**Preparing the experiment**\n\nI'm considering the time complexity for this experiment. Each seed will run about 20 outer loops, involving one LP for the step and approximately five LPs for the radii, which include around 500 pairs plus boundary constraints. This seems manageable within the 20-minute limit.\n\nI’ll use only the best and jitter seeds, and\
  \ for my message, I'll start with my identity and location. I’ll also acknowledge the inactivity notice and submit the experiment while keeping logs concise to avoid excessive token use. Let’s get this rolling!"
