tick: 456
role: model
parts:
- text: "Newton system (implementable)\n\nTwo practical formulations; start with a convex QP (Option A), optionally add curvature (Option B) if robust multipliers and LDLT become available.\n\nNotation\n- z = [x_1..x_N, y_1..y_N, r_1..r_N] ∈ R^{3N}.\n- Active equalities A(z)=0 collect E∪B∪G^+; their Jacobian at (C,r) is J := J_active ∈ R^{m×3N}.\n- Objective gradient g := ∇_z(−∑ r_i) = [0_{2N}, −1_N].\n- Trust-region radii: τ_c for centers; τ_r for radii.\n- We assume finalize has enforced A(z)≈0 within machine tolerance.\n\nOption A: Equality-constrained convex QP (null-space ascent with regularization)\n- Solve for Δz that stays in the linearized manifold and maximizes ∑ Δr, while regularizing movement:\n  min_{Δz}  0.5 ||W Δz||^2 − e_r^T Δz\n  s.t.      J Δz = −A(z)   (typically ≈0)\n  where W = diag(w_c I_{2N}, w_r I_N), with w_c ≈ 1/τ_c, w_r ≈ 1/τ_r, and e_r = [0_{2N}, 1_N].\n- KKT system (saddle-point; symmetric indefinite):\n  [ W^T W   J^T ] [Δz] = [ e_r ]\n  [   J      0  ] [ν ]\
    \   [ −A(z) ]\n  Add small Tikhonov ηI on the (1,1)-block if needed: W^T W + ηI, η∈[1e−9,1e−6].\n- Deterministic tie-breaking: add tiny index-weight to e_r if needed (e.g., e_r[i_r] += 1e−12(i+1)).\n\nProperties\n- If rank(J)=3N (rigid), feasible tangent is trivial; Δz≈0 unless A(z) has nonzero residual; triggers active-set pivot (see §5).\n- If dim null(J)>0, Δz follows the projected ascent in the null-space with movement penalized by W.\n\nOption B: Curvature-augmented (Active-Set Newton)\n- Approximate Lagrangian Hessian H (nonzero only on centers; radii curvature is zero):\n  For each contact (i,j)∈E with multiplier λ_{ij} ≥ 0 and d_ij>0:\n    S_ij := (I_2 − u_ij u_ij^T)/d_ij\n    H[ci,ci] += λ_{ij} S_ij\n    H[cj,cj] += λ_{ij} S_ij\n    H[ci,cj] −= λ_{ij} S_ij\n    H[cj,ci] −= λ_{ij} S_ij\n  Boundary terms linear → no curvature; cross blocks with radii are zero.\n- Require multipliers at the current iterate. Practical sources:\n  1) Duals from the exact radii LP (preferred, if exposed\
    \ by the solver).\n  2) Minimal-norm nonnegative least squares fit to stationarity (per-circle equations).\n- Newton/KKT system:\n  [ H+ηI   J^T ] [Δz] = −[ ∇_z L(z,λ,μ,ρ) ]\n  [  J      0  ] [Δπ]    [     A(z)       ]\n  with damping η>0 (Levenberg–Marquardt); solve via sparse LDL^T.\n- Start with Option A; add Option B only when robust λ are available and KKT linear algebra is stable.\n\nTrust region and line search (globalization)\n\n- Compute Δz from Option A (or B). Clip to box trust region:\n  Δx_i,Δy_i := clip(Δx_i, −τ_c, τ_c), clip(Δy_i, −τ_c, τ_c); Δr_i := clip(Δr_i, −τ_r, τ_r).\n- Backtracking on α ∈ {1, 0.7, 0.5, 0.35, 0.2, 0.12, 0.08}:\n  For each α:\n    z_trial := z + α Δz; enforce in-box for centers [0,1].\n    r_exact, ok := exact zero-slack radii LP at C_trial (SciPy HiGHS); if !ok, continue.\n    r_fin := zero-tolerance finalize(C_trial, r_exact)  // iterative, float-safe pair clamp + global nudge\n    gain := ∑ r_fin − ∑ r\n    If gain ≥ ε_accept (e.g., 1e−9 in refinement),\
    \ accept step; else try smaller α.\n- If all α fail:\n  - Reduce τ_c, τ_r ← 0.6× and/or pivot active set (see §5).\n  - If still failing, fall back to SCP‑LP iteration or terminate refiner for this seed.\n\nStationarity residual and merit function\n- Merit for acceptance is exact objective S(C) = ∑ r_exact(C) after finalize, not linearized.\n- Optional Armijo slope check: ensure gain ≥ σ α g^T Δz with σ∈[1e−3,1e−2] to discourage zig‑zag (optional since objective is re-solved exactly each trial).\n\nNumerical notes\n- Scale rows of J by constraint type to improve conditioning (pairs vs boundaries).\n- Precondition KKT with block Jacobi or use normal equations on the constraint subspace:\n  Solve (J (W^T W)^{-1} J^T) ν = rhs, then back-solve Δz = (W^T W)^{-1}(e_r − J^T ν).\n- Determinism: fix ordering of E and B; fixed η, τ schedules; fixed α list; stable linear algebra settings."
thinking_content: "**Continuing reflection ticks**\n\nI understand we need to keep moving forward with the reflection ticks. The user mentioned \"Reflection Tick 2 / 5,\" so I’ll build on that by discussing the details of the Newton system. This includes focusing on the unknowns, block structure, and regularization. I might want to derive the linearized KKT system as well. Then, I’ll touch on globalization, incorporating trust region and Armijo line search with exact zero-slack radii LP objectives, along with step acceptance and updates.**Detailing Newton system**\n\nSince we're on tick 2 out of 5, I'll focus on the Newton system. I can describe how to build this system by linearizing the constraints h(z) = 0, considering stationarity with gradients for both the objective and constraints. Since the objective is -sum r, the gradient will be constant. The KKT linear system will simplify to a zero-constrained quadratic programming step, which involves minimizing the weighted norm subject to\
  \ equality constraints. I’ll approach it from the angle of maximizing the sum while addressing inactive inequalities and Lagrange multipliers.**Exploring QP solutions**\n\nI'm considering another approach for solving the equality-constrained quadratic programming (QP). The goal is to maximize the sum of Δr while incorporating a quadratic term for Δz, all subject to the constraint A Δz = 0. The Hessian of the Lagrangian becomes indefinite due to the pairwise distance. For the Newton step on KKT conditions, I'll include stationarity conditions along with linearized constraints but be aware of the curvature from the Lagrangian gradient. This might involve a full Newton step or a Gauss-Newton style, where we simplify by ignoring curvature to reduce it to an equality-constrained LP step.**Focusing on KKT active-set Newton**\n\nI need to look at the KKT active-set Newton method with a block structure, focusing on the variables Δz and multipliers Δν. The equations involve a linearized complementarity\
  \ condition. For standard primal-dual Newton approaches with inequality constraints, we typically consider slack variables and complementarity conditions. However, in this case, since we're keeping the active set as equalities, I won't include complementarity in the linearized system. Instead, I'll focus exclusively on solving the equality constraints while ensuring stationarity.**Constructing the Newton system**\n\nI'm laying out the Newton system for the KKT approach that includes two variables: Δz and Δν. The equations will utilize the linearized complementarity condition while focusing primarily on equality constraints. The Hessian of the Lagrangian is defined with respect to the active set, and we'll compute λ from stationarity conditions with each iterate. I have a couple of options here: one is to solve an equality-constrained QP step while ignoring the Hessian, and the other is to consider a minimal Tikhonov regularization.**Designing the Newton system and globalization**\n\nI’m\
  \ establishing the Newton system for solving saddle points, especially focusing on the linear system involving QP. I first define the active sets and build the Jacobian for the current context. For the method, I have two options. \n\nOption A involves maximizing Δr under active constraints while solving a small saddle system. Option B incorporates curvature using an approximate Lagrangian Hessian but may add complexity. \n\nThen, once I compute Δz, I generate candidate values for C and r with a backtracking line search, ensuring active-set updates for acceptance. It's essential to monitor constraint slacks and maintain determinism in the design. Finally, I need to consider diagnostics and risks linked to the system, which allows me to refine the process further."
