  \ a new tick and have completed reflection, we can act. The system has issued a critical notice about inactivity, so it’s time to implement a minimal KKT Option A refiner code that can be integrated with the finalization and LP routines.**Implementing Python script**\n\nI need to provide a complete Python script for run.py, starting with the implementation of construct_packing. I’m thinking we can create a straightforward call flow where we load persisted best centers from storage as a seed, then apply 5 iterations of KKT Option A with trust region and backtracking while logging progress. It’s essential to meet a time limit of 20 minutes for determinism. \n\nI wonder if it's okay to embed everything in run.py for safety? This should help me avoid dependency issues with imports from storage. I’ve previously used code to compute exact radii with SciPy, so I can leverage that for building an active set and solve Option A via normal equations. It’s a big task, but I believe I can do it!**Crafting\
  \ robust experiment code**\n\nI need to be cautious about token limits and ensure we produce robust code. Currently, we have 72k tokens used out of 270k, which is manageable. Since we received an inactivity notice, we should submit at least one experiment. My plan is to implement the \"KKT-Option A small refiner,\" limiting it to a maximum of 8 iterations and using a small trust region. If there's no improvement, I’ll fallback on returning the baseline. \n\nI need to strictly follow the Research Task specifications, ensuring the experiment returns 32 circle centers and radii within the unit square. I’ll also make sure there’s zero slack, using a margin tweak from earlier if required. Oh, and I must remember to use SciPy’s linprog; if it’s unavailable, I’ve got to incorporate a fallback mechanism!**Constructing the experiment code**\n\nI need to implement a fallback for radii bounding with a repair mechanism while keeping the computation time under 20 minutes. The algorithm should run efficiently,\
  \ and I'll print logs to STDOUT for diagnostics. I plan to persist results in \"storage/verity,\" but only if the sum of radii is above a limit, and to avoid overwriting, I’ll write to a separate filename. \n\nI’ll outline core components, from building active sets to finalizing zero slack in the exact radii LP while adhering to constraints. Each step will involve careful adjustments, ensuring the algorithm runs smoothly. I’ll keep the logging and storing of final results in mind and build my code accordingly, ensuring no reliance on external imports. It’s a complex plan, but I’m ready to put it into action!**Implementing the function in YAML**\n\nI need to follow YAML conventions and ensure that the content section holds the Python code. First, I’ll code the `pair_indices` function and load the seed from \"storage/verity/best_centers.npy\" or fallback to a 6x6 grid minus corners. \n\nFor exact LP, I’ll add boundary constraints as before. I’ll import SciPy’s `linprog` for optimization.\
  \ Then I'll define slack variables for various dimensions and build the E and B lists based on slacks. \n\nThe next step is to create the J and A arrays, set up the necessary calculations, keep an eye on regularization, and ensure all components are working together smoothly. My acceptance threshold will stay at 1e-9 for safety, and I’ll integrate everything into the final code!"
