role: model
parts:
- text: "Identity: Verity II (Recursive Agent)\nLocation: Research Counter\n\nRecap: Read Archive #17 (hex initialization baseline). It’s solid and reproducible; reviewer suggests testing hex seeding’s transfer to MM‑LP. I’ll add a deterministic hex seeder to lineage storage and log a minimal, trigger‑gated ablation plan in Private Memory (no run now).\n\n/execute_action{storage write verity/seeders.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n\n  N = 32\n\n  def hex_rows_32(margin: float = 0.06, jitter: float = 0.0, seed: int = 2029) -> np.ndarray:\n      \"\"\"\n      Deterministic hex-row seeding for N=32 within [0,1]^2.\n      Pattern totals 32: [6, 5, 6, 5, 6, 4]. Rows are evenly spaced in y.\n      Smaller rows are offset by half the x step to emulate a hex lattice.\n      Optionally apply tiny deterministic jitter for symmetry breaking.\n      Returns:\n          centers: (32, 2) ndarray\n      \"\"\"\n      rows = [6, 5, 6, 5, 6, 4]\n      H = len(rows)\n      lo, hi = margin,\
    \ 1.0 - margin\n      ys = np.linspace(lo, hi, H)\n      maxk = max(rows)\n      if maxk > 1:\n          xs_full = np.linspace(lo, hi, maxk)\n          dx = xs_full[1] - xs_full[0]\n      else:\n          xs_full = np.array([0.5 * (lo + hi)])\n          dx = 0.0\n\n      centers = []\n      for j, k in enumerate(rows):\n          if k == maxk:\n              xs = xs_full\n          else:\n              xs = np.linspace(lo + 0.5 * dx, hi - 0.5 * dx, k)\n          y = ys[j]\n          for x in xs:\n              centers.append([x, y])\n\n      C = np.array(centers, dtype=float)\n      assert C.shape == (N, 2), f\"Expected (32,2), got {C.shape}\"\n\n      if jitter and jitter > 0.0:\n          rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\n          J = rng.uniform(-jitter, jitter, size=C.shape)\n          C = np.clip(C + J, 0.0, 1.0)\n      return C\n\n  def hex_rows_generic(n: int, margin: float = 0.06) -> np.ndarray:\n      \"\"\"\n      Generic hex-row generator for arbitrary n (best-effort fill).\n\
    \      Fills rows alternating between K and K-1 until reaching n.\n      Deterministic; useful for quick ablations outside N=32.\n      \"\"\"\n      if n <= 0:\n          return np.zeros((0, 2), dtype=float)\n      lo, hi = margin, 1.0 - margin\n      # Heuristic: choose K so K*(K-1) ~ n (alternating K, K-1 rows)\n      K = max(2, int(np.floor(np.sqrt(n)) + 1))\n      rows = []\n      total = 0\n      toggle = True\n      while total < n:\n          k = K if toggle else max(2, K - 1)\n          if total + k > n:\n              k = n - total\n          rows.append(k)\n          total += k\n          toggle = not toggle\n\n      H = len(rows)\n      ys = np.linspace(lo, hi, H)\n      maxk = max(rows)\n      xs_full = np.linspace(lo, hi, maxk) if maxk > 1 else np.array([0.5 * (lo + hi)])\n\n      centers = []\n      if maxk > 1:\n          dx = xs_full[1] - xs_full[0]\n      else:\n          dx = 0.0\n      for j, k in enumerate(rows):\n          xs = xs_full if k == maxk else np.linspace(lo\
    \ + 0.5 * dx, hi - 0.5 * dx, k)\n          y = ys[j]\n          for x in xs:\n              centers.append([x, y])\n\n      C = np.array(centers, dtype=float)\n      assert C.shape == (n, 2), f\"Expected ({n},2), got {C.shape}\"\n      return C\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{reply 12}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Hex seeding → MM‑LP transfer (plan)\n  Context: Read Archive #17 (Prometheus I). Hexagonal initialization + SLSQP gives a robust baseline (2.9199839187809937). Reviewer suggests testing whether hex init benefits stronger backbones like MM‑LP.\n  What I did:\n  - Added deterministic hex seeder to storage: storage/verity/seeders.py (hex_rows_32, optional jitter).\n  Proposed ablation (trigger‑gated; not running yet):\n  - Integrate hex_rows_32 as an extra seed family in Stage‑A prospecting.\n  - Minimal budget probe: N_PROSPECT_HEX=128 (mix of hex and tiny jitter), REFINE_TOP=8, REFINE_ITERS=10; accept only if sum ≥ current persisted best.\n  - Keep zero‑slack\
    \ SCP‑LP + exact radii LP + iterative zero‑tolerance finalize; storage guard prevents degradation.\n  - If any improvement, scale to full Stage‑A/Stage‑B in a follow‑up; otherwise document as negative result.\n  Rationale: Low‑cost check of cross‑method seeding transfer without heavy runs; maintains reproducibility and safety.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto research}"
