    \ hybrid not consistently helpful.\n  * HRG feasible‑path: SOTA seed locally rigid; jitter path plateaus < SOTA.\n- Next steps:\n  * Maintain SOTA and code; monitor for leaderboard changes.\n  * If robust KKT/active‑set solver tooling appears (e.g., reliable constrained Newton/SQP with line search), test KKT active‑set Newton with strict finalize (no “exact SOCP” claim for the joint centers+radii problem).\n  * Otherwise avoid redundant runs; continue documenting negative results and maintenance.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 79,612 / 270,000 used (29%)\n- Agent Age: 208 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Preview for Capsule #1: LP-guided Active-Set Methods for 32-Circle Packing in the Unit Square — Verified Results with Evaluation IDs**\nAuthor: Verity I, Created at Tick: 53\nAbstract: We report a reproducible 32-circle packing achieving sum of radii 2.9292366. Centers are optimized via MM-LP trust-region steps, LP-dual–informed active-set refinements,\
    \ and targeted relocations; radii are always finalized exactly via LP (HiGHS). This paper lists all key experiments with Evaluation IDs and scores to satisfy verifiability.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #3: Paper: Characterizing a Stable 2.866 Local Optimum in Gradient-Based Circle Packing**\nAuthor: Cognito I, Created at Tick: 69\nAbstract: This paper provides a rigorous, evidence-based characterization of a highly stable local optimum in the 32-circle packing problem, yielding a score of 2.8658500. We demonstrate that a specific methodology—combining a 4x8 grid initialization with the Adam optimizer in a JAX framework (Evaluation ID 43)—robustly converges to this point. We present verifiable evidence showing that numerous sophisticated initializers, including global search heuristics, converge to this same optimum. Furthermore, we demonstrate that substituting the Adam optimizer with SGD results in complete failure, proving the entire methodological pipeline is critical. This work\
    \ establishes a well-defined, reproducible benchmark and offers a detailed portrait of a significant attractor basin that traps general-purpose optimizers.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #4: Achieving New State-of-the-Art in 32-Circle Packing: A Two-Stage Multi-Start SLSQP with Enhanced Hybrid Seeding**\nAuthor: Aether I, Created at Tick: 90\nAbstract: This paper presents the development and validation of a Two-Stage Multi-Start Sequential Least Squares Programming (SLSQP) algorithm that has achieved a new State-of-the-Art score of 2.9316130 for the 32-circle packing problem in a unit square. The methodology combines diverse initial seeding strategies, including Praxis-style perturbed grids and Verity I's robust row-based and farthest-point samplers, with a high-precision SLSQP refinement stage. Key verifiable evaluation IDs demonstrate the iterative improvements leading to this breakthrough.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #5: A Foundational Multi-Start SLSQP Method for the 32-Circle\
    \ Packing Problem**\nAuthor: Praxis I, Created at Tick: 98\nAbstract: This paper presents and validates a robust multi-start constrained optimization method for the 32-circle packing problem. The algorithm utilizes `scipy.optimize.SLSQP` seeded with randomly perturbed grid initializations, consistently achieving a score of 2.9155. The paper provides a detailed methodology and presents a full set of five verifiable experiments, including baselines and ablation studies, to support its efficacy as a powerful and reproducible foundational approach.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #6: Research Paper: Achieving Robust, Near-SOTA 32-Circle Packing with a Two-Stage Hybrid Optimization (Parallelization-Enhanced)**\nAuthor: Quest I, Created at Tick: 119\nAbstract: This paper presents a robust and high-performing solution for the 32-circle packing problem in a unit square, aiming to maximize the sum of circle radii while ensuring non-overlap and boundary adherence. The developed methodology employs\
    \ a two-stage hybrid optimization: Stage 1 uses multi-start hill climbing with diverse seeding strategies (perturbed grid, row pattern, farthest-point sampling) for global exploration. Stage 2 refines the top candidates using gradient-based SLSQP, leveraging LP-exact radii finalization and a robust persistence policy with strict slack criteria. This work emphasizes the critical role of collaborative insights, numerical stability, and artifact persistence in complex optimization tasks.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #7: A Scalable Multi-Start SLSQP Method for Circle Packing via Parallelization**\nAuthor: Praxis II, Created at Tick: 178\nAbstract: This paper presents a robust and scalable method for the 32-circle packing problem, achieving a verifiable score of 2.9281238630529645 (Evaluation ID 320). The core of the method is a two-stage, multi-start Sequential Least Squares Programming (SLSQP) algorithm. We demonstrate that the performance of this method is significantly enhanced by\
    \ parallelizing the initial multi-start search phase using Python's multiprocessing library. An ablation study is presented, supported by verifiable Evaluation IDs, which shows a clear correlation between the number of parallel starts and the quality of the final solution.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #8: A Scalable, Massively Parallel SLSQP Method for the 32-Circle Packing Problem**\nAuthor: Cognito II, Created at Tick: 204\nAbstract: This paper presents a detailed experimental study of a scalable, massively parallel methodology for the 32-circle packing problem. We systematically investigate the relationship between computational scale (the number of parallel optimization runs) and solution quality, providing a series of verifiable experiments that demonstrate clear performance improvements as the number of parallel starts increases, culminating in a near-SOTA score of 2.9299927174063978. We present this method as a rigorous characterization of a powerful, scalable baseline for\
    \ this class of optimization problem.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #9: Orthogonal Split Motif (OSM) for 32‑Circle Packing: Methodology and Rigorous Evaluation**\nAuthor: Verity I, Created at Tick: 227\nAbstract: We define a single deterministic local move—the Orthogonal Split Motif (OSM)—and evaluate it under exact LP (HiGHS) gating with strict feasibility thresholds. We compare against deterministic baselines on two initializations (grid 6×6−corners and a strong artifact). OSM does not improve the objective under these criteria. We provide a clear algorithm description and report all results with evaluation IDs and full precision.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #10: Paper: An Adaptive Parallel Search Method for Discovering a New State-of-the-Art in Circle Packing**\nAuthor: Cognito III, Created at Tick: 292\nAbstract: This paper introduces the Adaptive Parallel Search (APS) algorithm, a two-stage meta-heuristic that achieved a new state-of-the-art score of 2.93652623708504 on the\
    \ 32-circle packing problem. The method allocates computational resources by first performing a broad, shallow 'prospecting' search to identify promising solution basins, followed by a deep, focused 'refining' search. An extensive, verifiable ablation study is presented, which demonstrates that the method's success is critically dependent on the prospecting stage's configuration. Subsequent experiments testing alternative seeding strategies and synthesis with other methodologies are also documented, all of which resulted in performance regressions, reinforcing the stability of the discovered optimum. This work establishes a new benchmark for the station and presents a robust, reproducible methodology for achieving it.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #11: Achieving New State-of-the-Art: Parallel Two-Stage Adaptive Search for 32-Circle Packing (Revised)**\nAuthor: Aether I, Created at Tick: 293\nAbstract: This revised paper presents the development and validation of a novel parallel two-stage\
    \ adaptive search architecture that has achieved a new State-of-the-Art (SOTA) score of 2.9365262370770777 for the 32-circle packing problem. The methodology leverages `multiprocessing.Pool` for massive initial exploration (1024 prospects with hybrid seeding), followed by a refined optimization of 32 elite candidates, enhanced by Localized Random Walk (LRW) perturbation. This architecture efficiently navigates the complex objective landscape, demonstrating a significant advancement in robust and high-performing solutions. An ablation study quantifies the contribution of the LRW perturbation.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #12: The Localized Random Walk Perturbation Method for Multi-Start Optimization (Revised)**\nAuthor: Praxis IV, Created at Tick: 339\nAbstract: Multi-start optimization methods are highly sensitive to the quality of their initial configurations. This paper introduces a novel, stable, and computationally inexpensive method for improving high-quality initial seeds: the\
    \ Localized Random Walk (LRW) perturbation. We demonstrate that a gentle, controlled \"jiggle\" applied to a strong initial configuration from Farthest-Point Sampling can nudge the solution into a superior basin of attraction. The development of this method was motivated by the failure of a more complex, unstable physics-based generator, leading us to favor a simpler, more robust approach. With careful tuning, the LRW perturbation led to a new state-of-the-art score of 2.9344937302385263, surpassing the established baseline of 2.9281238630529645. This paper presents the LRW method, a rigorous ablation study of its hyperparameters, and discusses its potential as a general-purpose enhancement for multi-start optimization pipelines.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #13: Zero‑Slack MM‑LP Adaptive Multi‑Start for 32‑Circle Packing: 2.939572768266679, Explicit LP Formulations, Ablations, and Local Rigidity**\nAuthor: Verity II, Created at Tick: 351\nAbstract: We present a fully specified,\
    \ deterministic zero‑slack LP framework for 32‑circle packing in the unit square and an MM‑LP adaptive multi‑start search that achieves 2.939572768266679. We define the exact radii LP, the linearized center‑update LP (SCP‑LP), and the complete two‑stage search (prospecting + refinement). We also formalize a feasible‑path homotopy (HRG) as a minimal‑move feasibility LP and report controlled ablations: slack mode × finalize policy, active‑constraint K‑sweep, selection rules (slack vs dual), and hybrid selection. Integrated tables (with IDs and numerical values) show that zero‑slack modeling is consistently superior, K≥3N matches all‑pairs, dual‑guided selection is brittle, and the SOTA configuration is locally rigid for first‑order feasible growth.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #15: Research Paper: Replication and Analysis of MM-LP Adaptive Search for 32-Circle Packing (SOTA Score: 2.939572768266756)**\nAuthor: Cogitator I, Created at Tick: 370\nAbstract: This paper presents a comprehensive\
    \ replication and in-depth analysis of the current State-of-the-Art (SOTA) MM-LP Adaptive Search method for the 32-Circle Packing in Unit Square problem. We successfully replicated the SOTA score of 2.939572768266756 and conducted six verifiable experiments, including a heuristic baseline, SOTA replication, and three controlled ablation studies on key parameters: `N_PROSPECT`, `DELTA0_VAL`, and iteration distribution (`PROSPECT_ITER` vs. `REFINE_ITER`). The methodology provides an explicit mathematical formulation of the center-update Linear Program (SCP-LP). Our findings validate the robustness of the MM-LP approach, highlight the critical role of `N_PROSPECT`, demonstrate the minimal impact of `DELTA0_VAL` within a tested range, and reveal a significant trade-off in iteration distribution, where different balances can yield identical SOTA results. This work contributes to the station's collective knowledge by providing a detailed, reproducible analysis of a leading optimization strategy.\n\
    \n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #14: An MM-LP Adaptive Search Achieving 2.939572768266756 and Analysis of its Local Rigidity**\nAuthor: Praxis IV, Created at Tick: 370\nAbstract: This paper presents a novel 'MM-LP Adaptive Search' architecture that achieves a new state-of-the-art score of 2.939572768266756 for the 32-circle packing problem. The method replaces the SLSQP optimizer in the SOTA 'Adaptive Search' framework with a Verity I-style MM-LP algorithm, demonstrating its superior performance in this context. We then present a comprehensive series of post-SOTA experiments, including the implementation of a second-order Active-Set Newton (ASN) method and a non-local 'Tightest Pair Rotation' search. The consistent failure of these advanced techniques to improve upon the new SOTA provides strong evidence that this solution represents a deep and exceptionally stable local optimum, highly resistant to the specific second-order and non-local methods tested.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule\
    \ #16: The Impact of Initial Seeding Strategy on the Performance of a Two-Stage MM-LP Optimizer**\nAuthor: Socrates I, Created at Tick: 392\nAbstract: This paper investigates the critical role of the initial seeding strategy within multi-start optimization architectures for the 32-circle packing problem. We present a rigorous and verifiable six-part ablation study that compares the performance of three distinct seeding methodologies—Perturbed Grid (Grid), Farthest-Point Sampling (FPS), and a Hybrid of the two—across two different optimization backbones: a foundational SLSQP optimizer and a state-of-the-art MM-LP architecture. Our results demonstrate that Farthest-Point Sampling is a significantly more effective strategy than the perturbed grid, and that its inclusion is critical for achieving state-of-the-art scores. The study provides strong evidence for the generalizability of this finding and offers a clear recommendation for prioritizing spatially diverse seeding methods in this\
    \ problem domain.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #17: A Robust Baseline for Circle Packing via Hexagonal Initialization**\nAuthor: Prometheus I, Created at Tick: 462\nAbstract: This paper presents a robust baseline method for the circle packing problem (N=32), achieving a stable score of 2.9199839187809937. The core of the method is a simultaneous, gradient-based optimization of all circle variables using the SLSQP algorithm. The key to its success is the use of a hexagonal grid for initializing the circle centers, which consistently guides the optimizer to a deep local optimum. The paper provides a self-contained algorithm for this initialization and reports exact numerical results for a comprehensive suite of experiments, including alternative methods that were less effective. While more advanced methods have since surpassed this score, this paper documents a foundational approach and its optimization landscape, serving as a valuable, reproducible benchmark for future research.\n\
    \n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #18: Deconstructing the SOTA: An Analytical Study of the MM-LP Adaptive Search**\nAuthor: Praxis V, Created at Tick: 474\nAbstract: This paper presents a comprehensive deconstruction and analysis of the state-of-the-art MM-LP Adaptive Search algorithm for the n=32 circle packing problem. We first establish a minimal, yet perfectly SOTA-replicating, baseline implementation. Using this validated baseline, we conduct a series of three rigorous ablation studies investigating the impact of seeding strategy, trust region scheduling, and computational budget allocation. Our results demonstrate conclusively that neither the SOTA's hybrid seeding strategy nor its adaptive trust region schedule are necessary for achieving the SOTA score; pure Farthest-Point Sampling and a fixed trust region are sufficient. Furthermore, a novel synthesis experiment, which combined the SOTA's MM-LP optimizer with a superior hexagonal grid seeder from another lineage, resulted in\
    \ a significant performance regression. This final negative result reveals a critical and previously undocumented synergy between the Farthest-Point Sampling method and the MM-LP optimization engine. Together, these findings simplify the SOTA architecture to its essential components and provide a deeper theoretical understanding of the key factors driving its success.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #19: On Seeding and Search Architecture: A Study of SLSQP-based Circle Packing**\nAuthor: Prometheus I, Created at Tick: 498\nAbstract: This paper presents a rigorous experimental study of SLSQP-based optimizers for the n=32 circle packing problem, achieving a new personal SOTA of 2.9331613959581295. We conduct a series of five controlled experiments to deconstruct the performance of a multi-start SLSQP engine. Our results demonstrate two key findings: first, a simple, single-stage multi-start architecture is as effective as a more complex two-stage (Prospecting/Refining) framework when\
    \ given an equivalent computational budget. Second, Farthest-Point Sampling (FPS) is a demonstrably superior seeding strategy to a Hexagonal Grid for this architecture, yielding a ~0.013 improvement in the final score. The paper also provides a self-contained definition of the Affine Warp Search method and confirms that the ~2.933 optimum is robust against such deformations. This work clarifies the essential components for a high-performing, non-LP-based solver.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Research Counter);\n2. `preview all` (in Archive Room);\n3. `goto research` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Preview command processed for all capsules. Preview(s) sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 2)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n\
    | 19 | On Seeding and Search Architecture: A Study of SLSQP-based Circle Packing | Prometheus I | Tick 498 | 666 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 18 | Deconstructing the SOTA: An Analytical Study of the MM-LP Adaptive Search | Praxis V | Tick 474 | 1122 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 17 | A Robust Baseline for Circle Packing via Hexagonal Initialization | Prometheus I | Tick 462 | 650 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 16 | The Impact of Initial Seeding Strategy on the Performance of a Two-Stage MM-LP Optimizer | Socrates I | Tick 392 | 698 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 15 | Research Paper: Replication and Analysis of MM-LP Adaptive Search for 32-Circle Packing (SOTA Score: 2.939572768266756) | Cogitator I | Tick 370 | 2840 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 14 | An MM-LP Adaptive Search Achieving 2.939572768266756 and Analysis of its Local Rigidity | Praxis IV | Tick 370 | 1349 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 13 | Zero‑Slack MM‑LP Adaptive Multi‑Start for 32‑Circle Packing: 2.939572768266679, Explicit LP Formulations, Ablations, and Local Rigidity\
    \ | Verity II | Tick 351 | 1961 | 8 | (All Read) |\n| 12 | The Localized Random Walk Perturbation Method for Multi-Start Optimization (Revised) | Praxis IV | Tick 339 | 851 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 11 | Achieving New State-of-the-Art: Parallel Two-Stage Adaptive Search for 32-Circle Packing (Revised) | Aether I | Tick 293 | 1172 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 10 | Paper: An Adaptive Parallel Search Method for Discovering a New State-of-the-Art in Circle Packing | Cognito III | Tick 292 | 793 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-2.)\n\n### Location: Research Counter\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Research Counter.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Research Tasks:**\n\n| ID | Task Name |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Circle Packing in Unit Square |\n\n**Running Experiments:**\n\nYou do not have any running experiments. Please submit a new experiment if needed.\n\n**Submitted Evaluations:**\n\n| Evaluation ID | Research Task ID | Title | Author | Submitted Tick\
    \ | Score |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 653 | 1 | Lex-SCP‑LP: two-pass LP (maximize radii, then minimize movement) + exact zero‑slack radii with st... | Verity II | 404 | 2.9395728 |\n| 624 | 1 | ASN small free‑set nullspace step + robust zero‑tolerance finalize (iterative; float‑safe) | Verity II | 375 | 2.9395728 |\n| 636 | 1 | Global affine micro‑warp search + exact zero‑slack radii (iterative zero‑tol finalize) | Verity II | 386 | 2.9395728 |\n| 640 | 1 | Contact‑graph and boundary‑slack auditor (full‑precision diagnostics; returns baseline) | Verity II | 391 | 2.9395728 |\n| 643 | 1 | Cycle Surgery v2: strict-safe finalize (eps pair fudge) + same pilot (deterministic) | Verity II | 395 | 2.9395728 |\n| 645 | 1 | Min-cut Free-set Newton: small free-set outward push with equality preservation (strict finalize;... | Verity II | 397 | 2.9395728 |\n| 597 | 1 | MMS‑LP v2: iterative zero‑tolerance finalize (multi‑pass) + max‑min slack reorg with exact zero‑s... | Verity II | 358 | 2.9395728\
    \ |\n| 609 | 1 | DCPP: Deterministic Contact‑Pair Pivot rotation + exact zero‑slack radii (line search; storage‑se... | Verity II | 364 | 2.9395728 |\n| 598 | 1 | Non-Local Search v2: Parameterized Pair Rotation | Praxis IV | 359 | 2.9395728 |\n| 617 | 1 | Hybrid Non-Local Search: Adaptive Jiggle & Refine | Praxis IV | 374 | 2.9395728 |\n\nPage 1 of 81 (sorted by: score)\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate pages 1-81, `/execute_action{rank mode}` to change sort order, `/execute_action{page_size N}` to set page size, `/execute_action{filter tag}` to filter by tag.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Research Counter\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help research}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for\
