    \ hybrid not consistently helpful.\n  * HRG feasible‑path: SOTA seed locally rigid; jitter path plateaus < SOTA.\n- Next steps:\n  * Maintain SOTA and code; monitor for leaderboard changes.\n  * If robust KKT/active‑set solver tooling appears (e.g., reliable constrained Newton/SQP with line search), test KKT active‑set Newton with strict finalize (no “exact SOCP” claim for the joint centers+radii problem).\n  * Otherwise avoid redundant runs; continue documenting negative results and maintenance.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 86,671 / 270,000 used (32%)\n- Agent Age: 214 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour archive submission 'Local Rigidity Audit at 2.939572768266679 for 32‑Circle Packing: LSS, Equality‑Constrained SCP‑LP, Near‑Contact Pivots, and Scalarized Steps' was not accepted for publication. Score: 1/10 (minimum required: 6).\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nStrong Reject. This paper is unpublishable due to a catastrophic failure in clarity.\
    \ While the underlying experimental work appears extensive and rigorous, the document is presented as a dense, unstructured data dump rather than a readable scientific paper. Its unconventional format, chaotic results section, and excessive, undefined jargon make it nearly impossible to understand. New methods are introduced in the results section without prior definition. A research paper's primary function is to communicate findings clearly, and this submission completely fails in that regard. The valuable research contained within is rendered inaccessible by its presentation.\n\n**Suggestions for Improvement:**\nYou must perform a complete rewrite of this paper. The content is valuable, but it needs to be presented in a standard scientific format. Structure the paper with clear sections: Introduction, Methodology, Results, and Conclusion. In the Methodology section, you must clearly define *every* technique you test *before* presenting its results. The Results section must be organized\
    \ logically, perhaps with a subsection for each method, clearly linking the experimental outcomes (with IDs) to the technique being evaluated. Define your technical terms and aim for a presentation that allows another expert in the field to understand your work without having to reverse-engineer your thought process.\n\n**Tip:** You can submit a revised version through the Archive Room again after making the necessary changes. Rejected submissions do not activate the Archive Room cooldown, meaning you can resubmit immediately. Focus on addressing the key concerns mentioned in the reviewer feedback to improve your chances of acceptance. You may need to perform additional experiments or reflect on your research methodology based on the feedback provided. \n**If you are low on tokens, go to the Token Management Room to restore your token budget. Low tokens are not an excuse for poor-quality submissions, such as reframing work without conducting follow-up experiments.**\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions\
    \ Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Research Counter);\n2. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, tags, abstract, content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Your archive submission 'Local Rigidity Audit at 2.939572768266679 for 32‑Circle Packing: LSS, Equality‑Constrained SCP‑LP, Near‑Contact Pivots, and Scalarized Steps' has been queued for evaluation by the review system. You will be notified of the results. The submission will be published if it meets the publication standards (score >= 6/10).\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 2)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 19 | On Seeding and Search Architecture: A Study of SLSQP-based Circle Packing | Prometheus I | Tick 498 | 666 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 18 | Deconstructing\
    \ the SOTA: An Analytical Study of the MM-LP Adaptive Search | Praxis V | Tick 474 | 1122 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 17 | A Robust Baseline for Circle Packing via Hexagonal Initialization | Prometheus I | Tick 462 | 650 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 16 | The Impact of Initial Seeding Strategy on the Performance of a Two-Stage MM-LP Optimizer | Socrates I | Tick 392 | 698 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 15 | Research Paper: Replication and Analysis of MM-LP Adaptive Search for 32-Circle Packing (SOTA Score: 2.939572768266756) | Cogitator I | Tick 370 | 2840 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 14 | An MM-LP Adaptive Search Achieving 2.939572768266756 and Analysis of its Local Rigidity | Praxis IV | Tick 370 | 1349 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 13 | Zero‑Slack MM‑LP Adaptive Multi‑Start for 32‑Circle Packing: 2.939572768266679, Explicit LP Formulations, Ablations, and Local Rigidity | Verity II | Tick 351 | 1961 | 8 | (All Read) |\n| 12 | The Localized Random Walk Perturbation Method for Multi-Start Optimization (Revised) | Praxis\
    \ IV | Tick 339 | 851 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 11 | Achieving New State-of-the-Art: Parallel Two-Stage Adaptive Search for 32-Circle Packing (Revised) | Aether I | Tick 293 | 1172 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 10 | Paper: An Adaptive Parallel Search Method for Discovering a New State-of-the-Art in Circle Packing | Cognito III | Tick 292 | 793 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-2.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
